2 CONC . c.445 Although Gwynedd remains whole politically, the landwithin it i
2 CONC s divided between Cunedda's surviving sons, who then operateas sub-king
2 CONC s to Einion Yrth. Ceredig ap Cunedda already rules inindependent Ceredi
2 CONC gion. A further sub-kingdom, Rhos, is added in c.480.------------------
2 CONC -------------------------------- FROM: MatthewGeneological Research Fou
2 CONC ndation , ..Matthew Geneological ResearchFoundation Web Site, www.users
2 CONC .qwest.net/~butchmatt, Internet. CuneddaGwledig, (the Imperator), born a
2 CONC bt. 380 in Manaw Gododdin, Scotland, 1stnative ruler of Cyrury after th
2 CONC e Romans left in 410. Married Gwawl ferchCoel Hen, (High-King of Northe
2 CONC rn Britain) born abt. 384. CuneddaGwledig Much has been written about t
2 CONC his Cunedda who came to Gwynedd inNorth West Wales from among the men o
2 CONC f the North. The inhabitants ofSouthern Scotland. Cunedda, the Atavus o
2 CONC f Maelgwn Gwyneed came with atleast 8 sons and maybe up to 12 and one g
2 CONC randson from Manaw Gododdin anddrove the irish out of Gwynedd. The only p
2 CONC lace where Cunedda's own nameis found is in Allt Cunedda near Cydweli, c
2 CONC onsidered amoung thefound-fathers of the Welsh Nation. Cunedda's family h
2 CONC ad been importantin Scotland for generations. His fathers name was Eder
2 CONC n, His grandfatherwas Padarn Peisrudd and his great grandfather Tegid. C
2 CONC unedda gave hissons Latin names. Called Gwledig, or Over-King, the perp
2 CONC etuator of thecommand and authority of the Dux Britanniarum. He was the f
2 CONC irst nativeruler of the Cymry after the retirement of the Romans in 410
2 CONC . His powerextended from Carlisle to Wearmouth, his court being held at t
2 CONC he formerplace. His retinue consisted of 900 horses, and he wore the go
2 CONC lden beltand other insignia of the office of Over-King. He was a ferven
2 CONC t Catholicand converted his subjects to Christianity; his descendants w
2 CONC ere, manyof them, ecclesiastics, who organised the Church in his Kingdo
2 CONC m. CuneddaWledig (or Cunedag) hailed from Manau Gododdin, a sub-divisio
2 CONC n of thegreater Kingdom of Gododdin (Lothian) in modern Scotland. His c
2 CONC apitalmay have been in the Clackmannan region.  His father, grandfather a
2 CONC ndgreat grandfather bore Roman names and were probably confederate alli
2 CONC esof the Roman Administration living just north of Hadrian's Wall.  The
2 CONC appendage to Paternus' name is particularly telling. Like many prominen
2 CONC tmen of his era, Cunedda claimed descent from Beli Mawr, the CelticSun-
2 CONC God, throught his son, Lludd Llaw Ereint, God of Healing andgrandson, A
2 CONC fallach, God of the Underworld. Excerts from 'TheFlame-Bearers Of Welsh H
2 CONC istory' That man is Cunedda the First, orCunedda the Great, for great h
2 CONC e must have been.  The oldest piece ofliterature we have is the poem in w
2 CONC hich his bard bewails his death,singing of his might and his conquest o
2 CONC f Bernicia, when he captured thegreat Southern Wall, and so made himsel
2 CONC f King of Upper Britain. It islikely he was a Pict.  But it must be rem
2 CONC embered that up there, betweenthe Walls, a Pict might be either an Iver
2 CONC nian, or a Goidel, or aBrython, so far as race went.  It only meant tha
2 CONC t he belonged to thefree tribes from beyond the Northern Wall, some of w
2 CONC hich still practisedtattooing. The old province between the Walls had b
2 CONC ecome alive withlittle states, homes of raiders and killers.  The Picts o
2 CONC f Galloway hadmarked themselves off from the rest.  The Picts of Manau o
2 CONC f the Gododin(meaning the Southern Shore of the Forth) were leaders of h
2 CONC ordes fromthe wilderness behind them.  The sea rovers had fortified the i
2 CONC sland ofInchkeith in the Forth.  Whichever way the Latin looked, with h
2 CONC is faceturned north or west from the watchtowers of Carlisle, there the g
2 CONC leamof weapons flashed across the land by day, and the glare of burning
2 CONC sreddened the clouds of night. There is small doubt but that it was out
2 CONC of the gleam and the glare that Cunedda came to the throne as king,seiz
2 CONC ing the office of the old Duke of Britain.  The greater the dangeris, t
2 CONC he greater the joy of shattering it.  The more terrible thethreatening o
2 CONC f fate, the sweeter the pleasure of defeating it.  It wasby mastering a
2 CONC ll the ferocious hordes of the invaders that Cunedda couldcapture and k
2 CONC eep the power.  Doubtless he was come of the blood of theirown ancient k
2 CONC ings, either Brython or Goidel, but he seized the Romanoffice too, and t
2 CONC hereby stepped into the history of the world. He livedin the same time w
2 CONC ith that of Vortigern of Projecting Lips, who wasdriven from his throne i
2 CONC n the south.  But while the one was losing histhrone, the other was set
2 CONC tling himself so firmly in the land that hisblood was never to be extin
2 CONC guished in it again. Thus, Cunedda seizedthe office of Dux Britanniarum
2 CONC , or Duke of Britain. Thus he kept it,having his royal court at Carlisl
2 CONC e on the Southern Wall, and his sonskeeping his frontiers.ll the old sp
2 CONC lendour of the Ruler of Britain wasseen again. He wore the golden belt o
2 CONC f the office, and had the old plumeof feathers carried before him when h
2 CONC e walked.  The old retinue of ninehundred horse went with him when he r
2 CONC ode, and the old red golden dragonwas borne above him when he went to w
2 CONC ar, as the silver dragon went withthe Count of Britain in the South.  A
2 CONC nd when you see the Red DragonRampant, on a green ground, remember that a
2 CONC n ancient poem, written acentury after Cunedda's death, speaks of the g
2 CONC reen standard of hisdescendants.  Cunedda, then, was the second of the f
2 CONC lame-bearers.Excerts from 'Land Of My Fathers' by Gwynfor Evans It may h
2 CONC ave beenbecause of the danger that the Irish might take over the countr
2 CONC y thatCunedda moved down around 300-400 AD from Manaw Gododdin, near St
2 CONC irlingin Scotland; perhaps indeed he was directed to Wales for this pur
2 CONC pose asone of the Dukes of Britain.  Cunedda and his army probably came b
2 CONC y sea,for it was very difficult to travel through Lancashire with its b
2 CONC ogs,forests and large rivers.  According to the history given by Nenniu
2 CONC s, itwas the coming of Cunedda with his eight sons and grandson at the t
2 CONC urnof the fourth century which was the most important event in theforma
2 CONC tion of Wales since the first century.  Assuming that Nennius wascorrec
2 CONC t, it is the memory of the sons-including Ceredig and Edern, andthe gra
2 CONC ndson Meirion-which still lives in the names of districts; butthe only p
2 CONC lace where Cunedda's own name is found is in Allt Cunedda nearCydweli.  T
2 CONC he family of Gwynedd, the principal royal line of Wales,claimed descent f
2 CONC rom Cunedda through Maelgwn Gwynedd, his greatgrandson, down to Prince D
2 CONC afydd who was executed in Shrewsbury in 1283 -a pedigree of more than s
2 CONC even centuries.  It is Cunedda Wledig, aBrython of Pictish descent, who c
2 CONC ame to Wales from Scotland, who ismainly responsible for the Welshness o
2 CONC f Wales.  His family had beenimportant in Scotland for generations: his f
2 CONC ather's name was Edern, hisgrandfather was Padarn Peisrudd, and his gre
2 CONC at-grandfather Tegid.  TheLatin forms of the names-Eternus, Paternus, T
2 CONC acitus - suggest Romanassociations, and this is made clearer by the wor
2 CONC d Peisrudd in thegrandfather's name, the pais rudd or red cloak showing R
2 CONC oman office.Cunedda gave his sons Latin names.  It is possible that he w
2 CONC as calledupon to defend Wales by Mascen or by the able Roman general St
2 CONC ilicho,was was reorganising the defence of England about this time.  Li
2 CONC ke thierfather, two of Cunedda's sons and his grandson had Christian na
2 CONC mes.  Itcan reasonably be deduced from this that Cunedda was a Christia
