2 DATE 30 DEC 2009
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Drahomira was a princess of the Hevelli or Hevellians, a Slavic tribeli
2 CONC ving around the river Havel in the Havelland area of Brandenburg inEast
2 CONC ern Germany from the 8th century onwards. In 906 she marriedWratislaw I
2 CONC , duke of Bohemia. They had six children, includingWenceslas, Boleslaw a
2 CONC nd Strezislava, of whom Boleslaw would haveprogeny.
2 CONT
2 CONT She led her husband to cooperation with her own people warring againstS
2 CONC axony. After her husband's untimely death in 921 she and hermother-in-l
2 CONC aw Ludmilla, widow of Wratislaw's father Borziwoy I, dividedthe governm
2 CONC ent of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Popular history depicts Ludmilla as a restrained and pious grandmother,
2 CONC but it is likely that the political demands of government called formor
2 CONC e energy and worldliness than history records. The issue of influenceov
2 CONC er Drahomira's eldest son Wenceslas, only thirteen when his fatherdied, w
2 CONC as one of the main reasons for the eventually fatal discordbetween Drah
2 CONC omira and Ludmilla. Ludmilla exerted great influence overWenceslas, lea
2 CONC ving Drahomira to concentrate her efforts on her youngerson Boleslaw.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite or perhaps as a result of her political and personal efforts,Lu
2 CONC dmilla attracted Drahomira's bitter enmity. Ludmilla fled to Tetincastl
2 CONC e, where he daughter-in-law's hired assassins, Tunna and Gommon,murdere
2 CONC d her in 927.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Wenceslas came to power he sent his mother into exile, though hela
2 CONC ter recalled her. She was said to have persuaded her younger sonBolesla
2 CONC w to murder his elder brother, and Wenceslas was killed inSeptember 935 b
2 CONC y a group of nobles allied to Boleslaw, who took over therule of Bohemi
2 CONC a. Drahomira died after 935. According to a chronicle shewas killed in a
2 CONC n earthquake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father was lord of Luticz, a Slavid state near both Pomerania andMe
2 CONC cklenburg. She was responsible for the murder of her mother-in-law,St.L
2 CONC udmilla, and persuaded her younger son, Boleslaw "the Gruesome", tomurd
2 CONC er the elder St.Wenceslaus. However, according to a chronicle,Drahomira w
2 CONC as eventually swallowed up in an earthquake.
