1 NOTE Wife of Boriwoi, the first Christian Duke of Bohemia, b. at Mielnik, c.
2 CONC 860; d. at Tetin, near Beraun, 15 September, 921. She and her husbandwe
2 CONC re baptized, probably by St. Methodius, in 871. Pagan fanatics drovethe
2 CONC m from their country, but they were soon recalled, and after reigningse
2 CONC ven more years they resigned the throne in favour of their sonSpitignev a
2 CONC nd retired to Tetin. Spitignev died two years later and wassucceeded by W
2 CONC ratislaw, another son of Boriwoi and Ludmilla. Wratislawwas married to D
2 CONC rahomira, a pretended Christian, but a secret favourerof paganism. They h
2 CONC ad twin sons, St. Wenceslaus and Boleslaus the Cruel,the former of whom l
2 CONC ived with Ludmilla at Tetin. Wratislaw died in 916,leaving the eight-ye
2 CONC ar-old Wenceslaus as his successor. Jealous of thegreat influence which L
2 CONC udmilla wielded over Wenceslaus, Drahomirainstigated two noblemen to mu
2 CONC rder her. She is said to have beenstrangled by them with her veil. She w
2 CONC as at first buried in the churchof St. Michael at Tetin, but her remain
2 CONC s were removed to the church ofSt. George at Prague before the year 110
2 CONC 0, probably by St. Wenceslaus,her grandson. She is venerated as one of t
2 CONC he patrons of Bohemia, and herfeast is celebrated on 16 September.
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Ludmilla and her husband, Borziwoy I, founder of the Bohemian dynas
2 CONC tyof Premyslid, entered into the Christian Church. Her religious andpol
2 CONC itical influence over her grandson Wenceslas (Vaclav, Wenzel) angereda s
2 CONC emi-pagan party among the nobility and, in 927 during the regency ofWen
2 CONC ceslas's mother, Drahomira, Ludmilla was murdered.
