1 NOTE When in 1015 his father died, strife occurred between Jaroslav and hisb
2 CONC rothers. Svyatopolk, the eldest, murdered Boris, Gleb and Syvatoslavand t
2 CONC hen was driven out by Jaroslav. Of the other brothers, Izyaslavremained s
2 CONC afe in Polotzk, Sudislav was imprisoned while Mstislav inTmutorokan pre
2 CONC vented Jaroslav, until 1036, from being an absolute ruleras their fathe
2 CONC r had been. Boris and Gleb were venerated as the firstRussian saints. J
2 CONC aroslav, like his father, ruled for thirty-five years.He brought prospe
2 CONC rity while the arts and literature flourished and inKiev the cathedral o
2 CONC f St. Sophia was built. In 1030 he conquered Estoniaand a year later, w
2 CONC ith his brother Mstislav's support, attacked Poland.Mstislav died in 10
2 CONC 36 leaving Jaroslav as the sole ruler. At last hedefeated the Pechenegs a
2 CONC nd in the ensuing peace Christianity flourishedand new monasteries were b
2 CONC uilt. Before his death he divided his landsbetween his five sons.
