2 NOTE St. Vladimir the Great (VLADIMIR or VOLODOMIR).
3 CONT Grand Duke of Kieff and All Russia, grandson of St. Olga, and the first
3 CONC Russian ruler to embrace Christianity, b. 956; d. at Berestova, 15 July
3 CONC ,1015. St. Olga could not convert her son and successor, Sviatoslav, fo
3 CONC rhe lived and died a pagan and brought up his son Vladimir as a paganch
3 CONC ieftain. Sviatoslav had two legitimate sons, Yaropolk and Oleg, and ath
3 CONC ird son, Vladimir, borne him by his court favourite Olga Malusha.Shortl
3 CONC y before his death (972) he bestowed the Grand Duchy of Kieff onYaropol
3 CONC k and gave the land of the Drevlani (now Galicia) to Oleg. Theancient R
3 CONC ussian capital of Novgorod threatened rebellion and, as boththe princes r
3 CONC efused to go thither, Sviatoslav bestowed its sovereigntyupon the young V
3 CONC ladimir. Meanwhile war broke out between Yaropolk andOleg, and the form
3 CONC er conquered the Drevlanian territory and dethronedOleg. When this news r
3 CONC eached Vladimir he feared a like fate and fled tothe Varangians (Variag
3 CONC s) of Scandinavia for help, while Yaropolkconquered Novgorod and united a
3 CONC ll Russia under his sceptre. A few yearslater Vladimir returned with a l
3 CONC arge force and retook Novgorod. Becomingbolder he waged war against his b
3 CONC rother towards the south, took the cityof Polotzk, slew its prince, Rag
3 CONC vald, and married his daughter Ragnilda,the affianced bride of Yaropolk
3 CONC . Then he pressed on and besieged Kieff.Yaropolk fled to Rodno, but cou
3 CONC ld not hold out there, and was finallyslain upon his surrender to the v
3 CONC ictorious Vladimir; the latterthereupon made himself ruler of Kieff and a
3 CONC ll Russia in 980. As aheathen prince Vladimir had four wives besides Ra
3 CONC gnilda, and by them hadten sons and two daughters. Since the days of St
3 CONC . Olga, Christianity,which was originally established among the eastern S
3 CONC lavs by Sts. Cyriland Methodius, had been making secret progress throug
3 CONC hout the land ofRuss (now eastern Austria and Russia) and had begun to c
3 CONC onsiderablyalter the heathen ideas. It was a period similar to the era o
3 CONC f theconversion of Constantine.
3 CONT Notwithstanding this undercurrent of Christian ideas, Vladimir erectedi
3 CONC n Kieff many statues and shrines (trebishcha) to the Slavic heathengods
3 CONC , Perun, Dazhdbog, Simorgl, Mokosh, Stribog, and others. In 981 hesubdu
3 CONC ed the Chervensk cities (now Galicia), in 983 he overcame the wildYatvi
3 CONC ags on the shores of the Baltic Sea, in 985 he fought with theBulgarian
3 CONC s on the lower Volga, and in 987 he planned a campaign againstthe Greco
3 CONC -Roman Empire, in the course of which he became interested inChristiani
3 CONC ty. The Chronicle of Nestor relates that he sent envoys to theneighbour
3 CONC ing countries for information concerning their religions. Theenvoys rep
3 CONC orted adversely regarding the Bulgarians who followed(Mohammed), the Je
3 CONC ws of Khazar, and the Germans with their plainmissionary Latin churches
3 CONC , but they were delighted with the solemn Greekritual of the Great Chur
3 CONC ch (St. Sophia) of Constantinople, and remindedVladimir that his grandm
3 CONC other Olga had embraced that Faith. The nextyear (988) he besieged Kher
3 CONC son in the Crimea, a city within the bordersof the eastern Roman Empire
3 CONC , and finally took it by cutting off itswater supply. He then sent envo
3 CONC ys to Emperor Basil II at Constantinopleto ask for his sister Anna in m
3 CONC arriage, adding a threat to march onConstantinople in case of refusal. T
3 CONC he emperor replied that a Christianmight not marry a heathen, but if Vl
3 CONC adimir were a Christian prince hewould sanction the alliance. To this V
3 CONC ladimir replied that he hadalready examined the doctrines of the Christ
3 CONC ians, was inclined towardsthem, and was ready to be baptized. Basil II s
3 CONC ent this sister with aretinue of officials and clergy to Kherson, and t
3 CONC here Vladimir wasbaptized, in the same year, by the Metropolitan Michae
3 CONC l and took alsothe baptismal name of Basil. A current legend relates th
3 CONC at Vladimir hadbeen stricken with blindness before the arrival of Anna a
3 CONC nd her retinueand had recovered his sight upon being baptized. He then m
3 CONC arriedPrincess Anna, and thereafter put away his pagan wives. He surren
3 CONC deredthe city of Kherson to the Greeks and returned to Kieff in state w
3 CONC ithhis bride. The Russian historian Karamsin (Vol. I, p. 215) suggests t
3 CONC hatVladimir could have been baptized long before at Kieff, since Christ
3 CONC iansand their priests were already there; but such an act would have hu
3 CONC mbledthe proud chieftain in the eyes of his people, for he would haveac
3 CONC cepted in a lowly manner an inconspicuous rite at the hands of asecret a
3 CONC nd despised sect. Hence he preferred to have it come from theenvoys of t
3 CONC he Roman Emperor of Constantinople, as a means of impressinghis people.
3 CONT When Vladimir returned to Kieff he took upon himself the conversion ofh
3 CONC is subjects. He ordered the statues of the gods to be thrown down,chopp
3 CONC ed to pieces, and some of them burned; the chief god, Perun, wasdragged t
3 CONC hrough the mud and thrown into the River Dnieper. These actsimpressed t
3 CONC he people with the helplessness of their gods, and when theywere told t
3 CONC hat they should follow Vladimir's example and becomeChristians they wer
3 CONC e willingly baptized, even wading into the river thatthey might the soo
3 CONC ner be reached by the priest for baptism. Zubryckithinks this readiness s
3 CONC hows that the doctrines of Christianity hadalready been secretly spread i
3 CONC n Kieff and that the people only waitedfor an opportunity to publicly a
3 CONC cknowledge them. Vladimir urged all hissubjects to become Christians, e
3 CONC stablished churches and monasteries notonly at Kieff, but at Pereyaslav
3 CONC , Chernigoff, Bielegorod, Vladimir inVolhynia, and many other cities. I
3 CONC n 989 he erected the large Church ofSt. Mary ever Virgin (usually calle
3 CONC d Desiatinny Sobor, the Cathedral ofthe Tithes), and in 996 the Church o
3 CONC f the Transfiguration, both in thecity of Kieff. He gave up his warlike c
3 CONC areer and devoted himselfprincipally to the government of his people; h
3 CONC e established schools,introduced ecclesiastical courts, and became know
3 CONC n for his mildness andfor his zeal in spreading the Christian faith. Hi
3 CONC s wife died in 1011,having borne him two sons, Boris and Glib (also kno
3 CONC wn a Sts. Roman andDavid, from their baptismal names). After this his l
3 CONC ife became troubledby the conduct of his elder children. Following the c
3 CONC ustom of hisancestors, he had parcelled out his kingdom amongst his chi
3 CONC ldren, givingthe city of Novgorod in fief to his eldest son Yaroslav; t
3 CONC he latterrebelled against him and refused to render either service or t
3 CONC ribute. In1014 Vladimir prepared to march north to Novgorod and take it a
3 CONC way fromhis disobedient son, while Yaroslav invoked the help of the Var
3 CONC angiansagainst his father. Vladimir fell ill and died on the way. His f
3 CONC east incelebrated on 15 July in the Russian Orthodox and Ruthenian Gree
3 CONC kCatholic calendars, and he has received the name of Ravnoapostol (equa
3 CONC lto the Apostles) in the title of the feast and the troparion of thelit
3 CONC urgy. The Russians have added in their service books words referringhis c
3 CONC onversion and intercession to the present Russian Empire(rossiiskaya ze
3 CONC mlya), but the Ruthenians have never permitted theseinterpolations.
3 CONT PELESZ, Gesch. der Union, I (Vienna, 1878), 79-127; NILLES, Kalendarium
3 CONC Manuale, I (Innsbruck 1896), 212; Acta SS., IV, July, p.4; Bogoslovskay
3 CONC aEnciclopedia, III (St. Petersburg, 1902), 564-67; GOLUBINSKI, IstoriaR
3 CONC usskoi Tserkvi, I (Moscow, 1901), pt. I, 105-87; MALTZEW, DieNachtwache (
3 CONC Berlin, 1892), 724-27; ADENEY, The Greek and EasternChurches (New York, 1
3 CONC 908), 358-65; MOURAVIEFF, Hist. of the RussianChurch (Oxford, 1842), 10
3 CONC -18; ZUBRYCKI, Gesch. des Fürstenthums Galicz(Lemburg, 1852).
3 CONT ANDREW J. SHIPMAN
