2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE After her husband had established peace, he wanted to increase thetribu
3 CONC tes from the Slavic tribe of the Derevlyanins. In 945 he went and,after p
3 CONC illaging them, returned to Kiev. However, when he prepared toleave agai
3 CONC n, the Derevlyanins went to meet him and killed him. TheDerevlyanins th
3 CONC en sent twenty of their best men to Olga and said: "Wehave killed your h
3 CONC usband, for he was like a wolf, stealing andplundering, while our princ
3 CONC es are good and have taken good care of theland of Dereva. Marry our pr
3 CONC ince Mal!" To this she replied that shecouldn't resurrect her husband a
3 CONC nd so asked them to come the next day tobe honoured before the people o
3 CONC f Kiev. She told them that as a specialhonour they should not ride on h
3 CONC orses nor go on foot, but would becarried in the boat they arrived in. D
3 CONC uring the night, a deep pit wasdug and the following day, as was announ
3 CONC ced, the twenty men were carriedin their boat, but which was then throw
3 CONC n into the pit where they wereburied alive. She then sent a message to t
3 CONC he Derevlyanins in which sheclaimed that the people of Kiev would not r
3 CONC elease her unless she wascollected by their most distinguished men. On t
3 CONC heir arrival Olga ordereda bath to be prepared for them. The hut was he
3 CONC ated up and theDerevlyanins went in. When they began to wash, Olga's me
3 CONC n secured thehut and, on her orders, set it on fire, and all inside wer
3 CONC e burnedalive. With her son and their army, she then went to the city o
3 CONC fIskorosten where her husband had been killed. Although they encircledt
3 CONC he city, they were unable to take it. After the siege had lasted almost
3 CONC the whole summer, she decided to take a different course. She sent ames
3 CONC sage to the town that they would be starved as they could not worktheir f
3 CONC ields, and that she only wanted them to pay tribute. For thistribute sh
3 CONC e requested three doves and three sparrows from each house.Gladly conse
3 CONC nting, the Derevlyanins collected three doves and threesparrows from ea
3 CONC ch house. Olga then announced that, as they hadsubmitted to her, she wo
3 CONC uld return to Kiev the next day. However, Olgathen distributed the dove
3 CONC s and sparrows with the order that lightedtinder, wrapped in little pie
3 CONC ces of cloth, be tied to each dove andsparrow. At dusk the birds were r
3 CONC eleased, the doves returning to theirdove-cotes and the sparrows to the
3 CONC ir nests under the eaves.Consequently, dove-cotes, outbuildings, towers a
3 CONC nd barns all caughtfire, and there was no household where there was no f
3 CONC ire. The peoplefled the town, only to be caught by Olga's soldiers. She t
3 CONC hen burned thewhole city, killing some of it's people and selling other
3 CONC s as slaves. In955 she went to Constantinople where the Emperor baptise
3 CONC d herpersonally. At first the Emperor had wanted to marry her but then g
3 CONC aveher many gifts and allowed her to return. She tried to convert her s
3 CONC onto Christianity but he refused, yet would allow anyone in Kiev to beb
3 CONC aptised but with the proviso that they would be ridiculed. In 965 herso
3 CONC n, as ruler of Kiev, defeated the Khazars, the Ossetians and theCircuss
3 CONC ians. While he was away in 968, the Pechenegs laid siege to Kievwhere O
3 CONC lga was with her grandsons. With Kiev hermetically sealed, no onecould s
3 CONC end a message to her son until a young boy said he would go. Hewent wit
3 CONC h a bridle in his hands, calling out, "Has no one seen myhorse?" As he s
3 CONC poke the Pechenegs language, they thought him one oftheir own. At the r
3 CONC iver he took off his clothes and swam across. Here hefound a small band o
3 CONC f soldiers who, next morning, approached the city insmall boats. On arr
3 CONC ival they blew hard on their trumpets, creating theimpression of being a l
3 CONC arge force, at which the Pechenegs fled.
