1 NOTE FROM: Encyclopedia Britannica, www.britannica.com, Internet. Ringerikek
2 CONC ommune (cocommune Ringerik)
2 CONT
2 CONT FROM: Encyclopedia Britannica, www.britannica.com, Internet. Ringerikek
2 CONC ommune (tommune) and geographic region, Buskerud fylke (county),southea
2 CONC stern Norway, just northwest of Oslo. The region covers a totalarea of 6
2 CONC 00 square miles (1,553 square km) adjacent to the northernshore of Tyri L
2 CONC ake and northward to Rands Lake. The Ringerike wasinhabited as early as A
2 CONC D 200 and existed as a petty kingdom in the 8thand 9th centuries. Durin
2 CONC g the 10th century Norway's greatest earlykings, Olaf I Trygvasson and O
2 CONC laf II Haraldsson, grew up at Bonsnes inRingerike. When Christianity wa
2 CONC s established in the region during thatcentury, a form of art emerged k
2 CONC nown as Ringerike style: this was aunique style of ornamentation on woo
2 CONC d, stone, and metal that used plantforms as the basis of the designs. T
2 CONC he Ringerike region had aflourishing timber industry that peaked in the 1
2 CONC 7th century. Throughoutthe 19th and early 20th centuries, lead mining w
2 CONC as carried out in theregion, and by 1870 one-seventh of the world's lea
2 CONC d came from localmines. Today, agriculture, lumbering, and wood-pulp mi
2 CONC lls are the maineconomic activities; ample hydroelectric power is produ
2 CONC ced by the Begnaand Rands rivers. Pop. (1990 est.) commune, 27,288.
