1 NOTE When his father died, in the divison of his lands Chilperich I received
2 CONC the poorest region, the kingdom of Soissons. However, when hishalf-brot
2 CONC her Charibert died, he received the better parts of his lands.He repudi
2 CONC ated his wife to be able to marry the Visigoth princessGalsvintha, but t
2 CONC hen murdered the latter to marry Fredegunde, hismistress. This sparked a f
2 CONC amily feud as his half-brother Sigebert I wasmarried to Galsvintha's si
2 CONC ster. Attacked and defeated by Sigebert I, heseemed vanquished until Fr
2 CONC edegunde had Sigebert I murdered. Chilperichthen tried to take his land
2 CONC s but was prevented by another half-brother,Guntram, King of Burgundy. F
2 CONC irst he tried to form an alliance withChildebert II, son of Sigebert I, b
2 CONC ut this lasted about two years andthen he made peace with his half-brot
2 CONC her Guntram. A year laterChilperich fell victim to an unknown assassin, l
2 CONC eaving a four-month-oldson, Chlotar II. Ambitious, brutal and debauched
2 CONC , Chilperichnevertheless had pretensions of being a man of learning; he w
2 CONC rote poorpoetry, became involved in theological matters, and ordered fo
