2 CONT
2 CONT Chilperic I b. c. 539 d. , September or October 584, Chelles, FranceMer
2 CONC ovingian king of Soissons whom Gregory of Tours, a contemporary,called t
2 CONC he Nero and the Herod of his age. Son of Chlotar I by Aregund,Chilperic s
2 CONC hared with his three half brothers (sons of Ingund, Aregund'ssister) in t
2 CONC he partition that followed their father's death in 561,receiving the po
2 CONC orest region, the kingdom of Soissons. To this wasadded, however, the b
2 CONC est part of Charibert's lands on the latter's deathin 567 or 568, so th
2 CONC at Chilperic's kingdom corresponded in large part tothat later known as N
2 CONC eustria. In 568 he repudiated his wives in order tomarry Galswintha, si
2 CONC ster of the Visigothic princess, Brunhild, who hadherself recently marr
2 CONC ied his half brother, Sigebert I; but he soon hadGalswintha murdered an
2 CONC d immediately married Fredegund, an earliermistress. The consequences o
2 CONC f this crime constitute virtually the onlyclearly discernible thread in t
2 CONC he tangled skein of Frankish history overthe next four decades, as firs
2 CONC t Sigebert, whose relations with Chilperichad in fact been bad from the s
2 CONC tart, and then his descendants, incitedby Brunhild, sought revenge for G
2 CONC alswintha's murder upon the persons ofChilperic, Fredegund, and their f
2 CONC amily. Saved from apparent disaster bythe assassination of Sigebert I i
2 CONC n 575, Chilperic was prevented fromseizing the lands of the dead king's y
2 CONC oung heir, Childebert II, by theaction of Guntram, his third half broth
2 CONC er and the king of Burgundy.Although Chilperic succeeded in forming an a
2 CONC lliance with Childebertagainst Guntram by recognizing the young king as h
2 CONC is heir (581), thiswas short-lived; in 583 Childebert and Guntram again c
2 CONC ame to terms. Ayear later Chilperic fell victim to an unknown assassin, l
2 CONC eaving afour-month-old son, Chlotar II. Ambitious, brutal, and debauche
2 CONC d,Chilperic nevertheless had pretensions to being a man of learning; he
2 CONC wrote poor poetry, became involved in theological matters, and orderedf
2 CONC our letters to be added to the alphabet. Regarding the church as amajor r
2 CONC ival to his wealth, he treated the bishops with hostility andcontempt; a
2 CONC t the same time, he had a reputation for injustice toward hissubjects a
2 CONC t large and imposed heavy taxes.
2 CONT
