1 NOTE FROM: www.britannia.com/bios/ebk/coelhnt.html Coel Hen, King of Norther
2 CONC nBritain (c.350-c.420) (Welsh-Coel, Latin-Coelius, English-Cole) CoelHe
2 CONC n or Coel the Old is known to most of us through the famous nurseryrhym
2 CONC e: Old King Cole was a merry old soul And a merry old soul washe. He ca
2 CONC lled for his pipe, And he called for his bowl, And hecalled for his fid
2 CONC dlers, three. He is also a familiar figure in ancientWelsh genealogies, f
2 CONC or most of the Celtic British monarchies claimeddescent from him in one f
2 CONC orm or another. He appears to have lived aroundthe turn from the 4th to t
2 CONC he 5th century, the time when the Romanofficials returned to Italy, lea
2 CONC ving Britain and her people to fend forthemselves. Coel's particular as
2 CONC sociation with the north of Britain hasled to the suggestion that he ma
2 CONC y actually have been the last of theRoman Duces Brittanniarum with his h
2 CONC eadquarters at York. He certainlyimposed his power over a great swathe o
2 CONC f the country, and can beconsidered the first King in Northern Britain. (
2 CONC This Coel should not beconfused with the legendary Coel Godhebog 'the M
2 CONC agnificent', Lord ofColchester, whose daughter, St. Helen, supposedly m
2 CONC arried the EmperorConstantius Chlorus two centuries earlier.) There is a
2 CONC n old story toldin the north about Coel's last campaign. What is now Sc
2 CONC otland wasoriginally inhabited by the Pictish race. It was during Coel'
2 CONC s time thatimmigrant Irishmen from the Scotti tribe began to settle the W
2 CONC esterncoast around Argyle. Coel, fearing that the two peoples would uni
2 CONC teagainst the British, sent raiding parties across his northern border t
2 CONC ostir up discord between them. The plan, however, backfired for the Pic
2 CONC tsand the Scots were not taken in. Coel merely succeeded in pushing the
2 CONC two even closer together, and they began to attack the British Kingdomo
2 CONC f Strathclyde. Coel declared all out war and moved north to expel thein
2 CONC vaders. The Picts and Scots fled to the hills ahead of Coel's army,who e
2 CONC ventually set up camp at what became Coylton alongside the Water ofCoyl
2 CONC e (Ayrshire). For a long time, the British were triumphant, whilethe Sc
2 CONC ots and Picts starved. Desperate for some relief, however, theenemy adv
2 CONC anced an all-or-nothing attack on Coel's stronghold. Coel andhis men we
2 CONC re taken by surprise, overrun and scattered to the winds. Itis said tha
2 CONC t Coel wandered the unknown countryside until he eventuallygot caught i
2 CONC n a bog at Coilsfield (in Tarbolton, Ayrshire) and drowned.Coel was fir
2 CONC st buried in a mound there before being removed to thechurch at Coylton
2 CONC . The year was about AD 420. After his death, Coel'sNorthern Kingdom wa
2 CONC s divided between two of his sons, Ceneu andGorbanian. FROM: P L Kessle
2 CONC r <plk@globalnet.co.uk>, The History Files,Home Publishing A(c)1999, ww
2 CONC w.users.globalnet.co.uk\\_plk\\history.htm,Internet Coel Hen Coel Hen i
2 CONC s a familiar figure in many ancient Welshgenealogies. Most of the Celti
2 CONC c British kings of the north of Britaincould trace their descent from h
2 CONC im in one form or another, as could manyWelsh kings. In the short time a
2 CONC fter his life that Central and NorthernBritain remained free of the inv
2 CONC ading Angles, between the start of thefifth century and mid-sixth centu
2 CONC ry, all of the kingdoms that wereestablished were by his sons or grands
2 CONC ons. Although the evidence istypically patchy, he appears to have lived f
2 CONC rom around 350 - 420, duringthe time when the last Roman officials retu
2 CONC rned to the heart of thefaltering empire, leaving Britain and her peopl
2 CONC e to fend for themselves.Coel's particular association with the north o
2 CONC f Britain has led to thewell-founded suggestion that he was the last of t
2 CONC he Roman DucesBrittanniarum (Dukes of the Britons). Only one existed at a
2 CONC ny time. Theywere selected as generals of the army with direct authorit
2 CONC y from thegovernor of Britannia to defend the coast from the increasing b
2 CONC arbarianraids). The Roman dux disappear from the Notitia Dignitatum in a
2 CONC bout 400and it is not unnatural to presume that Coel took his place. He s
2 CONC eems tohave made his headquarters at Britain's northern capital of Ebur
2 CONC acum(York), and he certainly imposed his power over a great swathe of t
2 CONC hecountry. Coel Hen can be considered by tradition to be the first king
2 CONC in, and of, Northern Britain, as seems to have overseen the transitionf
2 CONC rom direct Roman rule to an independent Britain which took care of itso
2 CONC wn defence. In the Celtic tradition, because of his dominance, he iskno
2 CONC wn fully as the High King of Northern Britain (as opposed to othermajor k
2 CONC ings of his generation, such as Cunedda Wledig, who was King ofNorth Wa
2 CONC les - later Gwynedd, or Antonius Donatus Gregorius (Anwn), whowas King o
2 CONC f South Wales - Demetia). From his headquarters Coel Hengoverned the te
2 CONC rritory between Eburacum and Hadrian's Wall (which formedthe later Brit
2 CONC ish kingdoms of Ebrauc, Deywr, and Bernaccia), and west tocover the are
2 CONC a of Rheged, (later North Rheged, South Rheged, Dunoting,Elmet, Caer-Gu
2 CONC endoleu, and a kingdom which, to deduce its name from thelater Saxon Pe
2 CONC cset, was probably called the Kingdom of the Peak).According to later c
2 CONC laims, he also had a hand in structuring theGoutoddin in the eastern te
2 CONC rritory between the Walls after the departureof Cunedda Wledig. As a re
2 CONC sult of the many kingdoms which wereinherited by his immediate descenda
2 CONC nts, Coel became the foundingancestor of what came to be known as The M
2 CONC en of the North (Gwyr yGogledd). These were the Britons of the survivin
2 CONC g kingdoms who werefighting the advancing Angles in the 6th and 7th cen
2 CONC turies. They weredrawn from the kingdoms of Goutoddin and Rheged, from S
2 CONC trathclyde andvarious minor principalities, and together they upheld th
2 CONC e tradition ofbattling Celtic warriors, feasting together before riding o
2 CONC ut with thewarband to do battle with the enemy. Their stubborn resistan
2 CONC ce was dealta fatal blow at Catreath (Catterick) in around 600, and the
2 CONC se events(detailed in The Mabinogion) cemented the reputation of The Me
2 CONC n of theNorth in their glorious, but ultimately futile, efforts of resi
2 CONC stance tothe Teutonic invaders. Most people today will have heard of Co
2 CONC el Hen(or 'King Coel' - with 'Hen' the Brito-Welsh word for 'old'), eve
2 CONC n ifthey don't realise it. He is immortalised in verse: Old King Cole w
2 CONC as amerry old soul And a merry old soul was he. He called for his pipe,
2 CONC And he called for his bowl, And he called for his fiddlers, three The l
2 CONC egends of the Northern British were preserved by Rhodri Mawr, whenhe be
2 CONC came King of Gwynedd. One of those legends concerned Coel Hen'slast cam
2 CONC paign. It was during Coel's time as High King that immigrantIrishmen fr
2 CONC om the Scotti tribe of Dalriata (in the region of Ulster)began to settl
2 CONC e the western coast of Pictland, around Argyle. Coel,fearing that the t
2 CONC wo peoples would unite against the British, sentraiding parties across h
2 CONC is northern border to stir up discord betweenthem. The plan backfired a
2 CONC s the Picts and the Scots were not taken in.Coel merely succeeded in pu
2 CONC shing the two even closer together, and theybegan to attack the British K
2 CONC ingdom of Strathclyde. Coel declared allout war and moved north to expe
2 CONC l the invaders. The Picts and Scots fledto the hills ahead of Coel's ar
2 CONC my, who eventually set up camp at whatbecame Coylton alongside the Wate
2 CONC r of Coyle (Ayrshire). For a long time,the British were victorious, whi
2 CONC le the Scots and Picts starved.Desperate for some relief, the enemy adv
2 CONC anced in a last-ditch attack onCoel's stronghold. Coel and his men were t
2 CONC aken by surprise, overrun andscattered to the winds. It is said that Co
2 CONC el wandered the unknowncountryside until he eventually got caught in a b
2 CONC og at Coilsfield (inTarbolton, Ayrshire) and drowned. Coel was first bu
2 CONC ried in a mound therebefore being removed to the church at Coylton. The y
2 CONC ear was circa AD420. After his death, Coel's Northern Kingdom was divid
2 CONC ed between two ofhis sons: Ceneu (St) assumed control of the kingdoms o
2 CONC f the North &Midland Britain, remaining based at Ebrauc. Gorbanian foun
2 CONC ded thedynasty that ruled over the Kingdom of Bernaccia (Bryneich), whi
2 CONC ch waslater taken over by the Angles, who pronounced it Bernicia. Becau
2 CONC se ofCoel's, and his son's, apparently continued use of Eburacum as a b
2 CONC ase ofoperations and also as the traditional Roman capital of North Bri
2 CONC tain,it makes sense to list the Kings of North Britain alongside the Ki
2 CONC ngs ofEbrauc (as the evolving Brito-Welsh language dubbed it). There we
2 CONC re onlythree of the former, with the next in line ruling only half the l
2 CONC and ofhis father, as the rest of it had been inherited by his brother. M
2 CONC agnusMaximus (Macsen Wledig) 383 - 388 AD Western Roman Emperor 383-388
2 CONC .Selected Coel Hen as his replacement in most of Northern Britain. 400A
2 CONC D The reorganisations of Magnus Maximus and his subsequent withdrawalof t
2 CONC roops from Britain virtually signals the end of Roman rule over theisla
2 CONC nd. From this point on, all of Britain's High Kings originate fromwithi
2 CONC n the country.
