1 NOTE Beli (Heli) Mawr(the Great) the 64th King of Britain, born Briton abt.1
2 CONC 10 b.c. died 62 b.c. married Don ferch Mathonwy. (c) A semi-legendaryBr
2 CONC itish king who was probably an historical ruler, though any facts haveb
2 CONC ecome so covered by the dust of myth that it is impossible to becertain a
2 CONC bout his true basis.  Bel was the name of one of the principalCeltic de
2 CONC ities, the god of the sun and of light, and it is notsurprising that th
2 CONC e name would be adopted by later warrior kings, thoughhow much the epis
2 CONC odes about Bel in myth are derived from those of a realking of that nam
2 CONC e (or vice versa) cannot be determined.  To haveacquired the cognomen M
2 CONC awr must mean that Beli was a great king and itis likely that he was on
2 CONC e of the first to impose his authority over manyof the tribes of Britai
2 CONC n, most likely over southern Britain and Wales.The Welsh legends make h
2 CONC im the father of LUD and Llefelys and possibleof Caswallon.  Since Casw
2 CONC allon was High King at the time of Caesar'sinvasion, this would place B
2 CONC eli's existence at the start of the firstcentruy BC.  It is possible to t
2 CONC race most of the British and Welshrulers back to Beli. Beli (Heli) Mawr C
2 CONC alled Belenos by the Romans, wasthe Celtic God of the Sun, representing t
2 CONC he curative powers of the Sun'sheat.  His festival of Beltane, when bon
2 CONC fires were lit to welcome in theSummer and encourage the Sun's warmth, w
2 CONC as held on May 1st, and isremembered in today's May Day festivities.  H
2 CONC is symbols were the horse(as shown, for example, by the clay horse figu
2 CONC rine offerings at Beli'sSainte-Sabine shrine in Burgundy), and also the W
2 CONC heel (as illustrated onthe famous Gundestrup Cauldron). Perhaps, like A
2 CONC pollo, whom he becameidentified with, Beli was thought to ride the Sun a
2 CONC cross the sky in ahorse-drawn chariot.  Indeed, a Celtic model horse an
2 CONC d wagon, carrying agilded sun-disc, has been found at Trundholm in Denm
2 CONC ark.  Sometimes heis illustrated riding a single horse, throuwing thund
2 CONC er-bolts (hence anoccasional idenification with Jupiter) and using his s
2 CONC ymbolic radiatingwheel as a sheild, as he tramples the chthonic forces o
2 CONC f a snake-limbedgiant.  This personification is similar to the classic d
2 CONC epiction of theArchangel St. Michael defeating the Devil.  Sacred pagan h
2 CONC illsassociated with Beli, are thought to have had their dedicationstran
2 CONC sferred to this saint (or sometimes St. George) by the earlyChristians.  W
2 CONC ell known examples include St. Michael's Mount (Cornwall)and the church
2 CONC es of St. Michael on Brent Tor (Devon), and Burrow Mumpand Glastonbury T
2 CONC or (Somerset): All on a supposed ley line that facesthe Rising Sun at B
2 CONC eltane.  He may also have been worshipped on DragonHill below the great U
2 CONC ffington White Horse in Berkshire.
