1 NOTE Trusted advisor of Eudaf Hen of Gwent. Dumnonia This large andwell-foun
2 CONC ded kingdom took in all of Cornwall (Cornubia), Devon, and muchof Somer
2 CONC set. Its original capital would have been Isca (Exeter), butarchaeology c
2 CONC onfirms that this was abandoned in the 5th century, and,given the advan
2 CONC ces of the West Saxons, the capital would continuallyhave been relocate
2 CONC d to the west. The main part of Dumnonia in modernDevon (the Defnas Bri
2 CONC tons) fell to Wessex between 650-670, while theCornish remnant was inde
2 CONC pendent until 825-829. Although under Romancontrol until the late 4th c
2 CONC entury, the Celtic tribe of the Dumnoniiprobably exercised a certain am
2 CONC ount of self-government in their ownlands. They most probably establish
2 CONC ed their kingdom as a power in itsown right by the time of Magnus Maxim
2 CONC us, as the latter preparedBritain's defences prior to establishing his o
2 CONC wn claim for control ofthe Roman Empire in AD 383, and Dumnonia was ful
2 CONC ly independent by 410.-------------------------------------------------
2 CONC - FROM: EncyclopediaBritannica, www.britannica.com, Internet. Caratacus f
2 CONC l. 1st century ADalso spelled CARACTACUS, CELTIC CARADOC, king of the B
2 CONC ritish tribe ofTrinovantes, and the son of Cunobelinus. Caratacus' king
2 CONC dom, embracingthe Atrebates of Hampshire and probably the Dobunni of Gl
2 CONC oucestershire,lay in the west. At the time of the Roman invasion of Bri
2 CONC tain during thereign of Claudius, he led the native resistance against A
2 CONC ulus Plautius(ad 43-47) and, after being defeated, withdrew into south W
2 CONC ales. He wasfinally defeated by Ostorius Scapula in AD 50, somewhere on t
2 CONC he Welshmarches, in the territory of the Ordovices. He himself fled to t
2 CONC heBrigantes, whose queen, Cartimandua, delivered him to the Romans. He a
2 CONC ndhis family were featured in a victory parade of Claudius, who granted
2 CONC them pardon and life. -------------------------------------------------
2 CONC -FROM Britannia Internet Magazine, www.britannia.com, Internet.Caratacu
2 CONC s. An historical person with some legendary accretions.Caratacus (somet
2 CONC imes spelled Caractacus) was the king of theCatuvellauni at the time of t
2 CONC he Roman invasion under the emperor,Claudius, and was the leader of the a
2 CONC nti-Roman campaign. His forcessuffered several defeats by the Romans an
2 CONC d, after a defeat at the handsof the then governor, Ostorious Scapula, i
2 CONC n 51 AD, Caratacus retreatedto the land of the Brigantes in northern Br
2 CONC itain. Unfortunately forCaratacus, Cartimandua, the Queen of the Brigan
2 CONC tes, was bound by aclient-ruler relationship with the Romans, so she ha
2 CONC nded Caratacus overto them. Caratacus was sent to Rome along with other c
2 CONC aptives, where hecame to Claudius' attention, according to Tacitus, for h
2 CONC is courtesy andbearing and so was pardoned. He and his family were perm
2 CONC itted to liveout their lives in peace in Italy. Some investigators have c
2 CONC ome to theconclusion that Caratacus is the historic original for King A
2 CONC rthur,while others insist that he and Arviragus are one and the same.
