1 NOTE FROM: Matthew Geneological Research Foundation , ..Matthew Geneological
2 CONC Research Foundation Web Site, www.users.qwest.net/~butchmatt, Internet. L
2 CONC ludd Llaw Encint (or Llud) (the silver handed), born Britain abt. 80b.c
2 CONC .. (c) Legendary king of the British mentioned in Geoffrey ofMonmouth's H
2 CONC istory. Lud is listed as the eldest son of Heli (or Beli),and the broth
2 CONC er of the historically real Caswallon, which would placeLud's existence a
2 CONC t about 60BC. Lud was that rare combination of warriorking and town pla
2 CONC nner. He rebuilt New Troy, or Trinovantum as it wasthen known, and rena
2 CONC med it KaerLud after him. This became Lud's Town orLondon. When he died h
2 CONC e was buried by the city wall where Ludgate isnamed after him. There is a s
2 CONC tory of Lud in the Welsh tale 'Lludd andLlefelys' collected in the Mabi
2 CONC nogion, wherein Lud consults his brotherLlefelys on how to combat three s
2 CONC upernatural plagues that are smitingBritain. He succeeds in defeating t
2 CONC he source of the plagues and rulespeacefully thereafter. This tale, lik
2 CONC e that of Merlin's, to which it isclosely related, may be about a real B
2 CONC ritish prince who ruled later thanGeoffrey's Lud, possible in the first o
2 CONC r second centruy AD. He hasbecome remembered in Welsh legend as the Cel
2 CONC tic god Llud, also known asNudd, the Celtic form of Nodens. A temple to N
2 CONC odens was built at Lydneyin Gloucestershire, where there are other plac
2 CONC es starting with Lyd-, andwhich may have some relation to a local princ
2 CONC e who assumed the name Lud.Lludd Llaw Encint (the silver handed) Called N
2 CONC odens by the Romans, wasthe Celtic god of Healing. He had a large shrin
2 CONC e at Lydney inGloucesterhire, where the devoted made offerings of small b
2 CONC ronzerepresentations of their deseased limbs. He was sometimes identifi
2 CONC edwith the protective Mars or the regenerative Silvanus and his compani
2 CONC onand symbol was the dog: a deerhound whose lick could cure the afflict
2 CONC ed.An old story explains his connection with amputees. At one time, Llu
2 CONC ddwas the leader of the gods, but he was wounded in battle and lost his
2 CONC hand. Gorfannon, the divine-smith, made him a new one out of Silver, bu
2 CONC the was still forced to abdicate in favour of his nephew, Lleu LlawGyff
2 CONC es. Later, Lludd was troubled by a constant scream that was heardthe ev
2 CONC e of every Beltane. He traveled to Gaul, where his brotherLlefelys, was p
2 CONC articularly worshipped, to ask his advice. He explainedthat the cry was m
2 CONC ade by two fighting dragons. Lludd managed to capturethe creatures and i
2 CONC mprisoned them deep below Dinas Emrys. Lludd may havebeen particularly w
2 CONC orshipped in London, which was said to have beennamed after him.
