1 NOTE Caswallon, died about 48 BC. (c) The earliest known historical Britishk
2 CONC ing, Caswallon (known to the Romans as Cassivelaunos) was ruler of theC
2 CONC atuvellauni tribe who dominated the lands to the north of the Thames,an
2 CONC d most of what is now Bedfordshire, Hertfordshire, Berkshire,Oxfordshir
2 CONC e and into Wiltshire. His stronghold was at what is nowWheathamstead. H
2 CONC e was evidently a powerful warrior king able toestablish himself as the h
2 CONC igh-king of the British tribes as, beforeCaesar's invasion of Britain i
2 CONC n 55 BC, Caswallon had already attackedthe tribe of the Trinovantes in E
2 CONC ssex and killed thier king Imanuentius.It is recorded that Caswallon ha
2 CONC d an army of over 4,000 charioteers letalone infantry. Even these, thou
2 CONC gh, were no match for the Romans. Thefact that Caesar was unable to con
2 CONC quer Britain outright says somethingfor the power and determination of t
2 CONC he British tribes and of Caswallonas leader. At some stage around 50 or 4
2 CONC 5 BC he was succeeded by his sonAndoco with whom he perhaps showed part o
2 CONC f his territory, andsubsequently by Tasciovanus, who may have been his s
2 CONC on or nephew.
