1 NOTE FROM: http://www.hickling.swinternet.co.uk/ Origins of the name Hicklin
2 CONC gThe fact that the Mercian royal family was known as Icelingasstrengthe
2 CONC ns the claim that it was Icel and his son Cnebba Iceling whocame to Bri
2 CONC tain in AD 499. The Icelingas entered Britain through theestuaries of t
2 CONC he Wash and the Trent. They settled in navigable rivervalleys and areas s
2 CONC erved by Roman canals. Roman influence had wainedsome 100 years earlier a
2 CONC lthough the network of roads and canalsremained. The English settlement
2 CONC s became part of a sophisticated andprosperous society never far away f
2 CONC rom means of communication bynavigable rivers and canals or stone surfa
2 CONC ced causeways and roads.Early pagan literature such as Beowulf, supplem
2 CONC ented by recentarchaeological discoveries, provide insight into the bel
2 CONC iefs of theAngles which were very similar to those of some non-christia
2 CONC ncivilizations today. There was a belief in life hereafter and a profou
2 CONC ndrespect for their ancestors. The more distant and admired ancestorsas
2 CONC sumed in legend the stature of gods, e.g. Tiuw, Woden, Thor and Freyr,a
2 CONC fter whom the days of the week were named in Anglo-Saxon England. In199
2 CONC 7 Northamptonshire archaeologists excavated a pagan and heroic burialin t
2 CONC he gravel plain of the Nene Valley at Wollaston. It was the grave ofan A
2 CONC nglian nobleman at the side of a road leading to a Roman vineyardand ha
2 CONC s been dated about AD 650. The most important content of the gravewas a b
2 CONC oar-crested helmet like those so often referred to in Beowulf.The boar w
2 CONC hich symbolised strength and was associated with the goddessFreyr would h
2 CONC ave been worn by Ickeling leaders of the time. 'He was anobleman and th
2 CONC e boar insignia on his helmet could mean that he was aprince. He appear
2 CONC s to have died when middle-aged, so he had probablybecome a war leader b
2 CONC y fighting many bloody battles in his youth. Hewould have grown up in a v
2 CONC illage, living in a timber-framed long-housewith a thatched roof. As an a
2 CONC ristocrat he would have learned how tofight with a spear and sword from a
2 CONC n early age. He would have honed hisskills hunting wild boar, deer, bea
2 CONC r and wolf in the forests thatcovered the country. As he grew older he w
2 CONC ould have carved out a namefor himself leading bands of men into war ag
2 CONC ainst rival tribes. After ahard day of hunting and pillaging he would h
2 CONC ave come home to his wivesand children. A goat, sheep or part of a cow w
2 CONC ould be thrown into thelong-hut's cauldron and his band would drink bee
2 CONC r, mead or wine. Theprince would have led a very war-like lifestyle. Ev
2 CONC en when he died hissword was buried with hime to prepare him for a simi
2 CONC lar existence in theafter-life.' Prof. R Cramp of Durham University, En
2 CONC gland. From meagresurviving records it appears that the first king of M
2 CONC ercia was Creodaruling from 585 and he was an Ickeling. He was succeede
2 CONC d by his sonPybba in 597. The most famous Ickeling and last of the 'old p
2 CONC aganreligion' was king Penda (582-654) and his genealogy links him with
2 CONC Woden and his spouse Freyr. The penny coin is named after him. Aformida
2 CONC ble ruler he rivalled the power of the Christian Northumbriankings. The r
2 CONC ivalry between the two kingdoms was notorious. Penda haddefeated and ki
2 CONC lled Edwin in 633 and Oswald in 642. Both Penda and hisspouse Cyneuise r
2 CONC emained lifelong adherents to their inherited beliefsat a time when the c
2 CONC onversion of the English to Christianity wasproceeding apace. Their eld
2 CONC est son Peada had been made Prince of theMiddle Angles by Penda. In 653
2 CONC , Peada along with all the Middle Anglesbecame Christian converts in or
2 CONC der to marry Alchfled, daughter ofPenda's rival, king Oswy of Northumbr
2 CONC ia. This did not deter Penda fromcontinuing his campaigns against North
2 CONC umbria and in the following yearon 15 Nov 654, Penda was defeated and k
2 CONC illed at the battle of the riverWinwaed by Oswy and this was hailed by B
2 CONC ede as a victory for Christ overthe pagan gods. SOURCES: Bede: Ecclesia
2 CONC stical History of the EnglishNation, The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle. Beowulf
2 CONC . The 'Pioneer' Burial: IanMeadows: Current Archaeology 154. Northampto
2 CONC n Chronicle & Echo, 23 April1997. Prof Rosemary Cramp of Durham Univers
2 CONC ity. FROM:http://www.bartleby.com/, The Columbia Encyclopedia, Sixth Ed
2 CONC ition. 2001 Anglo-Saxons name given to the Germanic-speaking peoples wh
2 CONC osettled in England after the decline of Roman rule there. They werefir
2 CONC st invited by the Celtic King Vortigern, who needed help fighting thePi
2 CONC cts and Scots. The Angles (Lat. Angli), who are mentioned in TacitusGer
2 CONC mania, seem to have come from what is now Schleswig in the laterdecades o
2 CONC f the 5th cent. Their settlements in the eastern, central, andnorthern p
2 CONC ortions of the country were the foundations for the laterkingdoms known a
2 CONC s East Anglia, Mercia , and Northumbria. The Saxons, aGermanic tribe wh
2 CONC o had been continental neighbors of the Angles, alsosettled in England i
2 CONC n the late 5th cent. after earlier marauding foraysthere. The later kin
2 CONC gdoms of Sussex, Wessex, and Essex were theoutgrowths of their settleme
2 CONC nts. The Jutes, a tribe about whom verylittle is known except that they p
2 CONC robably came from the area around themouths of the Rhine, settled in Ke
2 CONC nt and the Isle of Wight. TheAnglo-Saxons eventually formed seven separ
2 CONC ate kingdoms known as theheptarchy. The term 'Anglo-Saxons' was first u
2 CONC sed in Continental Latinsources to distinguish the Saxons in England fr
2 CONC om those on theContinent, but it soon came to mean simply the 'English'
2 CONC . The morespecific use of the term to denote the non-Celtic settlers of E
2 CONC nglandprior to the Norman Conquest dates from the 16th cent. In more mo
2 CONC derntimes it has also been used to denote any of the people (or theirde
2 CONC scendants) of the British Isles.  Heptarchy (hpA'tark)[Gr.,=seven-kingd
2 CONC om], name traditionally applied to the kingdoms ofAnglo-Saxon England i
2 CONC n the period prior to the Danish conquests of the9th cent. The term was p
2 CONC robably first used by 16th-century writers whobelieved that in those ea
2 CONC rly years England was divided into sevenkingdomsƒª'?orthumbria, East An
2 CONC glia, Mercia, Essex, Sussex, Wessex, andKent. Actually the political an
2 CONC d geographical divisions were neither soorderly nor permanent. At one t
2 CONC ime (c.600) there appear to have been asmany as 12 independent states, b
2 CONC ut the number of kingdoms, theirboundaries, and their political status s
2 CONC hifted constantly throughoutthis period.
