1 NOTE The Roman Period 55 BC - 401 AD. AD 63 - Joseph of Arimathea came toGla
2 CONC stonbury on the first Christian mission to Britain.
2 CONT Joseph of Arimathea was a wealthy disciple of Jesus, who, according tot
2 CONC he book of Matthew 27:57-60, asked Pontius Pilate for permission totake J
2 CONC esus' dead body in order to prepare it for burial. He alsoprovided the t
2 CONC omb where the crucified Lord was laid until hisResurrection. Joseph is m
2 CONC entioned in a few times in parallel passages inMark, Luke and John, but n
2 CONC othing further is heard about his lateractivities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Apocryphal legend, however, supplies us with the rest of his story bycl
2 CONC aiming that Joseph accompanied the Apostle Philip, Lazarus, MaryMagdale
2 CONC ne & others on a preaching mission to Gaul. Lazarus & Mary stayedin Mar
2 CONC seilles, while the others travelled north. At the English Channel,St.Ph
2 CONC ilip sent Joseph, with twelve disciples, to establish Christianityin th
2 CONC e most far-flung corner of the Roman Empire: the Island of Britain.The y
2 CONC ear AD 63 is commonly given for this "event", with AD 37 sometimesbeing p
2 CONC ut forth as an alternative. It was said that Joseph achieved hiswealth i
2 CONC n the metals trade, and in the course of conducting hisbusiness, he pro
2 CONC bably became acquainted with Britain, at least thesouth-western parts o
2 CONC f it. Cornwall was a chief mining district andwell-known in the Roman e
2 CONC mpire for its tin. Somerset was reknowned forits high quality lead. Som
2 CONC e have even said that Joseph was the uncle ofthe Virgin Mary and theref
2 CONC ore of Jesus, and that he may have brought theyoung boy along on one of h
2 CONC is business trips to the island. Hence thewords of Blake's famous hymn, J
2 CONC erusalem:
2 CONT
2 CONT And did those feet, in ancient time,
2 CONT Walk upon England's mountains green?
2 CONT
2 CONT It was only natural, then, that Joseph should have been chosen for thef
2 CONC irst mission to Britain, and appropriate that he should come first toGl
2 CONC astonbury, that gravitational center for legendary activity in theWest C
2 CONC ountry. Local legend has it that Joseph sailed around Land's Endand hea
2 CONC ded for his old lead mining haunts. Here his boat ran ashore inthe Glas
2 CONC tonbury Marshes and, together with his followers, he climbed anearby hi
2 CONC ll to survey the surrounding land. Having brought with him astaff grown f
2 CONC rom Christ's Holy Crown of Thorns, he thrust it into theground and anno
2 CONC unced that he and his twelve companions were "Weary All".The thorn staf
2 CONC f immediately took miraculous root, and it can be seenthere still on We
2 CONC aryall Hill. Joseph met with the local ruler,Arviragus, and soon secure
2 CONC d himself twelve hides of land at Glastonburyon which to build the firs
2 CONC t monastery in Britain. From here he becamethe country's evangelist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much more was added to Joseph's legend during the Middle Ages. He wasgr
2 CONC adually inflated into a major saint and cult hero, as well as thesuppos
2 CONC ed ancestor of many British monarchs. He is said to have broughtwith hi
2 CONC m to Britain a cup, said to have been used at the Last Supper andalso u
2 CONC sed to catch the blood dripping from Christ as he hung on theCross. A v
2 CONC ariation of this story is that Joseph brought with him twocruets, one c
2 CONC ontaining the blood and the other, the sweat of Christ.Either of these i
2 CONC tems are known as The Holy Grail, and were theobject(s) of the quests o
2 CONC f the Knights of King Arthur's Round Table. Onelegend goes on to sugges
2 CONC t that Joseph hid the "Grail" in Chalice Well atGlastonbury for safe-ke
2 CONC eping (Photo)
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a wide variance of scholarly opinion on this subject, however,
2 CONC and a good deal of doubt exists as to whether Joseph ever came toBritai
2 CONC n at all, for any purpose.
2 CONT
2 CONT Person Source
