2 CONC g8, 2001, Internet. Conn of the Hundred Battles #110 Alternate nameform
2 CONC s: Conn Ceadcatha, Conn Ceadcathach; ('Conn of the Hundred Fights'),Con
2 CONC n Cetchathach mc Feidelmid, Con Read Cead Chathach, Conn Ceadchatach,Co
2 CONC nn Cedeathach (the Hundred fighter) Son of Feidhlimidh Reachtmhar#108 a
2 CONC nd Una, daughter of the king of Lochloinn. Married to Eithne. Connand E
2 CONC oghan Mor, also called Mogha Nuadhad, fought a great battle atMaynooth i
2 CONC n 123 AD and split Ireland in half. 'Resulting from thisbattle, Mogha f
2 CONC orced Conn to divide Ireland with him into two equalparts by the bounda
2 CONC ry of Esker Riada, a long ridge of hills from Dublinto Galway, the sout
2 CONC h part he termed his and called it after his ownname, Leath Mogha, or '
2 CONC Mogha's Half of Ireland'. The northern part wascalled Leath Cuinn, or C
2 CONC onn's Half.' 'Conn also gave his daughter,Sadbh, in marriage to Oiloll O
2 CONC lum, Son of Eoghan Mor.' Descent from theGreat Kings of Ireland. 'Conn'
2 CONC s life and reign were ended by hisassassination at Tara. Fifty robbers h
2 CONC ired by the king of Ulster, cameto Tara, dressed as women, and treacher
2 CONC ously despatched the Monarch.' AHistory of the Irish Race. Another vers
2 CONC ion of his death is: Slain byTibraite (Tiobraide) Tireach, son of Mal #
2 CONC 107, son of Rochraidhe[Tipraiti Tirech la mc Mail m. Rochride 696], Kin
2 CONC g of Ulster, at TuathAmrois. Father of Art #112.
