1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Ebalus Mancer (also known as Manzer, Manser and Manzeras) was born abou
2 CONC t870, an illegitimate son of Ramnulf II, comte de Poitou. The meaning o
2 CONC fMancer is disputed. Mancer is a Germanic habitational name, but also a
2 CONC Germanic personal name formed from 'magin', meaning 'strength' or'might
2 CONC ' (magnus). It may also be a corruption of the Hebrew 'mamzer',meaning '
2 CONC bastard', hence the appellation sometimes seen, Ebalus 'theBastard', an
2 CONC d his supposed Jewish mother. The same name was used byanother prince f
2 CONC rom Occitania. Arnaud Manzer, count of Angoulême (born952 - died 988/92
2 CONC ) who was also a bastard. This fact makes problematicthe speculation ab
2 CONC out the Germanic origin of the name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ebalus was count of Poitou and duke of Aquitaine on two occasions: from
2 CONC 890 to 892 and from 902 (Poitou) and 927 (Aquitaine) to his death. Hesu
2 CONC cceeded his father Ramnulf in 890, but was driven out in 892 by Aymar,w
2 CONC ho was supported by Eudes of France. Ebalus gained the backing ofGuilla
2 CONC ume I 'le Pieux', count of Auvergne, who placed Aquitaine underhis own a
2 CONC uthority in 893.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 892 Ebalus married Aremburgis and they had two sons: Ebalus whobe
2 CONC came bishop of Limoges, and Guillaume who would have progeny.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 902 Ebalus launched the reconquest of his county with an army lent b
2 CONC yhis distant relative Guillaume I 'le Pieux'. He took Poitiers whileAym
2 CONC ar was away and established control of the county. He was invested asco
2 CONC unt by Charles III, with whom Ebalus had been raised. The comitaltitle w
2 CONC as the only one to which he ever had legitimate investiture.Ebalus allo
2 CONC tted the abbey of Saint-Maixent to Savary, viscount ofThouars, who had b
2 CONC een his constant supporter. He restructured Poitou bycreating new visco
2 CONC unties in Aulnay and Melle, and he dissolved the titleand position of v
2 CONC iscount of Poitou upon the death of its holder Maingaudin 925.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 904 Ebalus conquered the Limousin. In 911 he was in Chartres with an
2 CONC army to oppose Rollo, the Viking leader to whom King Charles 'theSimple
2 CONC ' gave Normandy the same year. Guillaume, the son of Ebalus, wouldmarry G
2 CONC erloc of Normandy, daughter of Rollo, in 935.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 927, Guillaume II, successor of Guillaume I 'le Pieux', and then his
2 CONC Guillaume II's successor, his brother Acfred, died within a year. Acfre
2 CONC dhad made Ebalus his heir; Ebalus thus found himself duke of Aquitaine,
2 CONC and count of Berry, Auvergne, and Velay.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 929 Raoul, king of France, started to reduce the power of Ebalus. He
2 CONC withdrew from him access to Berry, then in 932 he transferred the title
2 CONC sof Duke of Aquitaine and Count of Auvergne to Raymond Pons, comte deTo
2 CONC ulouse. Moreover, the territory of La Marche, which was under thecontro
2 CONC l of the lord of Charroux, a vassal of Ebalus, was transformedinto an i
2 CONC ndependent county.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ebalus died in 934, and was succeeded by his son Guillaume.
