1 NOTE Regina Rifles History
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT On June 14, German shells, the "moaning minnies," with their highpierci
2 CONC ng screech hailed down on the Battalion positions, hitting anammunition d
2 CONC ump in the unit's vehicle park. The fire burned throughoutmost of the n
2 CONC ight. Fortunately, there were no injuries but five vehicleswere destroy
2 CONC ed. In the three days between June 12 and 14, the Battalionsustained a f
2 CONC urther 14 fatalities.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 15, battalion headquarters fortuitously moved to a new position
2 CONC 400 yards further west. The headquarters had previously been locatednea
2 CONC r the church in Bretteville. Shortly after the headquarters moved,Germa
2 CONC n artillery destroyed the steeple with a direct hit, causing thebell an
2 CONC d spire to crash to the ground in rubble. The new battalionheadquarters p
2 CONC osition was also shelled that night. Because there hadbeen no previous s
2 CONC helling in that particular area there was a strongsuspicion that the Ge
2 CONC rmans might have received information from aninformer in the village. H
2 CONC owever, nothing could be proven. The shellingcaused two more fatalities f
2 CONC or the Battalion.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Bray for a Rest
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 17,1944, a fighting patrol sent out by C Company was caught inG
2 CONC erman crossfire. The patrol suffered 13 casualties, six of whom werefat
2 CONC al. During that day the Battalion was relieved in the line by theQueen'
2 CONC s Own Rifles, and moved back a couple of miles to Bray to rest.The Regi
2 CONC na Rifles had been continuously in the line for 11 days. Ontheir first d
2 CONC ay out of the line, June 18, it began to drizzle andcontinued raining f
2 CONC or the first two days of their brief rest. This badweather was to delay t
2 CONC he planned build-up of the Allied forces for theirbreak-out into Norman
2 CONC dy. The Rifles were to stay at Bray until June 29.During the following f
2 CONC ew days a course was held at the Battalion sniperschool. Candidates wer
2 CONC e sent out to the front line for on the jobtraining with live targets! D
2 CONC uring the rest period, the Battalion wasvisited by General Crerar and M
2 CONC ajor General Keller who both expressedsatisfaction with the Battalion's a
2 CONC ctions to that date, and with the waythe men had conducted themselves.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Move Inland: Securing the Bridgehead
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 19, a furious Channel storm broke up the Mulberry Harbour which
2 CONC had been towed over to Omaha Beach in the American assault sector. OnSu
2 CONC nday, June 25, Padre Jamieson held church services in the Companyareas a
2 CONC nd finally, on June 26, the allied break-out in Normandy began.The Batt
2 CONC alion was put on one hour's notice to move from noon, but theorder was r
2 CONC escinded and the men stood down at 1945 hours that night. OnJune 29, 19
2 CONC 44, the Battalion moved back into the line. They left Brayfor the villa
2 CONC ges of Rots and La Villeneuve to relieve the 7th RecceRegiment. There t
2 CONC he Battalion took up positions astride the Caen-Bayeuxroad to counter t
2 CONC he threat from German armour which might come from Caenwhere it had bee
2 CONC n massing for yet another counter-attack. Dominion Dayfound the Battali
2 CONC on digging in at La Villeneuve where A Company observeda German patrol t
2 CONC hat withdrew. The next day the Battalion was visited byGenerals Stuart, K
2 CONC eller, and Folkes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the chateau at Rots, Colonel Matheson held an officers' mess dinnerw
2 CONC hile shells from Carpiquet and Abbey of Ardennes bounced off the walls.
2 CONC Utilizing their army rations, the officers dined off fine dinnerwarefro
2 CONC m the Chateau recently abandoned by the owners. Although the food waspo
2 CONC or the wine from the cellars was good. Only half the officers of theBat
2 CONC talion were able to attend; the others had to stay on duty in case ofan a
2 CONC ttack.
2 CONT
2 CONT Carpiquet air field was one of the German strong points supposed to hav
2 CONC ebeen taken by 9th Brigade on D-Day or the day after. However, it did n
2 CONC otfall and remained a serious obstacle to the break-out. On July 4, as a
2 CONC prelude to the attack on Caen, Operation WINDSOR was launched, in which
2 CONC troops from 8th Brigade, including the Queens' Own Rifles and the Royal
2 CONC Winnipeg Rifles detached from 7th Brigade for that operation, stormedCa
2 CONC rpiquet and took it. The Reginas provided the fire support. WhenMatheso
2 CONC n went forward to get a better view from C Company's position, adud Ger
2 CONC man artillery round caved in his trench but he survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Abbey of Ardennes
2 CONT On the night of July 7, Operation CHARNWOOD, a three division frontalas
2 CONC sault on Caen, began with the first time attempt to use heavy bombingas a p
2 CONC relude to an attack by the army. The air attack devastated thecity. The n
2 CONC ext day, the move towards Caen began. Units of 9th Brigadetook Gruchy, B
2 CONC uron, and Authie. Ardennes Abbey was being used by the12th SS Panzer Re
2 CONC giment as a headquarters and was heavily defended withmortars, machine g
2 CONC uns, and 75mm guns. On July 8, the Regina Rifles weretasked to capture i
2 CONC t.
2 CONT
2 CONT To capture the Abbey, Matheson planned to attack with three companies,B
2 CONC , C, and D, and keep the still under strength A Company in reserve.Befo
2 CONC re attempting the assault on the Abbey, Gordon Brown and Major Tubbdid a c
2 CONC areful reconnaissance. They climbed a church steeple north ofRots where t
2 CONC hey were able to see the fields stretching out betweenAuthie and the Ab
2 CONC bey. They didn't like what they saw. The area was flat,open, and devoid o
2 CONC f cover where an attacking force would easily be seen.What is more, the d
2 CONC efenders had the advantage of dug in defences andclear fields of fire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The North Nova Scotia Regiment had reached Authie at 1600 hours. H hour
2 CONC for the Reginas' attack was set at 1700 hours. At that time of year, th
2 CONC esun did not set until around 10:30 so the attack would take place inda
2 CONC ylight. B Company, under Major Eric Syme, was the first to move to theB
2 CONC attalion start line at Authie. As the Company went forward from theasse
2 CONC mbly area, two German machine guns opened up on the advancing troopsinf
2 CONC licting heavy casualties. Circumstances were such that B Companyreceive
2 CONC d little artillery or armoured support. In spite of theconcentrated mac
2 CONC hine gun fire, the company struggled through Authie andreached their fi
2 CONC rst objective, some mounds between them and the abbey.They had taken 61 c
2 CONC asualties in this short advance.
2 CONT
2 CONT C Company moved to its start line at 1725 hours also under heavy fire.H
2 CONC owever, it pushed on, passing through B Company's position, andstruggle
2 CONC d over the open area towards the abbey. They were hit with tankand accu
2 CONC rate mortar fire which caused many casualties, including thecompany com
2 CONC mander, Major Tubb and all the officers and senior NCOs. Only21 men of t
2 CONC he Company remained in action and they were forced towithdraw to B Comp
2 CONC any's position under fire.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the left, with C Company, D Company moved forward from Authie towith
2 CONC in 500 yards of the abbey also under machine gun and rifle fire.Each of t
2 CONC he platoons wriggled forward using fire and movement. Sectionsshifted u
2 CONC nder covering fire of other sections, and the men were forcedto crawl o
2 CONC r run in shorts bursts. One of the company's platoons deployedin a left f
2 CONC lanking attack while the other two platoons attacked underthe cover of s
2 CONC moke fired from their two inch mortars. The raggedplatoons finally reac
2 CONC hed their objective, the east side of the abbey, at2230 hours.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gordon Brown returned from the abbey and guided A Company forward in th
2 CONC efailing light to help consolidate the position sustaining 15 casualtie
2 CONC son the way. All night long, the men held on behind the wall of theabbe
2 CONC y, against a deadly hail of German machine gun fire from as close as200 y
2 CONC ards. After a fierce fire fight at first light the abbey wassecured and t
2 CONC he Germans driven out. As the German counter fireslackened, the men's s
2 CONC pirits were given a further boost by theiruncovering the abbey's wine s
2 CONC tock!
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battalion suffered 11 officer and 205 other ranks casualties, 36 of
2 CONC them fatal, with one missing in action. This had been the worst fightin
2 CONC gfor the Battalion since D-Day. The capture of the abbey by the Riflesh
2 CONC elped pierce the ring of defences of Caen. That action, and the success
2 CONC of British forces on the left flank of the Canadians, forced the German
2 CONC sto withdraw back into Caen itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caen
2 CONT A short time later the depleted Battalion moved to St. Germain, a subur
2 CONC bon the western outskirts of Caen. Here it was visited by General Crera
2 CONC rand Brigadier Foster. On July 10, after prolonged and heavy fighting,C
2 CONC aen fell to the Allies. On that day the headquarters of 2nd CanadianCor
2 CONC ps under Lieutenant General Guy Simonds arrived in France, and thecomma
2 CONC nd of 3rd Canadian Division passed to 2nd Canadian Corps. TheBattalion w
2 CONC as then moved to another section of Caen to become thebrigade reserve b
2 CONC attalion.
2 CONT
2 CONT During its brief stay in Caen, the Battalion mounted the 1st Ceremonial
2 CONC Guard. One hundred men, under Captain J. Treleaven, were issued newbatt
2 CONC ledress, belts, and anklets, and were taken by vehicle to the centreof C
2 CONC aen where they mounted guard at La Place St. Martin. There they wereins
2 CONC pected by the Corps Commander, Lieutenant General Simonds, and theCanad
2 CONC ian Red Ensign was unfurled for the first time in France. On July13, th
2 CONC e command of the guard was passed to Captain Hector Jones. Atmidnight o
2 CONC n July 13, the Battalion moved to the Stormont, Dundas, andGlengarry Hi
2 CONC ghlanders. The relief was completed by 0230 hours underheavy German mor
2 CONC tar and artillery fire from positions across the OrneRiver. On July 14, t
2 CONC he Battalion scout officer. Lieutenant Bergeron,took his men forward to c
2 CONC omplete a reconnaissance in the German heldterritory. At 2330 hours Ber
2 CONC geron's patrol moved across the EpronBridge, a partially destroyed rail
2 CONC way bridge, leading to the city ofVaucelles. The bridge crossed the Orn
2 CONC e about 1,000 yards to the leftfront of the Battalion's position. The G
2 CONC ermans had not withdrawn fromVaucelles, and were still manning the tren
2 CONC ches which constituted thecity's defence. While the patrol was out on i
2 CONC ts reconnaissance, A and DCompanies were withdrawn to the town of Epron f
2 CONC or a rest. Bergeron andhis patrol returned without incident. On July 16
2 CONC , the remainder of theBattalion was also withdrawn to Epron, being reli
2 CONC eved by the 7th RecceRegiment. While they were in Epron the Battalion b
2 CONC egan preparations forits assault across the Orne.
2 CONT
2 CONT First Across The Orne
2 CONT On July 18, orders arrived. Operation GOODWOOD, the British armouredbre
2 CONC akout offensive across the River Orne southeast of Caen, was to belaunc
2 CONC hed. Operation ATLANTIC, the Canadian part of GOODWOOD, also began.The 3
2 CONC rd Canadian Division was given the task of crossing the Orne Riverand c
2 CONC apturing Vaucelles. As part of the operation, the Reginas were toget ac
2 CONC ross the river and link up with units of the 9th Brigade on theirleft. C
2 CONC ivilians together with members of the French Interior Force (FFI)were t
2 CONC o act as guides to the Battalion. Guides were attached to each ofthe co
2 CONC mpanies and given battle dress and regimental flashes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In preparation for the assault, 1,000 Lancaster and Halifax bombers wer
2 CONC esent over to attack the factory areas at Colombelles and Vaucelles. To
2 CONC beef up the weight of fire, an intense artillery bombardment was added.
2 CONC The Regina Rifles were put on one hour notice to move to theirconcentra
2 CONC tion area at 0800 hours.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after this initial phase of the attack, Lieutenant Bergeronagai
2 CONC n led a patrol across the Orne, this time in daylight, to determineif, a
2 CONC s a result of the heavy pounding the Germans had withdrawn. Thepatrol s
2 CONC curried across the Orne over two wrecked bridges under intenseGerman fi
2 CONC re which killed one man. Machine gun fire raked the bridge,pinning down t
2 CONC he patrol's communications group, who had set up their 46Set on the Cae
2 CONC n side of the river.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile the Battalion began moving to its forming up positions at St.
2 CONC Julien with A and D Companies moving out to their start line at 1500hou
2 CONC rs. Their passage was obstructed by heaps of rubble caused by theprevio
2 CONC us intense bombing and shelling. With C and B Companies following,the l
2 CONC ead companies began to cross the Orne on a two company front at twosepa
2 CONC rate points.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the other side of the river, the isolated patrol was in desperatenee
2 CONC d of reinforcements but none were available. Accordingly Bergerondecide
2 CONC d to construct a passageway over a gap in the bridge, where thecentre h
2 CONC ad collapsed but he came under increasing small arms andautomatic fire f
2 CONC rom German positions and had to go to ground.
2 CONT
2 CONT The carrier platoon and the battalion mortars now moved into position o
2 CONC nthe north bank of the Orne to give covering fire for the crossing. By1
2 CONC 700 hours, German fire had been neutralized and by 1715 hours thehazard
2 CONC ous crossing began. A and B Companies and the Carrier platoon gotacross t
2 CONC he river and began to clear out snipers on the other side. DCompany, on t
2 CONC he left hand crossing, was delayed by accurate Germanmortar and machine g
2 CONC un fire which damaged the company's assault boats.They finally managed t
2 CONC heir crossing by bridge, followed shortly by CCompany.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 2100 hours, all the companies of the Battalion had struggled acrossa
2 CONC nd were in position on their final objectives. The Battalion suffered18 c
2 CONC asualties, including three fatalities in this hard foughtengagement, bu
2 CONC t as a result could proudly claim the distinction of beingthe first Can
2 CONC adian infantry to cross the Orne.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 19, the companies tightened up their defensive positions inVauc
2 CONC elles, and completed their mopping up operations. To that date theyhad t
2 CONC aken 60 to 70 prisoners and   more kept trickling in. Also on thatday t
2 CONC he remainder of 7th Brigade crossed the Orne, and a patrol from theBatt
2 CONC alion was sent to the area that the Royal Winnipeg Rifles were tooccupy
2 CONC . The patrol bagged seven more prisoners of war, two motor cycles,two b
2 CONC ottles of cognac, and were treated to excellent meals. Here,unfortunate
2 CONC ly, the Battalion suffered two more fatalities. On July 20and 21, while t
2 CONC he rest of the British and Allied forces were crossingthe Orne on their w
2 CONC ay to Falaise, the Battalion remained at Vaucellesfor a brief breathing s
2 CONC pell. At this time things were not going well forGermany. A sign of how b
2 CONC ad things were occurred on July 20 when seniorGerman army officers unsu
2 CONC ccessfully attempted to assassinate Hitler.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 22, while still at Vaucelles, the Battalion was treated to amob
2 CONC ile bath parade with the accompanying exchange of socks, shirts, andund
2 CONC erwear. Both the Roman Catholic and Protestant chaplains conductedchurc
2 CONC h services. That night, at 1730 hours, a nasty surprise in the formof a "
2 CONC buzz bomb" landed in Caen, and a marked increase in German airattacks o
2 CONC ccurred from which the Battalion suffered a number ofcasualties.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Breakout
2 CONT On July 23,1944, the 1st Canadian Army headquarters under LieutenantGen
2 CONC eral H.D.G. Crerar became operational in France. Plans for thebreakout o
2 CONC f the Allied armies from the Normandy beachhead were now beingput into o
2 CONC peration. The America forces began their offensive, OperationCOBRA, und
2 CONC er General Omar N. Bradley on July 25. At the same time theCanadian arm
2 CONC y launched Operation SPRING, the first stage of the drive toFalaise. On J
2 CONC uly 25, the Battalion moved to an assembly area along arailway embankme
2 CONC nt prior to the attack on La Hague. Before the attackcould be launched t
2 CONC he troops had to wait for the North Nova ScotiaHighlanders to take Till
2 CONC y-la-Campagne. Although the railway embankmentoffered some protection, t
2 CONC he Battalion suffered many casualties from theconstant German artillery
2 CONC , and from bombing and strafing missions fromthe Luftwaffe. The North N
2 CONC ova Scotia Highlanders punched into Tilly, butwere pushed back and forc
2 CONC ed to withdraw through the Battalion lines. TheGermans then began conce
2 CONC ntrating tanks from Fontenay-le-Marmion andmoving them to La Hague. Bec
2 CONC ause of this build up of armour theBattalion's attack on La Hague was c
2 CONC alled off. During the waiting periodin this area, the Battalion suffere
2 CONC d three more fatalities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Given the change in plans, the Battalion was tasked to hold its present
2 CONC position and to prepare for an attack on Tilly in three or four daystim
2 CONC e. The Battalion was to keep up the pressure on the German forces andpr
2 CONC event them from withdrawing so they could be used against British andAm
2 CONC erican forces to the west. During these days, Allied armoured advancesw
2 CONC ere being made in other areas along the front. Consequently, theBattali
2 CONC on was forced to hang onto the positions at the embankment forseveral m
2 CONC ore days.
2 CONT
2 CONT At midnight, on July 30, and throughout the early hours of the nextmorn
2 CONC ing, while German aircraft dropped flares to illuminate the area forthe
2 CONC ir artillery, the Battalion pulled back through Vaucelles, over theOrne t
2 CONC o Caen. They eventually reached the Orchards of Colomby where theywere g
2 CONC iven a rest period.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since June 6, the Battalion had been continuously in action over aperio
2 CONC d of 55 days. From D-Day it had suffered a total of 678 casualties,incl
2 CONC uding 185 killed and eight missing in action. The Battalionsustained an a
2 CONC dditional fatality during its withdrawal to Colomby.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since D-Day the Battalion doctors had done a remarkable job under themo
2 CONC st difficult conditions. Battalion medical officers who were generalpra
2 CONC ctitioners often had to become surgeons because it was not possibleto w
2 CONC ait until ambulances took the wounded to field hospitals. CaptainW.S. H
2 CONC uckvale, who landed with Battalion Headquarters on D-Day, treatedhundre
2 CONC ds of Canadian and German soldiers as well as French civilians. InJuly 1
2 CONC 944 he suffered a severe head wound and was evacuated to Canada.Doctor H
2 CONC uckvale was awarded the Military Cross. He was succeeded byDoctor Harry D
2 CONC ickson. As well, the medical staff and stretcher-bearersunder Sergeant A
2 CONC lf Allen performed valiantly under trying conditions.Allen was also a m
2 CONC ember of the Battalion band.
2 CONT
2 CONT While the Battalion was regrouping, the men from the unit who had beens
2 CONC lightly wounded in previous engagements now returned as reinforcements.
2 CONC While at Colomby the Battalion played sports, went to the movies,splash
2 CONC ed in the mobile baths, and lazed on the beaches. There wereexcursions b
2 CONC ack to the scene of the D-Day assault and visits to thegraves of friend
2 CONC s who had fallen there. While the Battalion was at restin Colomby, the t
2 CONC roops were treated to the Canadian Army Show staged bythe Legion Auxili
2 CONC ary War Services in the "Windmill Theatre," a cavern inthe quarries of F
2 CONC ontaine-Henry that could seat over a thousand people.The Battalion was a
2 CONC lso visited by Lieutenant General Simonds and MajorGeneral Keller, and b
2 CONC y the war correspondents, J.A.M. Cook, who coveredthe Regina Rifles for t
2 CONC he Leader Post, and Gregory Clark. It was herethat the officers of the B
2 CONC attalion true to their old tradition heldtheir first formal mess dinner o
2 CONC f the war on the Continent.
2 CONT
