1 REFN 1164
1 REFN 218
1 OCCU a carpenter
1 EVEN
2 TYPE Misc
2 NOTE The ancestry of Francis Eaton is not known. He is believed to have been
3 CONC of the group that boarded the Mayflower in London.
3 CONT Francis came on the Mayflower with his wife Sarah and son Samuel who wa
3 CONC sa "suckling child".
3 CONT
3 CONT There are several clues to Eaton's ancestry, however. In anapprenticesh
3 CONC ip record dated 4 December 1626, reads as follows:
3 CONT
3 CONT "John Morgan son of Edward Morgan of the City of Bristol, sailor,deceas
3 CONC ed, apprenticed to Francis Eaton of the City of
3 CONT Bristol carpenter and Dorothy his wife for seven (7) years, paying 4s 6
3 CONC dfor the liberties of Bristol with two (2) suits of apparel
3 CONT and one suit 'telorum pertenr ad artem le carpinter.' [in margin: "TheM
3 CONC r. at New England"] . . . The Master consenteth at the
3 CONT end of the said term to convey to the apprentice and his heairs forever
3 CONC 25 acres of land lying in New England in America and
3 CONT also to give until him 15 bushels of wheat he serving him truly the ter
3 CONC mof his apprenticeship."
3 CONT
3 CONT This probaby isn't the Francis Eaton of the Mayflower however. Here are
3 CONC the reasons. This is because it says "Dorothy his wife
3 CONT for seven years". If this Dorothy is the second wife of Francis, whosen
3 CONC ame is not known, then he would have had to marry her
3 CONT in 1619--but he sailed on the Mayflower in 1620 with his wife Sarah.
3 CONT
3 CONT The things that make this document tempting, however, are two facts.Fir
3 CONC st it calls Francis Eaton a carpenter. The Francis Eaton
3 CONT of the Mayflower was also called a carpenter. Second, the Francis Eaton
3 CONC of the Mayflower is the only known "Francis Eaton"
3 CONT to have lived in America up through about 1700. So what would be thecha
3 CONC nces of there being an unrecorded Francis Eaton
3 CONT living in New England with the same occupation? Perhaps themisunderstan
3 CONC ding about the wife Dorothy is due to the slow
3 CONT exchange of news from the Plymouth colony and the rest of the world.
3 CONT
3 CONT Another possible clue is found in "The Pastor of the Pilgrims", a booka
3 CONC bout John Robinson. It says that a George and Francis
3 CONT Eaton were selling land c1591, in Fenton, England. That was thebirthpla
3 CONC ce of John Robinson, as well as of his wife, and Gov.
3 CONT John Carver's wife.
3 CONT
3 CONT
3 CONT
3 CONT BIOGRAPHICAL SUMMARY:
3 CONT
3 CONT Little is known about Francis Eaton, the man.  However, when he died in
3 CONC 1633, the inventory of his estate called him a
3 CONT carpenter.  There was some kind of disease going around in 1633, andsev
3 CONC eral Mayflower passengers died that year from it,
3 CONT including Francis Eaton and Peter Browne.
3 CONT
3 CONT The inventory of Francis Eaton's estate included one cow and a calf, tw
3 CONC ohogs, fifty bushels of corn, a black suit, a white hat
3 CONT and a black hat, boots, saws, hammers, an adze, square, awgers, achisel
3 CONC , boards, fishing lead, and some kitchen items.
