2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE Stephen Hopkins was one of only a few passengers on the Mayflower to ha
3 CONC vemade a prior trip to America. He came in 1609 on the Sea Venture head
3 CONC edfor Jamestown, Virginia. But instead, they were marooned on an island
3 CONC ,where the 150 passengers were stranded for nine months. Hopkins led an
3 CONC uprising ,challenging the governor's authority, and was sentenced todea
3 CONC th. But he begged and moaned about the ruin of his wife, and so waspard
3 CONC oned. The company built two vessels and escaped the island.
3 CONT After spending two years in Jamestown, Hopkins returned to England.Step
3 CONC hen Hopkins brought with him on the Mayflower his wife Elizabeth,childr
3 CONC en Giles and onstance by his first marriage, and Damaris by hissecond m
3 CONC arriage. A son Oceanus was born while the Mayflower was at sea.Stephen p
3 CONC articipated in the early exploring missions and was an"ambassador" alon
3 CONC g with Myles Standish for early Indian relations. In1636, Hopkins was f
3 CONC ined for the battery of John Tisdale, in 1637 he wasfound guilty of all
3 CONC owing men to drink on a Sunday at his house, and in1638 he was fined fo
3 CONC r not dealing fairly with an apprentice-girl,Dorothy Temple. He was als
3 CONC o charged with several other minor crimes,including selling glass at to
3 CONC o high a price, selling illegalintoxicants, and allowing men to get dru
3 CONC nk at his house. However, thisin no way indicated he was disloyal to th
3 CONC e Colony--in fact he was aprominent member, Assistant governor from abo
3 CONC ut 1633 until 1636, and hevolunteered to fight in the Pequot War of 163
3 CONC 7.
