2 NOTE Jabez Vaughn's place of residence during the revolution was Lyme, NewHa
3 CONC mpshire and Pomfret, Vermont. He is listed on the roll of the militiaco
3 CONC mpany in Lyme, 1776, under the command of Captain John Sloan. FromDecem
3 CONC ber 5 1776 to March 15 1777 he was a member of Captain JonasHaywords co
3 CONC mpany - last recorded rank was colonel. In 1777, he was onthe payroll o
3 CONC f Captain Jeremiah Post's Company in Colonel David Hobarts'Regiment and B
3 CONC rigadier General John Starkes Brigade, Militia July 1777Command of whic
3 CONC h company was given to Lieutenant Jabez Vaughn as Captainby Order of ge
3 CONC neral Stark the 27th day of August. On May 1, 1778, whilestill living i
3 CONC n Lyme, he bought of John Paddock for 50 pounds, 80 acresin Pomfret, Ve
3 CONC rmont, where he settled at once. Within two years, he sowon the good op
3 CONC inions of his fellow townsmen as to be elected asselectman. He held off
3 CONC ice frequently until 1791 when his family wasbroken up by the insanity o
3 CONC f his wife, who turned against him and alltheir children. He sold his f
3 CONC arm and arranged with John Paddock for thecare of his wife as long as s
3 CONC he should live, and went with three of hissons (Ebenezer, Jabez and Jon
3 CONC ah) to New Vineyard, Maine.
