1 REFN 740
1 EVEN
2 TYPE Misc
2 NOTE "was the first known Cobb to emigrate from England to the PlymouthColon
3 CONC y, Cape Cod, MA. Many distinguished descendants have long searchedfor h
3 CONC is English origin and background. In developing this vignette ofthe imm
3 CONC igrant I have drawn liberally from the scholarly works of PhilipL. Cobb
3 CONC , author of The Cobb Family (1907), Richard Cobb, Harvardprofessor and R
3 CONC ichard Cobb a retired Navy Supply Corps Captain. However,the hypotheses a
3 CONC nd conclusions contained herein are my own. (TracyAshley Crocker Sr.)
3 CONT
3 CONT       Much is known on the activities of Henry Cobb, the Puritan, in th
3 CONC eBay Colony but little has been done to unravel the specifics of hisori
3 CONC gin. There is general agreement that "The Elder Henry or the DeaconHenr
3 CONC y Cobb" came from an area in County Kent east of the Medway Riverwhich f
3 CONC lows out of the hills of southern Kent through Maidstone andChatham and i
3 CONC nto the Thames Estuary. It is within this area thatinhabitants are call
3 CONC ed "Men of Kent" (Jutish origin); those to the westof the Medway are kn
3 CONC own as "Kentish Men" (Saxon origin). ProfessorRichard Cobb, through stu
3 CONC dy and acquaintance with the Cape Coddescendants of Henry Cobb, suggest
3 CONC s that as a young man Henry Cobb was"rather short, with blue eyes and r
3 CONC eddish sandy hair." Interesting, asthis description agrees with the per
3 CONC ceived appearance of the GermanicJutes who invaded and settled in Great B
3 CONC ritain in the 5th century.
3 CONT       The most visible trail of "Henry the Elder" in England is found i
3 CONC nhis relationship with his church leader, the Reverend John Lothrop.Lot
3 CONC hrop, "a man of good family and education" was baptized at Eton on 20De
3 CONC cember 1584. He received BA and MA degrees at Queens College,Cambridge. I
3 CONC n 1611 we find him with the established Church of England atEgerton, Ke
3 CONC nt, a distance of about 15 miles from the probable home ofHenry Cobb in R
3 CONC eculver, Kent. Obviously disenchanted with the autocraticdogma of the K
3 CONC ing's Church, the Reverend Lothrop is found as minister ofan independen
3 CONC t church in London in 1623; and then on 29 April 1632, wefind him impri
3 CONC soned in London with 40 members of his flock for violatingthe laws rela
3 CONC ting to religious gatherings. Following two yearsimprisonment, Lothrop l
3 CONC eft England in the ship "Griffin" with his familyand some members of hi
3 CONC s church for the Plymouth colony, arriving there18 September 1634. Here H
3 CONC enry Cobb, the Lothrop protege who had been inthe Colony for about five y
3 CONC ears, responded to the call of his old friendand esteemed pastor. He ai
3 CONC ded the Reverend in getting his family andchurch established in the new
3 CONC ly formed town of Scituate. Lothrop'srecords published in the New Engla
3 CONC nd Register, Volumes IX and X, leaveslittle doubt of Henry Cobb's membe
3 CONC rship in Lothrop's London church:
3 CONT       "Uppon January 8, 1634, Wee had a day of humiliation and then atn
3 CONC ight joyned in covenaunt together, so many of us had beene in covenaunt
3 CONC before. To Witt.
3 CONT             Mr. Gilsonn and his wife
3 CONT             Goodman Anniball and his wife
3 CONT             Goodman Rowly and his wife
3 CONT             Goodman Cobb and his wife
3 CONT             Goodman Turner
3 CONT             Edward Foster
3 CONT             Myselfe
3 CONT             Goodman Foxwell
3 CONT             Samuel House."
3 CONT
3 CONT       It is very probable that immigrant Henry Cobb of Plymouth andAmbr
3 CONC ose Cobb of the Virgina Colony were of the same Kent Family, theyshared a c
3 CONC ommon progenitor in John Cobb, Esquire (b. ca 1300) of Cobb'sCourt, Rom
3 CONC ney, Kent. The suggested (but undocumented) relationshipsshown are base
3 CONC d primarily on the association of the family names,locations, and dates
3 CONC . That is, Henry Cobb of Plymouth Colony wouldappear to be a second son o
3 CONC f Henry Cobb (1561-1617) of Reculver, Kent.The son Henry was born about 1
3 CONC 605. (He married Patience Hurst inPlymouth in 1631; English Yeoman of h
3 CONC is time, married at age 26, thusthe basis for his date of birth). Most m
3 CONC en of Kent were farmers in anarea famous for hops, fruit, and grain. Ev
3 CONC en in this age, importance wasattached to the idea of status. The term "
3 CONC Yeoman" was commonly used inlegal and other documents to denote status a
3 CONC bove "Husbandman" (smaller,less prosperous farmer) and below that of "G
3 CONC entleman" (upper middleclass). Yeomen, from whom Henry Cobb was descend
3 CONC ed, were reasonably welleducated. Some Yeomen sons attended the univers
3 CONC ities; some becameclergymen. A review of the Cobbs of Kent and a person i
3 CONC nspection of theManor houses at Reculver and Eastleigh Court suggest 16
3 CONC th century gentrybut 17th century Yeomen. Suffice it to say that the em
3 CONC igrant Henry Cobbdid not inherit his father's estate. The major inherit
3 CONC ance, by custom,probably went to Benjamin Cobb, the first-born son. Thi
3 CONC s situation, aswell as the significant influence of Reverend Lothrop, c
3 CONC ould have giventhe impressionable 18 year old Henry Cobb ample justific
3 CONC ation to seek anapprenticeship in the shops or pubs of London in 1623, t
3 CONC he year thatLothrop formed his church there. The influence of the chari
3 CONC smatic Lotropon the Cobbs of Reculver must have been substantial. The C
3 CONC obb home atReculver was about 15 miles from Egerton, Kent where Lothrop w
3 CONC as inresidence from 1611 to 1623. Henry Cobb, the assumed father of the
3 CONC emigrant Henry, was himself censured by the establishment. He had becom
3 CONC eLord of the Manor of Bishopstone, Reculver Kent, when his father Richa
3 CONC rddied in 1582. In the record of the Visitations of Archdeacon ofCanter
3 CONC bury in 1599 is found the following "We present these personswhose name
3 CONC s are hereunder written for they refuse to pay unto a cessmade by diver
3 CONC s of our parish for the reparation of our said church:.....Henry Cobb 3 s
3 CONC hillings, 10 pence (owed)."
3 CONT       The nature of Henry Cobb's apprenticeship or trade in London isop
3 CONC en to conjecture. The fact that he came from an area rich in hops andgr
3 CONC ain and later in the Colony he was authorized to dispense winesuggests t
3 CONC he production and/or sale of ale, the national beverage of theera. From t
3 CONC he Plymouth Colony Record 1173; "5 June 1644, Henry Cobb islycensed to d
3 CONC raw wine at Barnstable."
3 CONT       What better place than an English pub in the 17th century to keep
3 CONC abreast of politics, religion and emigration. Henry Cobb of London must
3 CONC certainly have been aware of a number of significant events, viz: Thati
3 CONC n 1604, in a declaration at Hampton Court, James I said of thePuritans, "
3 CONC I shall make them conform themselves or I will harry them outof the Lan
3 CONC d or else do worst." Henry must have known the story of anundereducated g
3 CONC roup of separatists called "Pilgrims" who sough refugefirst in Amsterda
3 CONC m and subsequently in Leyden, Holland; and theunwilling to be assimilat
3 CONC ed into the Dutch culture, made their way toPlymouth in 1620 aboard the "
3 CONC Mayflower". (of the 101 passengers on thefirst voyage of the Mayflower, 3
3 CONC 5 were Leyden adventurers.) In 1628 thePuritans of Henry Cobb's sect be
3 CONC gan their mass exodus. In 1630 JohnWinthrop, a strong and able leader, l
3 CONC ed nearly 1000 Puritans with theircattle and horses to settlements in t
3 CONC he Massachusetts Bay Colony. Ingeneral the Puritans were a wealthier an
3 CONC d better educated class than thePilgrims but they shared their deeply r
3 CONC eligious convictions. It wasshortly after the Winthrop departure that H
3 CONC enry Cobb made his move,probably in the ship "The Anne" in 1629. Other p
3 CONC ossible ships include "Mayflower II' and the "Little James", which also a
3 CONC rrived at Cape Cod in1629. Professor Cobb said in his character analysi
3 CONC s of Henry, theemigrant, that he was "sensible, shrewd, adverse to maki
3 CONC ng trouble orbeing a part of it." Cobb didn't linger in London long eno
3 CONC ugh to bejailed in the famous "clink" with the zealous Lothrop and his f
3 CONC ollowersin 1632. When the great Civil War of 1642 came about to settle t
3 CONC hequestion of supremacy between King and Parliament, High Church andPur
3 CONC itans, Henry Cobb is found saving souls and selling wine inMassachusett
3 CONC s."
3 CONT (John E. Cobb, Col., US Army)
