2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE Andre was involved in some local Acadian history, and was quite famous i
3 CONC nthat area. It seems that Claude de LaTour held the St. John River area
3 CONC in what is now New Brunswick (then Acadia). Isaac de Razilly of Tourain
3 CONC egranted Latour the Signeurie de Jemseg, a rich hunting and fishing are
3 CONC aalong the Sainte John River in New Brunswick. It was there that Claude
3 CONC de LaTour built the fort Jemsig. Andre Bernard arrived in Acadia in 164
3 CONC 1in order to serve with Sieur Charles de Latour at Fort Jemseg. In Apri
3 CONC l,.... de Aulinay (Governor of Acadia) decided to mount a decisive atta
3 CONC ckon fort Jemseg. Madame de LaTour and 50 men bravely resisted the atta
3 CONC ck.At the time, Claude de LaTour was in Boston. It was a murderous figh
3 CONC t.d'Aulnay lost 33 men, but succeeded in capturing the fort after athre
3 CONC e-day battle ending on April 17, 1645. He seized 'jewels, silver andfur
3 CONC nishings worth up to 10,000 louis'. Many of LaTour's men were hangedaft
3 CONC er Council deliberations, 'to serve as an example, and as a lesson topo
3 CONC sterity of such an obstinate rebellion.' Andre Bernard's life wasspared w
3 CONC hen he signed an affidavid on May 15, 1645. After the taking ofFort Jem
3 CONC seg by d'Aulnay, in 1645 he escaped to France vai New York, andlater re
3 CONC turned to New France after the rehabilitation of Claude deLaTour, by th
3 CONC e court of France. It's possible that Andre's wife was anIndian, which w
3 CONC as not all that uncommon in those times, as he spent mostall his life i
3 CONC n the woods of Canada.
