2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE A legend states that a Gillis woman from Morar married one of the MacIa
3 CONC nMacDonaldfs from Glencoe, and lived in Glencoe. She escaped the Massac
3 CONC reperpetrated by the Campbells under English orders in 1692. Her husban
3 CONC dwas killed, but she made her way home over the mountains to Morarcarry
3 CONC ing their child. Living with her parents, she brought up the childas a G
3 CONC illis, fearing Campbell vengeance on a survivor of the Massacre.When An
3 CONC gus "Pioneer" settled in America nearly a century later he tookback the p
3 CONC roper name of MacDonald which his ancestor had abandoned.
2 SOUR @S121@
3 PAGE "Squire" Angus grand-daughters, Rachel and Dolena MacDonald, had thisexplanation received from their father.
3 _TMPLT
4 FIELD
5 NAME Page
5 VALUE "Squire" Angus grand-daughters, Rachel and Dolena MacDonald, had thisexplanation received from their father.
1 EVEN
2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE Subject: Descendants of Angus (Gillis) MacDonald, Morar, Inverness-shir
3 CONC e,Scotland and his wife, Elizabeth Grant.
3 CONT In St. Andrew's Cemetery, Egerton are two gravestones, side by side,clo
3 CONC se to the place where the Catholic Church stood during the earlyyears o
3 CONC f the 19th century. On one stone is "In memory of Angus McDonaldof Fren
3 CONC ch River. Died August 9, 1835, aged 80. Cairid na chiad Gaeil athainigI
3 CONC n duthch". On the other stone is "In memory of Elizabeth Grant,spouse t
3 CONC o Angus McDonald, departed this life April 18, 1825, aged 67years". She w
3 CONC as the daughter of Peter Grant, Little Harbour.
3 CONT Angus "Pioneer" MacDonald came to Prince Edward Island (then SaintJohn'
3 CONC s Island) with John MacDonald of Glenaladale, a Clanranaldchieftain, in t
3 CONC he early 1770ts. With his chieftain he fought against theAmerican invad
3 CONC ers who attacked Quebec in 1775.
3 CONT Angus "Pioneer" and his brother John (Ban) Gillis settled in Arisaig.Bu
3 CONC t Angus soon moved west,-taking up a lot of land on MerigomishHarbour, i
3 CONC n what is now Egerton. The home of Angus and Elizabeth becamea stopping p
3 CONC oint for many travellers making the journey between Pictouand the new s
3 CONC ettlements in Antigonish and Cape Breton. This is thereason for the Gae
3 CONC lic inscription on Angus's gravestone: "friend of thefirst Highlanders w
3 CONC ho came to the country". Family tradition has it thatFather (later Bish
3 CONC op) Angus B. MacEachern and Father Vincent de Paul theTrappist missiona
3 CONC ry enjoyed the MacDonald hospitality.
3 CONT We know of seven children born to Angus and Elizabeth:
3 CONT (1)Mrs. MacNeil (Anne)
3 CONT (2)John
3 CONT (3)Angus (Squire)
3 CONT (4)Donald
3 CONT (5)Peter (Paddy)
3 CONT (6)Mrs. MacK. (?)
3 CONT (7)Jane - Mrs. "Pilot" MacDonald, Pictou
3 CONT Explanations:
3 CONT (2)John had 13 children; his wife was a Chisholm.
3 CONT (3)"Squire" Angus, so-called because he was a Justice of the Peace, was
3 CONC killed in 1850 in an accident on the Albion Mines Railway. He wasmarrie
3 CONC d twice, both times to MacDonalds from Little Harbour.
3 CONT (4)Donald was married to Catherine MacDonald. They had four sons, twoda
3 CONC ughters - Donald, "Big Angus", Paddy, John (Custos), Annie andElizabeth
3 CONC . A descendant of this union, Donald "Point Betty", brought upa large f
3 CONC amily on Point Betty Island in Merigomish Harbour. His daughterKatherin
3 CONC e is the family historian. Donald's son "Big Angus" marriedEunice MacLe
3 CONC od. A son, Father W.B. MacDonald, was long-time pastor ofLourdes Parish i
3 CONC n Pictou County. Another descendant of "Big Angus" wasAngus "Printer" M
3 CONC acDonald of Egerton and New Glasgow. Angus "Printer's"first wife was "S
3 CONC quire" Angus' grand-daughter. By her he had a number ofchildren: Bernar
3 CONC d, Donald, Bertha (Mrs. Bob Beaton, Pictou), Vincent,and others. The "P
3 CONC rinter's" second wife was Pauline "Blacksmith"MacDonald, they had no ch
3 CONC ildren. John "Custos" was a public official(Custos Rotulorum) at North G
3 CONC rant, Antigonish County. Descendants ofJohn "Custos" include the childr
3 CONC en of John "Piper" MacDonald (well-knownas "Jack the carpenter" at St.F
3 CONC .X. in the 19401s). John Piper's wife wasCatherine Sutton, grand-daught
3 CONC er of John "Custos". They had 9 children:Robbie, well-known teacher in A
3 CONC ntigonish; Hugh A.; J.J.; Frank; SisterMonica, C.N.D.; Mrs. James Hanra
3 CONC han, and others. A daughter of John"Custos" son Donald was Mrs. "Johnny J
3 CONC im" Chisholm, Brierly Book. Herson, Dan, retired CN Telegraph foreman, l
3 CONC ived in New Glasgow. A brotherof Mrs. "Johnny Jim", Peter, was drowned i
3 CONC n Massachusetts in 1898. (Alineman, he was splicing a cable under a bri
3 CONC dge over the ConcordRiver.)
3 CONT (5)Peter "Paddy" married a Campbell from Antigonish.
3 CONT Comments
3 CONT (A)Of the numerous descendants of Angus "Pioneer" and his wife, no onen
3 CONC ow (1992) lives in Egerton to carry on the MacDonald name (Katherine,hi
3 CONC storian, is a summer resident).McCarrons, MacInnises and A.A.MacKenzie a
3 CONC re descendants, but are not MacDonalds. Many of the relativesare in the U
3 CONC nited States.
3 CONT (B)Barnabas McGee, Roys, Copelands and others were settled in what isno
3 CONC w Merigomish before Angus and Elizabeth came to these shores (andMicmac
3 CONC s and Acadian French before any of them). But Angus and Elizabethwere H
3 CONC ighland Scotch pioneers in what is now Egerton. The community nowcalled M
3 CONC erigomish was long known as French River. (The ICR railroadstation at M
3 CONC erigomish was called French River Station for some years).Angus lived n
3 CONC ear the mouth of Sutherland's River, and is known to somedescendants as "
3 CONC Angus the River".
3 CONT (C)We might note the large number of nicknames - "Squire" Angus;"Widow'
3 CONC s Angus" (also called "Cabbage Angus"); "John California"; "Sandythe Ca
3 CONC ulker"; Donald "Blacksmith"; Donald "Point-Betty"; "Old YoungJohn"; "Ol
3 CONC d Young John"s Big Johnny's John"; Angus "Paddy"s" Allan;Jimmy "Island J
3 CONC ohn" - his father was from Prince Edward Island; Katiefrom the Ponds; A
3 CONC ngus "Governor"; Angus "Jimmy Dan"; and there areothers.
3 CONT (D)There were many second marriages - usually of widowers. It was ahard l
3 CONC ife for women. One branch of the family had so many daughtersmarry wido
3 CONC wers that my grandmother - Janet (McVicar) MacEachen and hersister-in-l
3 CONC aw -- Catherine (Grant) McVicar wished to put a large sign atthe gate t
3 CONC o that relative's property: MORE WIDOWERS WANTED. Familypressure stoppe
3 CONC d the project.
3 CONT There was also a considerable amount of intermarriage among cousins.
3 CONT (E)Remarkably little is known yet of Angus "Pioneer's" three daughtersa
3 CONC nd their descendants.
3 CONT There may also have been another son of the pioneer -Alex.
3 CONT (F)How did Angus "Pioneer" come to change his name to MacDonald?Several s
3 CONC ources suggest that he did it out of gratitude to hischieftain, MacDona
3 CONC ld of Glenaladale. But "Squire" Angus grand-daughters,Rachel and Dolena M
3 CONC acDonald, had an explanation received from theirfather. A Gillis woman f
3 CONC rom Morar married one of the MacIan MacDonaldfsfrom Glencoe, and lived i
3 CONC n Glencoe. She escaped the Massacre perpetratedby the Campbells under E
3 CONC nglish orders in 1692. Her husband was killed,but she made her way home o
3 CONC ver the mountains to Morar carrying theirchild. Living with her parents
3 CONC , she brought up the child as a Gillis,fearing Campbell vengeance on a s
3 CONC urvivor of the Massacre. When Angus"Pioneer" settled in America nearly a c
3 CONC entury later he took back theproper name of MacDonald which his ancesto
3 CONC r had abandoned.
3 CONT A.A. MacKenzie
3 CONT Department of History
3 CONT St. F. X. University
