2 NOTE Very Sad News.--Last week a letter was received by Rev. Father McKenzie
3 CONC of Arisaig from Alexander Ross at Portland, Oregon, giving the sad news
3 CONC of the drowning in Alaska waters of the writer's brother, Andrew Ross,a
3 CONC nd his wife, who about three months ago left their former comfortableho
3 CONC me at Arisaig for the West. Mr. Ross had gone to the Pacific with thein
3 CONC tention of engaging in business, having some years ago lived in theStat
3 CONC es of Washington and Oregon. After reaching the Coast he visitedseveral o
3 CONC f the leading cities of British Columbia and Washington, andfinally eng
3 CONC aged with a large mercantile house at Seattle to take chargeof an agenc
3 CONC y for them at Skagway, Alaska. Accompanied by his wife he setout for th
3 CONC at place on a steamer which was wrecked on the Alaskan coast,between Fo
3 CONC rt Wrangel and Skagway a few weeks ago and many of whosepassengers peri
3 CONC shed. The profoundest sorrow is expressed on all handsover the sad fate o
3 CONC f this estimable young couple, both of whom wereuniversally admired and r
3 CONC espected. Mr. Ross was a young man of sterlingcharacter, always noted f
3 CONC or his through honesty and integrity. His agedparents, Mr. and Mrs. Don
3 CONC ald Ross, have the sincerest sympathy in theirsad affliction. Mrs. Ross w
3 CONC as a daughter of the late Donald McDonald ofAvondale, Pictou Co., and a g
3 CONC randdaughter of the late Allan McDonald, ofKeppoch, whose death at the a
3 CONC ge of 100 was reported last week's Casket.She was educated at Mount St. B
3 CONC ernard, here, and was a lady of more thanordinary refinement, and a gre
3 CONC at favorite with all her friends. May theyrest in peace.
