1 NOTE After having lost his child-wife, Isabelle of Flanders, he went oncrusa
2 CONC de, then hurried back to marry again for the sake of his dynasty ashis s
2 CONC on, Louis, was a sickly child. What he needed was the daughter of aking a
2 CONC nd, on 14 August 1193, he married Ingeborg (Isambour), daughter ofKing V
2 CONC aldemar of Denmark. Arrangements had been made for her to becrowned que
2 CONC en the day after the nuptials but, during the wedding night,Philippe's f
2 CONC eelings changed to repulsion.  In Compiègne, before anassembly, fifteen d
2 CONC uly sworn witnesses, twelve of them from the king'sfamily, solemnly cal
2 CONC culated the degrees of consanguinity and showed thatPhilippe and Ingebo
2 CONC rg were fourth cousins, a prohibiting degree formarriage. However, this s
2 CONC olution was not accepted by Ingeborg's brother,the Danish king, who app
2 CONC ealed to Pope Celestine III, claiming thegenealogies to be wrong, but t
2 CONC he pope gave Philippe no more than awarning.   In June 1196, Philippe I
2 CONC II married the beautiful Agnès deMeran. With Ingeborg still alive, this w
2 CONC as bigamy. The new pope,Innocent III, ordered Philippe to part from Agn
2 CONC ès and, laying Franceunder an interdict, wanted to suspend all religiou
2 CONC s services.Negotiations were to last fifteen years and, because of the C
2 CONC atharupsurge, the  interdict was not applied.    In 1201 in Soissons, t
2 CONC hechurch confronted Philippe but, after a fortnight's arguing, hedepart
2 CONC ed, taking Ingeborg with him. On 29 July 1201 Agnès de Meran diedand Ph
2 CONC ilippe could no longer be regarded as a bigamist; and so, inNovember of t
2 CONC hat year, the pope legitimised the two children of Philippeand Agnès. I
2 CONC n 1205 a 'damsel from Arras' bore him a bastard son and, asPhilippe wou
2 CONC ld have nothing to do with Ingeborg, she was spared theperils of childb
2 CONC earing.  As it had not been consummated, the pope waswilling to declare t
2 CONC he marriage with Ingeborg void. However, they hadnot counted on Ingebor
2 CONC g who maintained that she and Philippe had slepttogether. To satisfy po
2 CONC pe, king and queen, the only solution seemed tobe that the queen should t
2 CONC ake the veil and enter a convent; but then, inApril 1213, Philippe anno
2 CONC unced he was taking back his wife.
