2 CONC hesame year he married Eleanore de Poitou, Duchess of Aquitaine, soexte
2 CONC nding the Capetingian lands to the Pyrenees. He continued hisfather's p
2 CONC rogram of appointing trustworthy people of lower origin to theadministr
2 CONC ation of his government, thus improving the prestige of themonarchy. Fr
2 CONC om 1141 to 1143 he was involved in a fruitless conflict withThibaut, Co
2 CONC unt of Champagne, and the papacy. After this period hisrelations with t
2 CONC he popes improved to such an extent that he supportedPope Alexander II a
2 CONC gainst Frederick Barbarossa and even allowed the poperefuge in France. H
2 CONC owever, the main threats to his kingdom came fromGeoffrey, Count of Anj
2 CONC ou, and later from Geoffrey's son, the futureHenry II, King of England. W
2 CONC hen Louis VII went on crusade, he took hiswife with him. However, on th
2 CONC eir return he divorced her, the groundsofficially being consanguinity, b
2 CONC ut the reasons were her rumouredaffairs. Almost immediately she married K
2 CONC ing Henry II of England, takingAquitaine with her. Louis VII then marri
