2 CONC ho became the mother of hisson and heir, the future King Philippe II Au
2 CONC gust. Louis VII might havedefeated King Henry II had he made a concerte
2 CONC d attack instead of thehalf-hearted attacks on Normandy, while at the s
2 CONC ame time France wasspared attacks from the Anglo-Normans because of the
2 CONC ir internalquarrels. He also benefited from the long-standing quarrel b
2 CONC etween HenryII and Thomas Becket, Archbishop of Canterbury, and a revol
2 CONC t of Henry'ssons.  @ M  k  EAlix de Blois-Champagne  F  E(1140-1206), P
2 CONC age 2.  FKing Louis VII of France was in urgent need of a son and heir a
2 CONC s hisfirst two wives, Eleonore de Poitou and Constance of Castile, had m
2 CONC adehim the father of only four girls. And so, on 13 November 1160, only a
2 CONC month after the death of his second wife, he married Alix de Blois. In1
2 CONC 165 she became the mother of a son, Philippe II August, later to befoll
2 CONC owed by two more daughters. In 1180, Louis VII died, making the15-year-
2 CONC old Philippe II king of an area little larger than the Ile deFrance. Pe
2 CONC rhaps this was the reason why he took some of his mother'scastles, caus
2 CONC ing a quarrel. However, these disagreements were settledand, when Phili
