1 NOTE At age twenty, Philippe I, King of France, married Bertha of Holland, t
2 CONC hestep-daughter of his first cousin. This marriage had been arranged to
2 CONC seal the reconciliation between the king and his first cousin, the Coun
2 CONC tof Flanders. However, it took Bertha about six years before she produc
2 CONC eda daughter, Constance, which was not the hoped-for heir. Arnoul, a ho
2 CONC lyhermit of Saint-Médard in Soissons who was always consulted on family
2 CONC problems, prayed to heaven; however, it still took another three yearsb
2 CONC efore the heir, the future King Louis VI, was born. He was followed byt
2 CONC hree more sons. Twenty years after the marriage, Philippe imprisonedBer
2 CONC tha in comfort in the château at Montreuil-sur-Mer. He then wed thestil
2 CONC l- married Bertrade de Montfort l'Amauri, wife of the Count ofAnjou, an
2 CONC d they produced four children. It is lost in time whether sheseduced hi
2 CONC m or he her, but most likely Philippe had an understandingwith the Coun
2 CONC t of Anjou. In any case Betrade was more than willing asshe did not wan
2 CONC t to be "sent away like a whore," as her husband had doneto her predece
2 CONC ssors. Philippe's remarriage caused a sensation but notdisapproval. The o
2 CONC nly one who caused problems was Yves, bishop ofChartres, who had been a
2 CONC ppointed by Pope Urban II without consultationswith Philippe and this h
2 CONC ad been resented by the latter. The king hadinvited all bishops to his s
2 CONC econd wedding but Yves declined, referringto Philippe as committing big
2 CONC amy. Although Philippe had married with theblessing of the Archbishop o
2 CONC f Reims as well as the Papal legate, Yveswrote to the Pope who then for
2 CONC bade the bishops to crown Bertrade andtold Philippe to cease all relati
2 CONC ons with her or else be excommunicated.Next, Bertha, his first wife, di
2 CONC ed and Philippe gathered two archbishopsand eight bishops in Reims who a
2 CONC ll confirmed the royal second marriage.The Pope also put pressure on th
2 CONC e womanising Count of Anjou who thenobediently complained about the kin
2 CONC g's committing adultery with hiswife. In 1096 Philippe pretended to hav
2 CONC e broken with Bertrade andconsequently the excommunication was lifted. H
2 CONC owever, when it becameobvious in 1099 that Bertrade was still with him, t
2 CONC he excommunicationwas renewed. It took until 1105 before peace was rest
2 CONC ored and from thenon Philippe and Bertrade remained together till Phili
2 CONC ppe died in 1108.
