1 NOTE Roi de France
2 CONT
2 CONT King of France and founder of the Capetingian dynasty, he was the eldes
2 CONC tson of Hugues "The Great". When his father died in 956, he succeeded t
2 CONC ohis numerous fiefs around Paris and Orl‚ans; and his cousin, theFranki
2 CONC sh king Lothair, recognised him somewhat reluctantly as Duke ofthe Fran
2 CONC ks. Many of the counts in northern France did homage to him astheir ove
2 CONC rlord, and Richard I, Duke of Normandy, was both his vassal andhis brot
2 CONC her-in-law. His authority extended over certain districts southof the L
2 CONC oire and, owing to his interference, Lothair was obliged torecognise hi
2 CONC s brother Henri as Duke of Burgundy. Hugues Capet supportedhis royal su
2 CONC zerain when Lothair and Emperor Otto II fought for thepossession of Lor
2 CONC raine; but chagrined at the king's conduct in makingpeace in 980, he we
2 CONC nt to Rome to conclude an alliance with Otto. Layingmore stress upon in
2 CONC dependence than upon loyalty, Hugues appears to haveacted in a haughty m
2 CONC anner toward Lothair, and also towards his son andsuccessor, Louis V. H
2 CONC owever, neither king was strong enough to punishthis powerful vassal. W
2 CONC hen Louis V died without children in May 987,both Hugues and the late k
2 CONC ing's uncle, Charles, Duke of Lorraine, werecandidates for the vacant t
2 CONC hrone, and in this contest the energy ofHugues's champions Adelberon, A
2 CONC rchbishop of Reims, and Gerbert,afterwards Pope Sylvester II, prevailed
2 CONC . Declaring the Frankish crown tobe an elective and not an hereditary d
2 CONC ignity, Adelberon secured theelection of his friend and crowned him, pr
2 CONC obably at Noyon, in July 987.Charles of Lorraine was not prepared to bo
2 CONC w before his successful rivaland, before Hugues had secured the coronat
2 CONC ion of his son Robert as hiscollegue and successor in December 987, he f
2 CONC ound allies and attacked theking. Hugues was worsted during the earlier p
2 CONC art of the struggle and wasin serious straits until he was saved by the w
2 CONC iles of his partisan,Adelberon, who, in 991, treacherously seized Charl
2 CONC es and handed him overto the king. This capture virtually ended the war
2 CONC s, but one of its sideissues was a quarrel between Hugues and Pope John X
2 CONC V, who was supportedby the empire, then under the rule of the Empresses A
2 CONC delaide andTheophano as regents for the young Emperor Otto III. The ori
2 CONC gin ofHugues's name of Capet, which was also applied to his father, has b
2 CONC eenthe subject of some discussion. It is derived undoubtedly from the L
2 CONC atinCapa, or Cappa, 'a cape'; but whether Hugues derived it from the ca
2 CONC pewhich he wore as Abbot of St. Martin's, or from his youthful and play
2 CONC fulhabit of seizing capes, or from some other cause, is uncertain.
