1 NOTE Charles has become known as Charles The Great or Charlemagne for verygo
2 CONC od reasons. His long reign changed the face of Europe politically andcu
2 CONC lturally, and he himself would remain fixed in the minds of people inth
2 CONC e Middle Ages as the ideal king. In more recent times, many historiansh
2 CONC ave taken his reign to be the beginning of the Middle Ages 'proper'.Yet i
2 CONC n terms of territorial expansion and consolidation, of churchreform and e
2 CONC ntanglement with Rome, Charlemagne's reign was merelybringing the polic
2 CONC ies of his father Pippin to their logical conclusions.Charlemagne becam
2 CONC e the subject of the first medieval biography of alayman, written by Ei
2 CONC nhard, one of his learned courtiers. Following hisliterary model, Sueto
2 CONC nius's word portrait of the Emperor Augustus,Einhard described Charlema
2 CONC gne's appearance, his dress, his eating anddrinking habits, his religio
2 CONC us practices and intellectual interests,giving us a vivid if not perhap
2 CONC s entirely reliable picture of theFrankish monarch. He was strong, tall
2 CONC , and healthy, and ate moderately.He loved excercise: riding and huntin
2 CONC g, and perhaps more surprising,swimming. Einhard tells us that he chose A
2 CONC achen as the site for hispalace because of its hot springs, and that he u
2 CONC sed to bathe there withhis family, friends, and courtiers. He spoke and r
2 CONC ead Latin as well ashis native Frankish, and could understand Greek, an
2 CONC d even speak it alittle. He learned grammar, rhetoric, amd mathematics f
2 CONC rom the learnedclerics he gathered around him, but although he kept wri
2 CONC ting-tabletsunder his pillow for practice (he used to wake up several t
2 CONC imes in thenight) he never mastered the art of writing. It was because h
2 CONC e was atireless and remarkably successful general that he was able to m
2 CONC ake sucha mark upon European history. He concluded Pippin's wars with A
2 CONC quitaine,and proclaimed his son Louis as king in 781; the one serious d
2 CONC efeat hesuffered was in these wars, at Roncevaux in the Pyrenees, a def
2 CONC eat oneday immortalized in "The Song of Roland" and later 'chansons de g
2 CONC este'.He added Saxony to his realm after years of vicious campaigning; a
2 CONC nd,towards the end of his reign, moving against the Danes; he destroyed t
2 CONC hekingdom of the Avars in Hungary; he subdued the Bretons, the Bavarian
2 CONC s,and various Slav people. In the south he began the reconquest of Spai
2 CONC nfrom the Arabs and established the Spanish March in the north-east oft
2 CONC he peninsula. But perhaps his most significant campaigns were south oft
2 CONC he Alps, in Italy. Pope Hadrian appealed to Charlemagne for helpagainst D
2 CONC esiderius of the Lombards. The campaign in the winter of 773-4was short a
2 CONC nd decisive. Desiderius was exiled, and Charlemagne, "king ofthe Franks
2 CONC ", added "and the Lombards" to his title; later he appointedhis son Pep
2 CONC in as King of Italy. But popes were still not free of alltheir enemies. I
2 CONC n 799 Leo III was ambushed by a rival party of Romanaristocrats, who tr
2 CONC ied to gouge out his eyes and cut off his tongue. Leofled to Charlemage
2 CONC , who was at Paderborn preparing for another waragainst the Saxons. Cha
2 CONC rlemagne ordered Leo III to be restored and,later in the year 800, came t
2 CONC o Rome himself. On Christmas Day, in St.Peter's, Pope Leo III crowned C
2 CONC harlemagne Emperor of the Romans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naissance : ou le 2 Avril 742 à Ingelheim ALLEMAGNE Profession : Roi de
2 CONC sFrancs de 768 à 814, Empereur d'Occident de 800 à 814.
