2 NOTE Charlemagne (French for Carolus Magnus, or Carlus Magnus ("Charles theG
3 CONC reat"); German Karl der Grosse). first sovereign of the Christian Empir
3 CONC e of the West; born 2 April, 742;died at Aachen, 28 January, 814. Note, h
3 CONC owever, that the place of hisbirth (whether Aachen or LiŠge) has never b
3 CONC een fully ascertained, whilethe traditional date has been set one or mo
3 CONC re years later by recentwriters; if Alcuin is to be interpreted literal
3 CONC ly the year should be745. At the time of Charles' birth, his father, Pe
3 CONC pin the Short, Mayorof the Palace, of the line of Arnulf, was, theoreti
3 CONC cally, only the firstsubject of Childeric III, the last Merovinigian Ki
3 CONC ng of the Franks; butthis modest title implied that real power, militar
3 CONC y, civil, and evenecclesiastical, of which Childeric's crown was only t
3 CONC he symbol. It isnot certain that Bertrada (or Bertha), the mother of Ch
3 CONC arlemagne, adaughter of Charibert, Count of Laon, was legally married t
3 CONC o Pepin untilsome years later than either 742 or 745. chief protector a
3 CONC nd coadjutor in temporals, by Constantinople as atleast Basileus of the W
3 CONC est. This reign, which involved to a greaterdegree than that of any oth
3 CONC er historical personage the organicdevelopment, and still more, the con
3 CONC solidation of Christian Europe, willbe sketched in this article in the s
3 CONC uccessive periods into which itnaturally divides. The period of Charlem
3 CONC agne was also an epoch of reformfor the Church in Gaul, and of foundati
3 CONC on for the Church in Germany,marked, moreover, by an efflorescence of l
3 CONC earning which fructified inthe great Christian schools of the twelfth a
3 CONC nd later centuries. Short had appealed to Pope Zachary to recognize his a
3 CONC ctual rule with thekingly title and dignity. The practical effect of th
3 CONC is appeal to theHoly See was the journey of Stephen III across the Alps t
3 CONC wo years later,for the purpose of anointing with the oil of kingship no
3 CONC t only Pepin,but also his son Charles and a younger son, Carloman. The p
3 CONC ope then laidupon the Christian Franks a precept, under the gravest spi
