1 NOTE Emperor Conrad I had designated him as his successor and this wassuppor
2 CONC ted by the Saxon and Franconian nobility. Although he was thefirst non-
2 CONC Frank ruler of the Holy Roman Empire, he nevertheless adoptedFrankish c
2 CONC ustoms, dress and manners. As he regarded the support of thenobility as s
2 CONC ufficient for his position, he refused to be crowned by thebishops. Als
2 CONC o, as Duke of Saxony he had shown his independence fromEmperor and Chur
2 CONC ch; yet, as Emperor himself, he grew closer to thechurch. The most seri
2 CONC ous problem of his reign was the independence ofthe higher nobility. Th
2 CONC is had been caused by the disintegration ofmonarchical powers in the pr
2 CONC eceeding fifty years. He re-establishedimperial control over Suabia, Lo
2 CONC tharingia and Bavaria. However, ingeneral he allowed the dukes a free h
2 CONC and within their own territories.Several times after 925 he raided the t
2 CONC erritories of his uncivilizedneighbours, the Slavic Wends and the Hunga
2 CONC rian Magyars, takingBrandenburg from the Wends and, in 933, defeating t
2 CONC he Magyars in battle.Then in the conquered lands he built fortified cit
2 CONC ies as militarystrongholds. His rule restored much of the power and pre
2 CONC stige of themonarchy so that, before he died, he obtained the recogniti
2 CONC on of hisson, Otto, as his successor.
