1 NOTE St. Matilda
2 CONT Queen of Germany, wife of King Henry I (The Fowler), b. at the Villa of
2 CONC Engern in Westphalia, about 895; d. at Quedlinburg, 14 March, 968. Shew
2 CONC as brought up at the monastery of Erfurt. Henry, whose marriage to ayou
2 CONC ng widow, named Hathburg, had been declared invalid, asked forMatilda's h
2 CONC and, and married her in 909 at Walhausen, which he presentedto her as a d
2 CONC owry. Matilda became the mother of: Otto I, Emperor ofGermany; Henry, D
2 CONC uke of Bavaria; St. Bruno, Archbishop of Cologne;Gerberga, who married L
2 CONC ouis IV of France; Hedwig, the mother of HughCapet. In 912 Matilda's hu
2 CONC sband succeeded his father as Duke of Saxony,and in 918 he was chosen t
2 CONC o succeed King Conrad of Germany. As queen,Matilda was humble, pious, a
2 CONC nd generous, and was always ready to helpthe oppressed and unfortunate. S
2 CONC he wielded a wholesome influence overthe king. After a reign of sevente
2 CONC en years, he died in 936. Hebequeathed to her all his possessions in Qu
2 CONC edlinburg, Poehlden,Nordhausen, Grona, and Duderstadt.
2 CONT It was the king's wish that his eldest son, Otto, should succeed him.Ma
2 CONC tilda wanted her favourite son Henry on the royal throne. On the pleath
2 CONC at he was the first-born son after his father became king, she induceda f
2 CONC ew nobles to cast their vote for him, but Otto was elected andcrowned k
2 CONC ing on 8 August, 936. Three years later Henry revolted againsthis broth
2 CONC er Otto, but, being unable to wrest the royal crown from him,submitted, a
2 CONC nd upon the intercession of Matilda was made Duke ofBavaria. Soon, howe
2 CONC ver, the two brothers joined in persecuting theirmother, whom they accu
2 CONC sed of having impoverished the crown by her lavishalmsgiving. To satisf
2 CONC y them, she renounced the possessions the deceasedking had bequeathed t
2 CONC o her, and retired to her villa at Engern inWestphalia. But afterwards, w
2 CONC hen misfortune overtook her sons, Matildawas called back to the palace, a
2 CONC nd both Otto and Henry implored herpardon.
2 CONT Matilda built many churches, and founded or supported numerousmonasteri
2 CONC es. Her chief foundations were the monasteries at Quedlinburg,Nordhause
2 CONC n, Engern, and Poehlden. She spent many days at thesemonasteries and wa
2 CONC s especially fond of Nordhausen. She died at theconvents of Sts. Servat
2 CONC ius and Dionysius at Quedlinburg, and was buriedthere by the side of he
2 CONC r husband. She was venerated as a saintimmediately after her death. Her f
2 CONC east is celebrated on 14 March.
2 CONT Two old Lives of Matilda are extant; one, Vita antiquior, written in th
2 CONC emonastery of Nordhausen and dedicated to the Emperor Otto II; edited b
2 CONC yKOEPKE in Mon. Germ. Script., X, 575-582, and reprinted in MIGNE, P.L.
2 CONC ,CLI, 1313-26. The other, Vita Mahtildis reginae, written by order of t
2 CONC heEmperor Henry II, is printed in mon. Germ. Script., IV, 283-302, and i
2 CONC nMIGNE, P.L., CXXXV, 889-9220. CLARUS, Die heilige Mathilde, ihr Gemahl
2 CONC Heinrich I, und ihre Sohne Otto I, Heinrich und Bruno (Munster, 1867);S
2 CONC CHWARZ, Die heilige Mathilde, Gemahlin Heinrichs I. Konigs vonDeutschla
2 CONC nd (Ratisbon, 1846); Acta SS., March, II, 351-65.
2 CONT MICHAEL T. OTT
2 CONT
2 CONT Possible. Identité du père : Théodoric de Ringelheim et Dietrich deSaxe
2 CONC -Amelant pourraient être une seule et même personne, selon PierreRiché.
