1 NOTE In 917 the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle recorded: "Many people who had beenund
2 CONC er the rule of the Danes both in East Anglia and in Essex submittedto h
2 CONC im; and all the army in East Anglia swore agreement with him, thatthey w
2 CONC ould agree to all that he would, and would keep peace with allwith whom t
2 CONC he king wishes to keep peace, both at sea and on land."Overshadowed by h
2 CONC is father Alfred and upstaged by his son Athelstan, itwas Edward who re
2 CONC conquered much of England from the Danes (909-919),established an admin
2 CONC istration for the kingdom of England, and securedthe allegiance of Dane
2 CONC s, Scots, Britons, and English. Using Alfred'smethods and in alliance w
2 CONC ith Mercia, he spread English influence andcontrol. The Danes of Northu
2 CONC mbria were defeated (910) at Tettenhall (inStaffordshire), the Viking k
2 CONC ingdom of York acknowledged his power in918, and most Welsh kings submi
2 CONC tted to him. In 921 the submission of notonly Viking York and Northumbr
2 CONC ia but also of the kings of Strathclydeand of the Scots gave his kingdo
2 CONC m primacy in the British Isles. Edwardwas a patient planner, systematic o
2 CONC rganizer, a bold soldier; by the timehe died, he had completed the New M
2 CONC inster at Winchester where he himselfwas buried. Though twice married, h
2 CONC is eldest son and successor,Athelstan, was the son of a mistress.
2 CONT
2 CONT defeated the Danes and took East Anglia
2 CONT
2 CONT conquered Mercia
2 CONT
2 CONT conquered Northumbria
