1 NOTE About 1081 he married Adela of Normandy, the formidable daughter ofWill
2 CONC iam The Conqueror, and by her fathered seven children. WhenStephen's fa
2 CONC ther died in 1090 he became Count of Blois and Chartres.In1095 Adela be
2 CONC came Regent when her husband took part in the firstcrusade. He had no e
2 CONC nthusiasm for this duty but Adela considered that heought to go, so he w
2 CONC ent. There was never any nonsense in their householdabout who made the d
2 CONC ecisions---she did. Stephen de Blois went togetherwith Adela's brother, R
2 CONC obert, Duke of Normandy. Having marched souththrough Italy, Stephen and h
2 CONC is brother-in-law decided to spend thewinter comfortably in southern It
2 CONC aly before continuing on their way.When at last these two leaders decid
2 CONC ed to embark their men at Brindisi,disaster struck them. It was a very m
2 CONC ediaeval kind of disaster: thefirst ship to leave port capsized and san
2 CONC k with the loss of all handstogether with many pack-animals, stores, an
2 CONC d chests of money. Most oftheir armies allowed themselves to be shipped a
2 CONC nd, after a rough andunpleasant crossing, they reached Constantinople i
2 CONC n May 1096. Stephen deBlois was impressed by the city but reserved his g
2 CONC reatest admiration forthe Emperor Alexius. "Your father, my beloved," h
2 CONC e wrote to Adela, "mademany gifts, but he was almost nothing compared w
2 CONC ith this man". Onecannot help but wonder how the formidable Adela react
2 CONC ed to this remarkabout her father, William the Conqueror; however, sinc
2 CONC e her husband wasthe better part of two thousand miles away, perhaps fo
2 CONC r once he himselfdid not much mind how she reacted. Godfrey of Bouillon h
2 CONC ad attacked thecity of Nicaea and was joined, in early June 1096, by St
2 CONC ephen de Blois,Robert of Normandy and Raymond of Toulouse. They celebra
2 CONC ted theirassault by cutting off the heads of as many Turks as they coul
2 CONC d find andcatapulting them over the city walls into the streets. Others w
2 CONC ere setup on spikes in front of the gates in full view of the depressed
2 CONC defenders. A Byzantine force took control of the city and deprived thec
2 CONC rusaders of another massacre by allowing the Turks to escape. Stephenwr
2 CONC ote to his wife in high spirits: "In five weeks' time we shall be inJer
2 CONC usalem, unless we are held up at Antioch". After the jointcrusader-forc
2 CONC e defeated another Turkish army, the retreating Turks laidwaste to the c
2 CONC ountry, burning the crops and destroying or befouling thewells. The wea
2 CONC ther was still painfully hot and it was not long beforehunger and thirs
2 CONC t began to take their toll. Stephen wrote to Adela tosay that it was a g
2 CONC reat mistake to imagine that the sun always shone inSyria, for "through
2 CONC out this winter we have endured intense cold andincessant rain". Later S
2 CONC tephen marched his men to Alexandretta as he sawno point in having them m
2 CONC assacred by the Atabeg of Mosul. Afterwards hewas to be rebuked bitterl
2 CONC y for this 'cowardice' by the formidable Adela.He returned to France in 1
2 CONC 099 but was forced by the inexorable Adela toreturn to the East, there t
2 CONC o redeem his tarnished reputation which hedid by being killed in the Ba
2 CONC ttle of Ramleh.
