1 NOTE Adela of Normandy, daughter of William the Conqueror, was the mother of
2 CONC Stephen, King of England, whose right to the throne derived through her
2 CONC .She was married to Stephen, Count of Meaux en Brie. Upon the death ofh
2 CONC is father in 1090, her husband succeeded to the counties of Blois andCh
2 CONC artres. She took an active interest in civil and ecclesiasticalaffairs a
2 CONC nd was instrumental in rebuilding the cathedral of Chartres instone. In 1
2 CONC 095 Adela became Regent when her husband took part in thefirst crusade. H
2 CONC e had no enthusiasm for this duty but Adela consideredthat he ought to g
2 CONC o, so he went. There was never any nonsense in theirhousehold about who m
2 CONC ade the decisions---she did. Stephen de Blois wenttogether with Adela's b
2 CONC rother, Robert, Duke of Normandy. In 1099,Stephen returned to France bu
2 CONC t was forced by Adela to return to the Eastto redeem his, according to A
2 CONC dela, 'tarnished' reputation, which he didby being killed in the battle o
2 CONC f Ramleh, on 19 May 1102. Adela continuedas Regent during the minority o
2 CONC f her sons and was increasingly active inpublic life. Anselm, her guest a
2 CONC nd teacher in 1097, was oftenentertained by her during 1103 and 1105. C
2 CONC onsequently she affected atemporary reconciliation between him and her b
2 CONC rother, King Henry I ofEngland. In 1107 Adela entertained Pope Pascal d
2 CONC uring Easter and thefollowing year was hostess to Boemund of Antiochia. S
2 CONC he made her sonThibaud her successor in 1109 and entered a convent in t
2 CONC he diocese ofAutun. Here she continued to wield an important influence i
2 CONC n public andclerical affairs. She persuaded Thibaut to join her brother
2 CONC , Henri I,against France in 1117 and was a benevolent patroness of chur
2 CONC ches andmonasteries. She died in 1137 and was buried at Caen.
