1 NOTE He was born at Falaise, the bastard son of Robert, Duke of Normandy, by
2 CONC Arlette, a tanner's daughter. On his father's death in 1035, the nobles
2 CONC accepted him as duke, but his youth was passed in difficulty and danger
2 CONC .When in 1047 the lords of the western part of the duchy rebelled, Henr
2 CONC iI of France came to his help and the rebels were defeated atVal-des-Du
2 CONC nes. In 1051 he visited his cousin, Edward the Confessor, andreceived t
2 CONC he promise of the English succession. He married Matilda,daughter of Ba
2 CONC udouin V, Count of Flanders, in 1053. In the next tenyears William repu
2 CONC lsed two French invasions, and in 1063 conqueredMaine. Although he was n
2 CONC ever keen on actual capital punishment, Williamthe Bastard could get to
2 CONC uchy about jokes too near the bone and, when hecaptured the town of Ale
2 CONC ncon that had displayed flayed skins on itswalls in allusion to the tan
2 CONC ner's trade (his maternal grandfather,Fulbert, had been a tanner), he c
2 CONC hopped the right hand and left foot offeach citizen to teach them a les
2 CONC son about laughing last. Probably in1064, Harold was at his court and s
2 CONC wore to help him gain the Englishcrown on Edward's death. When, however
2 CONC , Edward died in 1066, Haroldbecame king. William laid his claim and, o
2 CONC n October 14, defeated Haroldat the battle of Hastings or Senlac. Harol
2 CONC d was slain and William wascrowned on December 25. The west and north o
2 CONC f England were subdued in1068; but next year the north revolted, and Wi
2 CONC lliam devastated thecountry between York and Durham. The constitution u
2 CONC nder William assumeda feudal aspect, the old national assembly becoming a c
2 CONC ouncil of theking's tenants-in-chief, and all title to land being deriv
2 CONC ed from hisgrant. The Domesday Book contains the land settlement. He al
2 CONC so broughtthe English Church into closer relations with Rome. The Conqu
2 CONC eror's rulewas stern and orderly. In 1070 there was a rebellion in the F
2 CONC en Countryand, under the leadership of Hereward the Wake, the rebels he
2 CONC ld out forsome time in the Isle of Ely. English exiles were sheltered b
2 CONC y theScottish king, Malcolm, who plundered the northern shires; but Wil
2 CONC liamin 1072 compelled Malcolm to do him homage at Abernethy. In 1073 he
2 CONC reconquered Maine. He also made a successful expedition into SouthWales
2 CONC . His eldest son, Robert, rebelled against him in Normandy in 1079.Havi
2 CONC ng entered on a war with Philippe I of France in 1087, Williamburned Ma
2 CONC ntes. As he rode through the burning town, his horse stumbledand he rec
2 CONC eived an injury of which he died at Rouen on September 9. Heleft Norman
2 CONC dy to his son Robert, and England to William.
