2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE There are very few families where nationality and religion played such a
3 CONC critical part of its history as it is with the Melanson/Melançon. InLou
3 CONC isiana and perhaps elsewhere, it is considered an "old" French name.In f
3 CONC act, it is not French, but rather a "Gallicized" version of theEnglish n
3 CONC ame, Mellison.
3 CONT The story begins with the fall of LaRochelle in 1628 when the Calvinist
3 CONC swere forced to leave France. One of these Calvinists was PierreLaverdu
3 CONC re. He went to England and married Priscilla Mellison in 1630.They had t
3 CONC hree sons, Pierre, Charles, and John. In 1657 the entirefamily boarded t
3 CONC he vessel "Satisfaction" under the command of PeterButler. They formed p
3 CONC art of the company which Thomas Temple, the newGovernor of English Nova S
3 CONC cotia, was transporting to Acadia. Pierre andPriscilla settled in Acadi
3 CONC a. Two of their sons married into the FrenchAcadian families already th
3 CONC ere. England ceded Acadia back to France in1667 in the Treaty of Breda. P
3 CONC ierre, his wife, and youngest son, Johnleft for Boston, seeking refuge i
3 CONC n Protestant New England "from thewrath of his Countrymen Papists, at J
3 CONC ohns fort and thereabouts." The twoolder Melanson sons remained in Acad
3 CONC ia after 1667. The elder Pierre hadto leave all his property in Acadia w
3 CONC hen he left for Boston, and thuswas forced to live in extreme poverty. H
3 CONC is son, John, was the onlysupport for the family. John quickly ran into p
3 CONC roblems of his own. Inlate 1676, he and Henry Lawton tricked some India
3 CONC ns from near CapeSable, Acadia onto a vessel so they could sell them as s
3 CONC laves on FayalIsland. Seventeen Indians, men, women, and children were i
3 CONC nvolved. Thisbehavior was illegal, and they were arrested. John was gra
3 CONC nted temporaryleave (bail) for 100 pounds supplied by Samuel Sendall, w
3 CONC hom his motherreferred to as her landlord. John never made it to trial a
3 CONC nddisappeared. His father, Pierre Laverdure searched for him as far asA
3 CONC cadia but never found him. The journeys weakened the elderly Pierre,and h
3 CONC e died in 1677. His widow, Priscilla, was obligated to repay the100 pou
3 CONC nds to her landlord and came to depend on public charity. Shemarried Ca
3 CONC ptain William Wright, an innkeeper from Dorchester,Massachusetts on Apr
3 CONC il 8, 1680 . Priscilla had sent for a granddaughterstill living in Acad
3 CONC ia, Marie Melanson.. Marie was the daughter of herson, Charles, who had m
3 CONC arried an Acadian and remained in Acadia. Sheraised Marie in the Protes
3 CONC tant religion in Massachusetts. Her son senthis daughter to Massachuset
3 CONC ts along with a loan of 40 pounds upon apromise that Marie would get al
3 CONC l of Priscilla's household belongingsupon her death. Priscilla's belong
3 CONC ings were inventoried, and thisinventory can be found in the Mass. arch
3 CONC ives (Vol. 37). Marie eventuallymarried David Basset. It appears that w
3 CONC hen Priscilla died in late 1691or 1692, while in her early nineties, Ca
3 CONC ptain Wright wanted to keep thepots and pans only to be sued by the gra
3 CONC nddaughter, Marie. The suit waspresumably settled out of court. Meanwhi
3 CONC le, the two "Catholic sons" wereraising their families in Acadia. The o
3 CONC ldest of the two sons whoremained in Acadia was Pierre. He was born in o
3 CONC r around 1632 and marriedMarie Marguerite Anne Mius d'Entremont at Port R
3 CONC oyal in 1665. Heapparently remained in contact with Boston, because he b
3 CONC orrowed 3000pounds from his nephew-in-law, David Basset. Pierre had 10 c
3 CONC hildren, 6girls and 4 boys.
1 EVEN
2 TYPE Misc
2 NOTE Many a writer has tried to explain the origins of this family. It wasge
3 CONC nerally believed that the Melanson were of Scottish origin, but thisass
3 CONC umption was based on a historic mistake. There is no document thatdemon
3 CONC strates that the Melansons who came to Acadia were of Scottishdecent, i
3 CONC n fact to the contrary there is proof that they were of Englishdecent. T
3 CONC he two Melanson brothers, PIERRE, dit Laverdure, a stonemason,born in 1
3 CONC 632, the spouse of Marie Marguerite Muis d'Entremont, thedaughter of th
3 CONC e Lord of Pomcoup, Philippe Mius d 'Entremont, andCHARLES, born in 1643
3 CONC , the husband of Marie Dugas, daughter of ArmourerAbraham Dugas and Mar
3 CONC guerite Doucet of Port Royal, arrived in Port Royalaccompanied their pa
3 CONC rents aboard the ship the Satisfaction, with SirThomas Temple, the new E
3 CONC nglish Governor of Acadia, which arrived in1657. During almost a centur
3 CONC y, just until the deportation of theAcadians in 1755, the settlement wa
3 CONC s the place of residence of CharlesMelanson and his wife Marie Dugas, a
3 CONC nd their descendents and associates."The Melanson Settlement" is one of t
3 CONC he principle Canadian archeologicalsites illustrating the way of rural l
3 CONC ife in Acadia in the 17th and 18thcenturies.
