2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE In 1631, Charles de La Tour gave to Philippe Mius d'Entremont the choic
3 CONC eto settle wherever he would like. He chose what was then known to theI
3 CONC ndians as Pobomcoup, (a place where holes have been made through theice t
3 CONC o fish). Charles La Tour erected the place into a barony, the firstever c
3 CONC onstituted in Acadia, and the second in all Canada. He gave toPhilippe M
3 CONC ius d'Entremont the title of Baron. The centre of the baronywas located o
3 CONC n the east side of the harbour, not far from its head. Itwas at this ti
3 CONC me, 1653, that Philippe came to live here with his wife(Madeleine Helie
3 CONC ) and their daughter (Marguerite). Who was born inFrance and was to bec
3 CONC ome the wife of Pierre Melanson, the founder ofGrand-Pre. It is here th
3 CONC at Philippe's four sons (Jacques, Abraham,Philippe, and Madeleine) were b
3 CONC orn. Philippe having been named AttorneyGeneral of the King in Acadia, h
3 CONC ad to follow the Governor wherever hewould be. That is why he did not s
3 CONC tay very long at the barony. He diedin Grand-Pré in 1700, probably at h
3 CONC is daughter's , being about the ageof 91. Charles La Tour, died in 1663
3 CONC .
1 OCCU MIUS (Muis) D’ENTREMONT, PHILIPPE, esquire, first baron of Pobomcoup (Pubnico), near Cap de Sable, lieutenant-major, king’s attorney, settler, the first of the d’Entremonts of Nova Scotia; b. c. 1601 (or 1609) in Normandy (probably at Cherbourg); d. c. 1700 (or 1701).
2 PLAC http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/mius_d_entremont_philippe_1E.html
