2 TYPE Misc
2 NOTE from the area of Picardy, (now in Vendee), along the English Channel.Ma
3 CONC rried in France to Marie Laurence. Confirmed as being in Quebec onOctob
3 CONC er 8, 1659, at 40 years of age. He was a mechant in the city ofQuebec. W
3 CONC orking for the Jesuites about 1645 and abt 1647 EustacheLambert had a '
3 CONC dit' name of Champagne. He came over with his brotherPierre, who was ki
3 CONC lled by the Iroquois on October 20, 1663 and buried atChateau-Richer. E
3 CONC ustache came to New France as a 'donne' or layassistant to the Company o
3 CONC f Jesus (Jesuits) and was a faithful companionof Father Gabriel Laleman
3 CONC t S.J., today known as Saint Gabriel Lalemant,one of the Canadian holy m
3 CONC artyrs. Eustache was born in 1618 in Boulognein the province of Picardy i
3 CONC n France, in a place known as Pas de Calais.From 1645 to 1647 he was in t
3 CONC he company of the Jesuit Fathers on theirdistant and perilous mission t
3 CONC o the West. On their mission to spread theGood News of Jesus to the dif
3 CONC ferent peoples that inhabited the countryat that time, the missionaries w
3 CONC ere supposed to establish posts ormissions as places where the people c
3 CONC ould convene together. This is whatthey did in this distant corner of N
3 CONC ew France, and gave the mission thename of Sainte-Marie. However, in 16
3 CONC 49, facing the menace of theIroquois who had already caused the martydo
3 CONC m of some missionaries, theyhad to abandon the settlement of Sainte-Mar
3 CONC ie and set fire to it toavoid it's desecration. Unfortunatly, Eustache L
3 CONC ambert didn't know howto write, but he was gifted with remarkable intel
3 CONC ligence. He veryquickly learned the Huron language and became an excell
3 CONC ent interpreter,a skill which proved to be very useful to the missionar
3 CONC ies. He was alsoskillful with a sword and rifle. In 1653, he was in com
3 CONC mand of the campwhich was organized several years prior to protect the c
3 CONC apital (Quebec).His courage and his dedication guided him. In 1653, whe
3 CONC n the city ofTrois-Rivieres was attacked by about 500 Iroquois, Eustach
3 CONC e commanded adetachment of fifty men sent from Quebec to aid in the def
3 CONC ense of thecity. According to the historian Monsieur Sulte, Eustache La
3 CONC mbert wasthe first commander of a militia corps in Canada. The first co
3 CONC lonizersat that time were given land grants and permitted to become pro
3 CONC pertyholders which is how Eustache acquired a tract of land on December 1
3 CONC 5,1652 in the territory held by the lord of Lauzon, and the contract wa
3 CONC sapproved by mr. Lauzon-Charny on October 11, 1653. That piece of landc
3 CONC omprised eight acres of frontage along the Saint Laurence River and was
3 CONC 40 acres deep. A fishery had been established on that land earler. Theo
3 CONC nly obligation imposed on the new land owner was that he give back toth
3 CONC e lord each year a tecnth of the eels that he caught. In exchange thelo
3 CONC rd was to furnish the barrels and the salt. Marie was buried in theceme
3 CONC tary that then existed in the cellars of the Cathedral-Bascilica. Hewas b
3 CONC uried in the old (now gone) St-Joseph cemetery next to theCathedral-Bas
3 CONC cilica of Quebec."
