1 NOTE Rollo, baptised Robert and so sometimes numbered Robert I to distinguis
2 CONC hhim from his descendants, was a Norwegian or Danish nobleman and thefo
2 CONC under and first ruler of the Viking principality in what soon becamekno
2 CONC wn as Normandy. The name "Rollo" is a Frankish-Latin name probablytaken f
2 CONC rom the Old Norse name Hrólfr, modern Scandinavian name Rolf
2 CONT In 885, Rollo was one of the lesser leaders of the Viking fleet whichbe
2 CONC sieged Paris under Sigfred second official king of the Danes. Legendhas i
2 CONC t that an emissary was sent by the king to find the chieftain andnegoti
2 CONC ate terms. When he asked for this information, the Vikings repliedthat t
2 CONC hey were all chieftains in their own right. In 886, when Sigfredretreat
2 CONC ed in return for tribute, Rollo stayed behind and was eventuallybought o
2 CONC ff and sent to harry Burgundy.
2 CONT Later, he returned to the Seine with his followers (known as Danes, orN
2 CONC orsemen). He invaded the area of northern France now known asNormandy.
2 CONT In 911 Rollo's forces were defeated at the Battle of Chartres by thetro
2 CONC ops of King Charles III of France.In the aftermath of the battle,rather t
2 CONC han pay Rollo to leave, as was customary, Charles the Simpleunderstood t
2 CONC hat he could no longer hold back their onslaught, anddecided to give Ro
2 CONC llo the coastal lands they occupied under thecondition that he defend a
2 CONC gainst other raiding Vikings. In the Treaty ofSaint-Clair-sur-Epte (911
2 CONC ) with King Charles, Rollo pledged feudalallegiance to the king, change
2 CONC d his name to the Frankish version, andconverted to Christianity, proba
2 CONC bly with the baptismal name Robert. Inreturn, King Charles granted Roll
2 CONC o the lower Seine area (today's upperNormandy) and the titular rulershi
2 CONC p of Normandy, centred around the cityof Rouen. There exists some argum
2 CONC ent among historians as to whetherRollo was a "duke" (dux) or whether h
2 CONC is position was equivalent to thatof a "count" under Charlemagne. Accor
2 CONC ding to legend, when required tokiss the foot of King Charles, as a con
2 CONC dition of the treaty, he refusedto perform so great a humiliation, and w
2 CONC hen Charles extended his foot toRollo, Rollo ordered one of his warrior
2 CONC s to do so in his place. Hiswarrior then lifted Charles' foot up to his m
2 CONC outh causing him to fall tothe ground.
