1 NOTE His father's only surviving son, he was crowned emperor by his father i
2 CONC n813 without assistance from the clergy. However, in 816 he was anointe
2 CONC dby the pope, implying that the honour depended upon the pope. In 817 h
2 CONC eissued the "Ordinatio Imperii" which effectively divided the Empirebet
2 CONC ween his three sons. However, this was not the only reason for thedisin
2 CONC tegration of the Carolingian Empire. High offices had becomehereditary a
2 CONC nd so less subject to the Emperor's favour. As well, theVikings began r
2 CONC aiding the Empire more frequently. Just as Charlemagnehad been a Franki
2 CONC sh warrior, Louis saw himself as a servant of theChurch. As a result, w
2 CONC here the papal elections had previously requiredthe imperial approval, t
2 CONC his was no longer the case under the rule ofLouis the Pious. In 817 he b
2 CONC rutally suppressed his nephew, Bernard ofItaly; however, feeling guilty a
2 CONC bout the brutality, in 821 he pardonedthose involved in the uprising, o
2 CONC nly to have this public confessionabout his guilt interpreted by the Fr
2 CONC ankish nobles in 822 as a sign ofweakness. By now he had lost control o
2 CONC ver both church and nobility and,as well, as a widower he had married a
2 CONC gain and a fourth son was born.Now he was also going to be plagued by d
2 CONC ynastic problems. His secondwife, Judith, wanting the largest part of t
2 CONC he empire for her son, joinedforces with Louis's sons, Ludwig the Germa
2 CONC n and Pippin, against Lothar,the eldest son. The results were that two f
2 CONC actions developed in theEmpire, one wanting to keep the Empire united a
2 CONC nd the other to continuethe Frankish custom of dividing lands between a
2 CONC ll sons. In 829 Judithpursuaded Louis the Pious to set aside his settle
2 CONC ment of 817 and includeJudith's son, Charles, in the partition of the E
2 CONC mpire. However, Ludwigthe German and Pippin, jealous of Charles's porti
2 CONC on, joined forces withLothar, their eldest brother and, in 830, rebelle
2 CONC d against their father.The eldest three sons, supported by Pope Gregory I
2 CONC V, defeated theirfather in 833. Lothar was restored as Emperor designat
2 CONC e and Louis thePious was forced to perform a humiliating penance. Howev
2 CONC er, Ludwig theGerman and Pippin were still dissatisfied and again took u
2 CONC p arms. In 838Pippin died followed, in 840, by Louis the Pious; but it t
2 CONC ook until 843when, at Verdun, the Frankish tradition triumphed and the E
2 CONC mpire wasdivided between the three surviving sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Profession : Roi d'Aquitaine en 781, Empereur d'Occident de 814 au 20Ju
2 CONC in 840.
