2 CONC endof the Volga River and the Ural Mountains. Around 889, or perhaps a f
2 CONC ewyears earlier, they were defeated and forced to leave their tradition
2 CONC algrounds. Their shamanist priests decided on a mass emigration; butfir
2 CONC st, to pacify their God, Isten "creator of all created things", asacrif
2 CONC ice was needed, a supreme sacrifice. The princes leading the seventribe
2 CONC s chose Almos, the most able and senior amongst them, to be theChief of a
2 CONC ll Magyars. Then they opened their veins in their arms andeach drank th
2 CONC e mingled blood of all and they took five vows. The firstwas: "As long a
2 CONC s they and their progeny after them shall live, theirduke and ruler sha
2 CONC ll always be taken from the House of Almos. Then DukeAlmos, as the best p
2 CONC ossession they had, was a willing and honouredsacrifice to their god Is
2 CONC ten. The Magyars then set out under their newleader, Duke Arpad, the so
2 CONC n of Almos. After many raiding parties intothe area now known as Hungar
2 CONC y, they settled there, ethnically andlinguistically isolated from their n
2 CONC eighbours. These early Magyars hadbeen seven semi-nomadic tribes who as
2 CONC sociated loosely for offence aswell as defence. Consequently they were w
2 CONC arlike and bent upon conquestand plunder. Their move to Hungary was und
2 CONC er the leadership of Arpad,the first elected chieftain of all the seven t
2 CONC ribes. Being semi-nomadic,they considered the Carpathian Basin as only a b
2 CONC ase for operations. Thepastoral section of these tribes would herd thei
2 CONC r cattle from mountainpastures in summer to milder lowlands in winter. A
2 CONC t the same time themore war-like Magyars would maraud from Constantinop
2 CONC le to the North Sea,and from southern Italy to the Pyrenees, returning w
2 CONC ith booty while atthe same time leaving behind a resentment resulting i
2 CONC n a catastrophicdefeat at Augsburg. This was a turning point in the his
2 CONC tory of Hungaryas from then onwards they not only became more settled b
2 CONC ut began toidentify with their adopted country.
