2 NOTE The Lusatian Sorbs are the residue of the Slavs of the Elbe who oncespr
3 CONC ead across the Oder and Elbe, inhabiting the whole of the presentGerman
3 CONC y. During centuries of combat with the Germans their numbersgradually d
3 CONC ecreased. They are divided into three main groups: theObotrites who inh
3 CONC abited the present Mecklenburg, Lüneburg, and Holsteinwhence they exten
3 CONC ded into the Old Mark; the Lutici or Veltae, who livedbetween the Oder a
3 CONC nd Elbe, the Baltic and the Varna; the Sorbs, wholived on the middle co
3 CONC urse of the Elbe between the Rivers Havel andBober. The Lutici died out o
3 CONC n the Island of Rügen at the beginning ofthe fifteenth century. In the m
3 CONC iddle of the sixteenth century there werestill large numbers of Slavs i
3 CONC n Lüneburg and in the northern part of theOld Mark, while their numbers w
3 CONC ere less in Mecklenburg and inBrandenburg. However, even in Lüneburg th
3 CONC e last Slavs disappearedbetween 1750-60. Only the Lusatian Sorbs who li
3 CONC ved nearer the borders ofBohemia have been able to maintain themselves i
3 CONC n declining numbers untilthe present time. The reason probably is that f
3 CONC or some time theirterritory belonged to Bohemia. At present the Lusatia
3 CONC n Sorbs numbersabout 150,000 persons on the upper course of the Spree. T
3 CONC hey are dividedinto two groups, which differ so decidedly from each oth
3 CONC er in speech andcustoms that some regard them as two peoples; they also h
3 CONC ave twoseparate literatures. They are rapidly becoming Germanized, espe
3 CONC ciallyin Lower Lusatia. The Lusatian Sorbs are Catholics with exception o
3 CONC f15,000 in Upper Lusatia.
