1 NOTE fondateur de l'abbaye de St-Denis
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert, King of the Franks:
2 CONT Grant of an Estate to Monks of St. Denis, 635
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONC ---------
2 CONT A grant of an estate in the seventh century was perhaps the gift ofgrea
2 CONC test value that could be given by one person to another. No land wasgra
2 CONC nted as a rule without the accompanying forms of wealth described byDag
2 CONC obert in this charter. Note that serfs and bondsmen were not excludedin m
2 CONC aking the gift. Twenty-seven estates were given at one time byDagobert t
2 CONC o the Abbey of St. Denis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagobert, King of the Franks, illustrious monarch, to Wandelbert, theDu
2 CONC ke. Whatever we have devoutly granted for the relief of the poor, webel
2 CONC ieve we shall have returned to us with profit in the next life.Therefor
2 CONC e be it known that we have exchanged our villa called Saclas,situated o
2 CONC n the River Juine, in the district of Etampes, and which wehave receive
2 CONC d from Lord Ferreol, Bishop of the diocese of Autun, andfrom Abbot Deod
2 CONC atus, the clergy and church or basilica of Symphorian, inwhose care it i
2 CONC s known to have been, for another villa called Amica,which is in the di
2 CONC strict of Marseilles, to the increase of our fortune.And that same Sacl
2 CONC as we have devoutly granted in its entirety to themonks of St. Denis, t
2 CONC he martyr, at the monastery where his precious bodynow rests, being wit
2 CONC hin their gates. Therefore we have ordered that fromthe present date th
2 CONC ey shall possess the villa of Saclas, with itshouses, serfs, bondsmen, w
2 CONC oods, meadows, pastures, mills, flocks,shepherds, wholly and entirely, j
2 CONC ust as it was formerly held by thechurch of Autun and Symphorian until w
2 CONC e, as has been said, exchanged itfor another. Therefore, because it has b
2 CONC een granted of our bounty, forthe salvation of our soul, to the monks o
2 CONC f St. Denis, according to God'swill, neither the abbot nor any other pe
2 CONC rson shall at any time presumeto destroy this gift to the monks; but le
2 CONC t it be administered in thename of God by the hand of their abbot in wh
2 CONC ose assiduous care the monkslive. And in whatever way the fisc can augm
2 CONC ent its aid to the poor monkslet it do so, so that they and their succe
2 CONC ssors may delight in thestability of our kingdom and pray for the salva
2 CONC tion of our soul. Andthat this charter may endure for all time we have d
2 CONC ecreed that it besigned with our signature. Ursin obtained it. Dagobert g
2 CONC ranted it.
2 CONT Given on July 28th in the fourteenth year of our reign, at Clichy.
2 CONT Amen.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT DAGOBERT I (d. 639), king of the Franks, was the son of Clotaire II. In
2 CONC 623 his father established him as king of the region east of theArdenne
2 CONC s, and in 626 revived for him the ancient kingdom of Austrasia,minus Aq
2 CONC uitaine and Provence. As Dagobert was but yet a child, he wasplaced und
2 CONC er the authority of the mayor of the palace, Pepin, andArnulf, bishop o
2 CONC f Metz. At the death of Clotaire II in 628, Dagobertwished to re-establ
2 CONC ish unity in the Frankish realm, and in 629 and 630make expeditions int
2 CONC o Neustria and Burgundy, where he succeeded on thewhole in securing the r
2 CONC ecognition of his authority. In Aquitaine he gavehis brother Caribert t
2 CONC he administration of the counties of Toulouse,Cahors, Agen, Perogeux an
2 CONC d Saintes; but at Caribert's death in 632Dagobert became sole ruler of t
2 CONC he whole of the Frankish territoriessouth of the Loire. Under him the M
2 CONC erovingian monarchy attained itsculminating point. He restored to the r
2 CONC oyal domain the lands that hadbeen usurped by the great nobles and by t
2 CONC he church; he maintained atParis a luxurious, though, from the example h
2 CONC e himself set, a disorderlycourt; he was a patron of the arts and delig
2 CONC hted in the exquisitecraftsmanship of his treasurer, the goldsmith, St. E
2 CONC loi. His authoritywas recognized through the length and breadth of the r
2 CONC ealm. The duke ofthe Basques came to his court to swear fidelity, and a
2 CONC t his villa atClichy the chief of the Bretons of Domnone promised obedi
2 CONC ence. Heintervened in the affairs of the Visigoths of Spain and the Lom
2 CONC bards ofItaly, and was heard with deference. Indeed, as a sovereign, Da
2 CONC gobertwas reckoned superior to the other barbarian kings. He entered in
2 CONC torelations with the eastern empire, and swore a 'perpetual peace' with
2 CONC the emperor Heraclius; and it is probable that the two sovereigns tookc
2 CONC ommon measures against the Slav and Burgundian tribes which ravaged int
2 CONC urn the Byzantine state and the German territories subject to theFranks
2 CONC . Dagobert protected the church and placed illustrious prelates atthe h
2 CONC ead of the bishoprics---Eloi (Eligius) at Noyon, Ouen (Audoenus) atRoue
2 CONC n and Didier (Desiderius) at Cahors. His reign is also marked by thecre
2 CONC ation of numerous monasteries and by renewed missionary activity inFlan
2 CONC ders and among the Basques. He died on Jan 9, 639, as was buried atSt. D
2 CONC enis. After his death the Frankish monarchy was again divided. In634 he h
2 CONC ad been obliged to give the Austrasians a special king in theperson of h
2 CONC is eldest son Sigebert, and at the birth of a second son,Colvis, in 635
2 CONC , the Neustrians had immediately claimed him as king. Thusthe unificati
2 CONC on of the realm, which Dagobert had re-established with somuch pains, w
2 CONC as anulled.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 622 he became king of Austrasia and in 629 of all the Frankishlands.
2 CONC He secured peace by making a friendship treaty with theByzantineEmperor
2 CONC , Heraclius, by defeating the Gascons and theBretons, thencampaigning a
2 CONC gainst the Slavs on his eastern frontier.In 631 he sent anarmy to Spain t
2 CONC o help the Visigothic usurper, Swinthila. He moved hiscapital from Aust
2 CONC rasia to Paris, a centrallocation from which thekingdom could be govern
2 CONC ed more effectively. He then appeased theAustrasians by making his thre
2 CONC e-year-old son,Sigebert, their king in634. Dagobert loved justice but w
2 CONC as alsogreedy and dissolute. During hisreign there was a revival of the a
2 CONC rts, a revision of the Frankish law,and encouragement for learning. Dag
2 CONC obert founded the first great abbeyof Saint Denis to which he mademany g
2 CONC ifts. His chief advisers were twoAustrasian aristocrats, Arnulf, bishop
2 CONC of Metz, and Pippin, who was mademayor of Dagobert's palace. It was ama
2 CONC rriage arranged between Arnulf'sson and Pippin's daughter that was to f
2 CONC orm the powerful dynasty knownlater as the Carolingians
