1 NOTE King of the Germans and Emperor of Rome, son of Otto I and Adelaide, b.
2 CONC 955; d. in Rome, 7 Dec., 983. In 961 he was elected king at Worms, andw
2 CONC as crowned at Aix, 26 May. Frail in body, he possessed an intrepid anda
2 CONC rbitrary spirit. With him began that extravagant policy of imperialism,
2 CONC which aimed at restoring the world boundaries of the ancients, and toen
2 CONC compass the Ancient Sea (the Mediterranean). Germany and Italy were tow
2 CONC ield the balance of power. Reacting against this imperialistic policywa
2 CONC s the revived strength of particularism. The conflict with the ducalHou
2 CONC se of Bavaria gave a dangerous aspect to affairs. In Bavaria (withOtto'
2 CONC s approval) the duchess dowager Judith acted as regent for her sonHenry
2 CONC . Upon coming of age he was given the Duchy of Bavaria in fee byOtto II
2 CONC , who, at the same time, invested Ludolph's son Otto with Swabiaon the d
2 CONC eath of Duke Burchard, ignoring the latter's widow, Hedwig, adaughter o
2 CONC f Judith. Henry, named the "Quarrelsome", supported by Abrahamof Friesi
2 CONC ng, Boleslaw of Bohemia, and Mesislav of Poland, opposed this.The war f
2 CONC inally ended by Judith being immured in a cloister and Henrydeclared to h
2 CONC ave forfeited his duchy. Ludolph's son Otto received thevacant ducal th
2 CONC rone. The Eastmark was separated from Bavaria and givenin fee to Luitpo
2 CONC ld of Babenberg, who laid the foundation of the futurerenown of his fam
2 CONC ily. In 978 Lothair, who aspired to the acquisition ofWestern Germany, i
2 CONC nvaded Lorraine, and pillaged Aix where Otto narrowlyescaped capture. B
2 CONC ut Lothair did not advance further. In Dortmund a warof reprisal was at o
2 CONC nce decided upon; with 60,000 men, Otto marched uponParis, which he fai
2 CONC led to take. Lothair, however, was obliged to come toterms, and in 980 t
2 CONC he two kings met near Sedan, where Otto obtained anagreement securing t
2 CONC he former boundaries.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rome, Crescentius, a son of Theodora, headed a disorderly factionalg
2 CONC overnment and sought to settle the affairs of the Holy See by coercion.
2 CONC Otto crossed the Alps and freed the papacy. While in Rome his mindbecam
2 CONC e imbued with dreams of ancient imperialism; he would give hisimperiali
2 CONC stic policy a firm foundation by bringing all Italy undersubjection. In S
2 CONC outhern Italy the Byzantines and Saracens united againstthe German pret
2 CONC ensions, and in 982 the war with these ancient powerscommenced. Tarentu
2 CONC m fell into the hands of the German king, but 15 July,982, he was defea
2 CONC ted near Capo Colonne, not far from Cotrone. Thisbattle resulted in the s
2 CONC urrender of Apulia and Calabria and destroyedthe prestige of the imperi
2 CONC al authority throughout Italy. The effectspread to the people of the No
2 CONC rth and the turbulent Slavs on the East,and shortly after the Danes and W
2 CONC ends rose up in arms. But Otto wasvictorious. The Christian mission, un
2 CONC der the leadership of pilgrims ofPassau, had made great progress in the t
2 CONC erritory of the Magyars. Thencame the defeat in Calabria, whereupon all o
2 CONC f Slavonia, particularly theheathen part, revolted against German sover
2 CONC eignty. The promisingbeginnings of German and Christian culture east of t
2 CONC he Elbe, inauguratedby Otto, were destroyed. In Bohemia the ecclesiasti
2 CONC cal organization wasthorougly established, but the emperor was unable t
2 CONC o support the bishopwhom he had placed there. On the Havel and the Spre
2 CONC e Christianity wasalmost annihilated. Affairs were in equally bad condi
2 CONC tion among theWends. The reign of Otto II has been justly called the pe
2 CONC riod ofmartyrdom for the German Church. The missions which had been org
2 CONC anizedby Otto I were, with few exceptions, destroyed. Otto II now renew
2 CONC ed thedespotic policy towards the Saxonian border nobles and incited op
2 CONC endiscontent. In 983 he held an Imperial Diet where his son was elected
2 CONC king as Otto III and where the assembled nobles pledged their support.H
2 CONC e departed with high hopes for Southern Italy. Fortune seemed to favour
2 CONC the imperial leaders, who expected to wipe out the disgrace suffered in
2 CONC the south. He chose a new pope, Peter of Pavia (John XIV). While in Rom
2 CONC ehe was stricken with malaria and was buried in St. Peter's. At the tim
2 CONC eof his death the relations of the empire towards the papacy were still
2 CONC undefined. He had been unable to maintain his political ascendency inRo
2 CONC me. His imperialistic policy had placed the restraints of progressivean
2 CONC d pacific Christianity and Germanization on the borders; and he,pursuin
2 CONC g fanciful dreams, believed that he might dare to transfer thegoal of h
2 CONC is policy to the south.
