1 NOTE Probably daughter of Leo Phokas son of Sophia Phokas by her husbandCons
2 CONC tantin Skleros, brother of Marie (1st wife of the Basileus JohnTsimices
2 CONC )
2 CONT  She was about fifteen years old when she arrived in Italy to be marrie
2 CONC dto the sixteen-year-old Otto, heir to the Holy Roman Emperor and alrea
2 CONC dyKing of the Romans. Their marriage had been arranged as part of apeac
2 CONC e-treaty between the Eastern and Western Empires. Very little hasbeen r
2 CONC ecorded about her, except that she was beautiful and loved by herhusban
2 CONC d. However, although she apparently did not always get on with hermothe
2 CONC r-in-law, Theophano became a competent Empress in due course, attimes a
2 CONC cting in his absence as his representative. Yet they did manageto spend m
2 CONC ost of their lives together, indicating that their marriagehad become m
2 CONC ore than just pre-arranged. This had taken place in Rome on14 April 972 a
2 CONC nd, according to tradition, she was crowned by Pope JohnXIII. In the ma
2 CONC rriage contract, as a dowry she was given extensiveproperties in Italy a
2 CONC s well as in The Netherlands. Suddenly, on 7 May973, her father-in-law d
2 CONC ied and as a result her husband became EmperorOtto II and she his Empre
2 CONC ss. In 977 their first child, a daughter, wasborn followed by another d
2 CONC aughter almost a year later. In 980 a son andheir, Otto, was born follo
2 CONC wed by a third daughter. She accompanied herhusband in his unsuccessful c
2 CONC ampaigns against the Saracens in SouthernItaly. Having ignored his heal
2 CONC th, Otto II died in Theophano's arms on 7December 983 and was buried in R
2 CONC ome's St. Peter's Basilica. Manydifficult and unhappy years were now to c
2 CONC ome for Theophano, deserted ina time of great need by those people once f
2 CONC avoured by her husband.Bishop Diederik of Metz, often favoured by Otto I
2 CONC I, became herimplacable enemy trying to damage her reputation. This he c
2 CONC ontinued todo even after she had died. Her son, Emperor Otto III, was c
2 CONC rowned asKing on 25 May 983 in Aachen as a first step to being acknowle
2 CONC dged asHoly Roman Emperor. Bishop Warin of Cologne, custodian of the li
2 CONC ttleboy, gave him to his great-uncle Heinrich, Duke of Bavaria. However
2 CONC ,this Heinrich had only just been released from prison, where he had be
2 CONC enlocked up because of his opposition to his brother Emperor Otto I and
2 CONC his nephew Emperor Otto II. At first Heinrich pretended that he wantedt
2 CONC o act as Regent for the three-year-old Emperor Otto III, but it soonbec
2 CONC ame obvious that he wanted to become Emperor himself. Next he tookposse
2 CONC ssion of Theophano's eldest daughter, Adelheid, and in Quedlinburgwas a
2 CONC ccepted as King of the Romans by some of Germany's nobles. However,many m
2 CONC ore nobles had sworn to protect the young Emperor and so forcedHeinrich t
2 CONC o promise to return the boy to his mother. Before thekidnapping of her s
2 CONC on, Theophano had made arrangements to return toGermany and take upon h
2 CONC er the Regency which was justly hers. Hermother-in-law, Dowager Empress A
2 CONC delaide, remained in Italy as Regent ofthe Italian kingdom. On 29 June 9
2 CONC 84, Heinrich was forced to appear at acourt at Rohr near Meiningen-im-G
2 CONC rabfeld but refused to hand over theEmperor. In the afternoon of that d
2 CONC ay an unusual event took place;apparently a bright star appeared in the s
2 CONC ky and, impressed by such asupernatural phenomenon, he returned both ch
2 CONC ildren to their mother. As aresult of the Empress Widow Adelaide having i
2 CONC nterceded for Heinrich, hewas left unpunished, though for some time he s
2 CONC till tried to becomeEmperor. This was until 985 when he was forced to a
2 CONC bandon any claims,while at the same time Theophano had forced his suppo
2 CONC rters to accept herrule. Neither Heinrich nor his supporters were punis
2 CONC hed when Theophanoagain refused to take revenge as she, with political i
2 CONC nsight, realisedthat this would only extend the unrest in the Empire. W
2 CONC hile her threedaughters were brought up in convents, two would remain t
2 CONC here to becomeAbbesses. Her son remained at her court where she took gr
2 CONC eat care withhis education and, as a result, Otto III became one of the b
2 CONC est educatedamongst the Holy Roman Emperors. Had he not died at twenty-
2 CONC one, he wouldhave made a much larger impact on history. As Regent, Theo
2 CONC phano wasintelligent and active, thus achieving much more than what oth
2 CONC ers wouldhave done by the sword. There were border skirmishes with Slav t
2 CONC ribes aswell as with the Frankish kings. She obtained more co-operation f
2 CONC rom themagnates than did her father-in-law, Otto The Great. As regent s
2 CONC he wasrequired to travel a great deal and, due to her capacity of negot
2 CONC ation,she ensured a peaceful period for Germany. After a visit to Italy
2 CONC , shereturned to Germany in May 990 and from there to Nijmegen, where s
2 CONC hedied on 15 June 991, probably only thirty-five years of age.
