1 NOTE The eldest son of Emperor Heinrich the Fowler and Matilda von Ringelhei
2 CONC m,his younger years were disturbed by quarrels between his parents. His
2 CONC mother wanted their seven years' younger son, also named Heinrich,ackno
2 CONC wledged as Heinrich the Fowler's heir. However, when Heinrich theFowler d
2 CONC ied in 936, it was still Otto who, at the age of twenty-four,succeeded. A
2 CONC s in his younger years he preferred to be away from hismother, he did s
2 CONC o by accompanying his father who was subduing the tribesin Bavaria and S
2 CONC uabia. This avoidance of his mother was made even easierwhen, at the ag
2 CONC e of seventeen, he married Eadgyth, a daughter of theEnglish king, Edwa
2 CONC rd the Elder. However, his mother's thoroughlyreligious education had l
2 CONC eft its mark as not only would he hardly evermiss mass but also founded s
2 CONC everal convents. As well, he consideredhimself master of the church and n
2 CONC ot it's servant. Otto never learned toread or write, which in later lif
2 CONC e he resented and tried to remedy. Hedid learn a little French but not e
2 CONC nough to speak it and he had noknowledge of Latin at all. However, he w
2 CONC as very intelligent and had botha very good memory and judgement of men
2 CONC . He loved hunting and ridingwild horses yet was reserved, calm and dig
2 CONC nified. On the other hand hehad sudden bursts of temper which he would q
2 CONC uickly forget, though thepeople concerned did not. Consequently, he was m
2 CONC ore respected thanloved. At his coronation in 936 by Hildebert, Archbis
2 CONC hop of Mayence, hemade it clear that he intended to be an absolute rule
2 CONC r. To emphasizethis he made the Dukes of Bavaria, Suabia, Franconia and L
2 CONC orraine waitupon him at the banquet following the coronation. Some of t
2 CONC hese Dukesresented this and revolted. Amongst the rebels were Otto's ha
2 CONC lf-brother,Thankmar, as well as the resentful younger brother, Heinrich
2 CONC . Heinrichwas joined by Giselbert, Duke of Lorraine, and marched toward
2 CONC s theRhine. Although Otto was greatly outnumbered, he was a clever stra
2 CONC tegistand so was victorious. Superstition also played a role in his vic
2 CONC tory ashe carried a "Holy Lance". Further victories were won with this "
2 CONC HolyLance" so that his brother Heinrich fled to the court of King Louis I
2 CONC Vof France. As Otto wanted peace and not revenge, after a period hesoug
2 CONC ht the friendship of the defeated dukes. His brother was forgiven,made D
2 CONC uke of Bavaria, and so became Otto's loyal follower. To pacify theDuke o
2 CONC f Suabia, he arranged the marriage of his son, Liudolf, to Ida,daughter o
2 CONC f Hermann I of Suabia, while the Duke of Lorraine was replacedby Otto's s
2 CONC upporter, Conrad the Red. With peace now within the empire,he wanted to "
2 CONC secure" the borders at the eastern front. His first stepwas the conques
2 CONC t of Bohemia and then inflicting devastation on the Slavsliving beyond t
2 CONC he Elbe and Oder. While establishing himself in the east,trouble was ca
2 CONC used by King Louis IV of France who wanted to regainLorraine. Otto, bel
2 CONC ieving in family ties, was furious with his Frenchbrother-in-law and so m
2 CONC arched into France. The defeated Louis IVwithdrew into France only to b
2 CONC e defeated again by Hugues the Great.However, Hugues the Great was also a b
2 CONC rother-in-law, and, sick of familysquabbles, Otto defeated him as well. H
2 CONC aving secured the westernborders, Otto was next invited to interfere in I
2 CONC taly. The Italian ruler,Lothar, had died and his position was taken by t
2 CONC he cruel andunscrupulous Berengar II of Ivrea. Berengar tried to seize t
2 CONC he dowry ofLothar's widow, the beautiful Aelis (or Adela‹de). Although s
2 CONC he escaped,she was still in danger and so asked for the protection of O
2 CONC tto. Comingwith an army to Lombardy, he forced Berengar to acknowledge h
2 CONC im as theoverlord of Italy, and assumed the title of King of Italy. The
2 CONC n, as hewas a widower, he married Aelis with whom he had fallen in love
2 CONC .Although he hoped to remain in Italy to make sure of its becoming part
2 CONC of the Empire, he was nevertheless forced to return to Germany when his
2 CONC son, Liudolf, revolted out of fear for possible sons Otto might fatherb
2 CONC y Aelis. Liudolf was joined by the Archbishop of Mayence and, to add to
2 CONC the troubles, the Magyars then attacked Bavaria. While reasserting hisa
2 CONC uthority in Germany, the Duke of Lorraine, who acted as hisrepresentati
2 CONC ve in Lombardy, joined Berengar against him. In 961 thedebauched pope J
2 CONC ohn XII appealed to Otto to get rid of Berengar, so Ottoreturned to Ita
2 CONC ly and Berengar was forced to acknowledge Otto'ssupremacy. On 31 Januar
2 CONC y 962 in Rome's St. Peter's, Otto was thencrowned Holy Roman Emperor by P
2 CONC ope John XII. Consequently, Otto was nowsecular master of Europe as wel
2 CONC l as head of the church, as he forcedPope John XII to swear allegiance t
2 CONC o him. By doing so, the pope becameOtto's vassal. When Pope John XII th
2 CONC en made it clear that he regrettedthis action, Otto deposed and replace
2 CONC d him with Pope Leo VIII. Havingsettled his affairs in Italy, he return
2 CONC ed to Germany; but in 965 PopeLeo VIII suddenly died and the clergy wan
2 CONC ted to elect his successorwithout Otto's consent. Consequently, Otto re
2 CONC turned to Italy andappointed the Bishop of Narni as Pope John XIII. How
2 CONC ever, the Romansthen drove John XIII into exile; but in 966, Otto in tu
2 CONC rn forced theRomans to accept John XIII. Otto then left Italy for the l
2 CONC ast time andhis last years saw peace prevail in Europe. He died on 6 Ma
2 CONC y 973 and wasburied in the Cathedral of Magdeburg which he had built.
