1 EVEN
2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE came to Acadia in 1632 with Commander Isaac de Razilly by order ofCardi
3 CONC nal Richelieu (Minister of State to King Louis XIII). They came tore-oc
3 CONC cupy the colony after the St. Germain-en-Laye Treaty of March 29,1632.A
3 CONC ccording to author Andrew Hill Clark re ACADIA: THE GEOGRAPHY OFEARLY N
3 CONC OVA SCOTIA TO 1760 (page 91): "Razilly ... sailed from France onJuly 4, 1
3 CONC 632 in L'Espérance à Dieu, shepherding two transports, anddisembarked s
3 CONC ome three hundred people (mostly men) and a variety oflivestock, seeds, t
3 CONC ools, implements, arms, munitions, and other suppliesat La Have (LaHève
3 CONC , at the mouth of La Have River in present LunenburgCounty) on Septembe
3 CONC r 8." Razilly was a cousin of Richelieu and a royalcouncillor. One of t
3 CONC he leaders of The Company of New France, he wasdesignated Lieutenant-Ge
3 CONC neral of all the parts of New France called"Canada" and the Governor of "
3 CONC Acadia." According to authors Sally Rossand Alphonse Deveau re THE ACAD
3 CONC IANS OF NOVA SCOTIA ~ PAST AND PRESENT(page 16): "In September Isaac de R
3 CONC azilly arrived in Acadia with threesailing vessels, 300 hand-picked men
3 CONC , three Capuchin Fathers and a fewwomen and children."
2 SOUR @S175@
3 _TMPLT
4 FIELD
5 NAME Page
1 EVEN
2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE was at Port Royal and Captain of the Army of Pentagoët as well as theri
3 CONC ght-hand man of the Governor of Acadia (Charles de Menou d'Aulnay deCha
3 CONC rnizay) according to a document dated 14 July 1640. After the deathof t
3 CONC he Governor in 1650, Germain was the Commander at the fort of PortRoyal a
3 CONC nd Deputy Guardian of the Governor's children. On August 16,1654, when 5
3 CONC 00 Bostonian soldiers under the command of Robert Sedgewickattacked the f
3 CONC ort of Port Royal, Germain found it wise to give upwithout a struggle a
3 CONC s he had only 100 men to oppose them. All militarypersonnel were repatr
3 CONC iated back to France.
