1 NOTE On September 28, 1162 he married Ada, sister of king William I ofScotla
2 CONC nd, also known as William the Lion. The county of Holland adoptedfrom h
2 CONC im the rampant lion in the coat of arms and the name of William.
2 CONT Floris III was a loyal vassal to Frederick I Barbarossa. He accompanied
2 CONC the emperor on two expeditions to Italy in 1158 and 1176-1178. Frederic
2 CONC kthanked him by making Floris part of the imperial nobility.
2 CONT The emperor gave Floris the toll right of Geervliet, the most important
2 CONC toll station in Holland at that time. This was actually the legalisatio
2 CONC nof an existing situation, because the counts of Holland had chargedtol
2 CONC ls illegally since the start of the 11th century.
2 CONT Many farmers came to Holland to turn the swamps into agricultural lands
2 CONC .Dikes and dams were build and the border between Holland and thebishop
2 CONC ric of Utrecht had to be determined. There was a dispute betweenFloris a
2 CONC nd the bishop of Utrecht about a new dam in the Rhine atZwammerdam in 1
2 CONC 165, which had to be settled by emperor Frederick. Thebrother of Floris
2 CONC , Baldwin became bishop of Utrecht in 1178.
2 CONT War broke out between Flanders and Holland. Count Philip of Flanderswan
2 CONC ted to have Zeeland. Floris was captured in Brugge and had to acceptFle
2 CONC mish overlordship in Zeeland as ransom in 1167.
2 CONT During his reign Floris III had troubles with West Friesland and a warw
2 CONC ith Philip count of Flanders concerning their respective rights in West
2 CONC Zeeland, in which he was beaten. In 1170 a great flood caused immensede
2 CONC vastation in the north and helped to form the Zuider Zee.
2 CONT In 1189 Floris accompanied Frederick Barbarossa upon the third Crusade,
2 CONC of which he was a distinguished leader. He died in 1190 at Antioch ofpe
2 CONC stilence and was buried there.
