1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Malcolm was born about 1031. He was still a child when his father KingD
2 CONC uncan was murdered by Macbeth in 1040. He spent his youth inNorthumbria w
2 CONC ith his uncle Earl Siward, who in 1054 established him inCumbria and Lo
2 CONC thian.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1057, after Macbeth was killed, Malcolm became king of all Scotland.
2 CONC With his first wife Ingibiorg Finnsdottir av Austraat of Halland, widow
2 CONC of Thorfinn of Orkney, he had several sons of whom Duncan would havepro
2 CONC geny and briefly succeeded him. After the death of Ingibiorg in 1069Mal
2 CONC colm married Margaret of Wessex, sister of Edgar Atheling, whosecause h
2 CONC e adopted. Malcolm and Margaret had six sons and two daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT He attacked Northumbria five times (in 1069, 1070, 1079, 1091 and 1093)
2 CONC and there were counter-invasions by William the Conqueror and PrinceRob
2 CONC ert in 1072 and 1080. In 1092 William's son William II Rufus wrestedfro
2 CONC m Scotland all of Cumbria south of the Solway. In the following yearMal
2 CONC colm marched into England, but he was trapped and killed at Alnwickon 1
2 CONC 3 November 1093. Four of his sons succeeded him: Duncan, Edgar,Alexande
2 CONC r and David.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1054 twenty-three year old Malcolm returned to Scotland,aft
2 CONC er a period in hiding in England from the ruthless King Macbeth. Hehad c
2 CONC ome back to Scotland to reclaim the throne from Macbeth, who hadtaken i
2 CONC t from his father, King Duncan, in 1040.
2 CONT Malcolm and his army defeated Macbeth twice in battle; the second timea
2 CONC t Lumphanan, where Macbeth and his son were both killed, leaving thethr
2 CONC one for anyone with royal connections to take. Malcolm was crownedking o
2 CONC n 25th April 1058.
2 CONT King Malcolm III had only been on the throne of Scotland for a shorttim
2 CONC e when he sent troops to raid, loot and destroy the villages ofNorthumb
2 CONC ria. This act of force was a shock and a blow to England, sinceKing Edw
2 CONC ard the Confessor of England had helped Malcolm gain theScottish throne i
2 CONC n the first place.
2 CONT Malcolm's aim, and life-long personal ambition, was to conquer northern
2 CONC England. His strikes were initially successful and many innocent people
2 CONC lost their money and their homes. In spite of the attacks, however,Engl
2 CONC and retained control in the north.
2 CONT The Coming of the Normans.
2 CONT Only seven years later further events in the north caused the downfallo
2 CONC f the English King. Harold Hardrada, King of Norway attempted to takeov
2 CONC er Northumbria and Malcolm decided to help the Norwegians. This waspart
2 CONC ly because his (first) wife was related to Harold Hardrada, andpartly b
2 CONC ecause he wanted a share of Northumbria.
2 CONT  Edward the Confessor had died at the end of 1065 and Harold Godwinsonh
2 CONC ad taken the throne, to the fury of William, Duke of Normandy, who hadu
2 CONC nderstood from his kinsman, Edward, that he would succeed him. In fact,
2 CONC when Harold Godwinson had been blown off course during a storm in theEn
2 CONC glish Channel in 1064 William had made Harold promise to supportWilliam
2 CONC ’s claim to the throne as the price for his freedom.
2 CONT When the King of England heard about the Norse invasion, he marched his
2 CONC army northwards. The English defeated the Norwegians, but were forced t
2 CONC oreturn to the south immediately because an invasion force led by DukeW
2 CONC illiam had landed.
2 CONT Harold met William at Hastings where in a great battle the King ofEngla
2 CONC nd was fatally wounded. According to the Bayeux Tapestry, Haroldwas kil
2 CONC led either by an arrow piercing his eye or by the sword of aNorman knig
2 CONC ht. William marched to Westminster Abbey where he crownedhimself King o
2 CONC f England.
2 CONT This event is known as the Norman Conquest; it changed the course ofBri
2 CONC tish history. Many Saxons fled north to Scotland after the arrival ofth
2 CONC e Normans. Among these were Edgar Atheling, last of King Alfred’sline, a
2 CONC nd his sister Margaret.
2 CONT In 1070 Malcolm married Margaret, Edgar's sister, in Dunfermline and in
2 CONC the same year he invaded England. This was partly so that he could help
2 CONC his brother-in-law, Edgar Atheling gain control of the English throne,b
2 CONC ut mainly because he wanted to extend his Kingdom. Through Margaret,his s
2 CONC econd wife, he had a slight, but positive claim to the throne ofEngland
2 CONC . (This was because Edgar and Margaret were the grandchildren ofKing Ed
2 CONC mund Ironside of England.)
2 CONT This meant that Malcolm could try to become the King of both Scotlandan
2 CONC d England. When William discovered Malcolm's link to royalty, he knewhe m
2 CONC ust crush any such ideas of Malcolm’s. William marched an army up toSco
2 CONC tland in 1072 and met the king of Scotland at Stirling. EdgarAtheling a
2 CONC bandoned Canmore and fled to Flanders in Belgium. When Malcolmsaw the s
2 CONC ize of William's army, he promised that he would not hurt, orencroach o
2 CONC n, the English or their kingdom. He also made the decision topay homage t
2 CONC o the Monarch of England. This decision was to have fatefulconsequences f
2 CONC or the independence of Scotland in later, even moredangerous times.
2 CONT For about seven years, Malcolm kept to his promise. For no known reason
2 CONC though, in 1079 he once more invaded the north of England. He viciously
2 CONC attacked Northumberland in Scottish style, which meant thatNorthumberla
2 CONC nd was savagely torn apart. In the following year theNormans again atta
2 CONC cked Scotland. They decided to build a castle over theriver Tyne for mo
2 CONC re protection, and to stand watch against furtherScottish attacks. Malc
2 CONC olm was then, of course reminded of his peaceagreement, and pledged to k
2 CONC eep it for another twelve years.
2 CONT Queen Margaret (St. Margaret)
2 CONT Whilst all of this was taking place, Malcolm's wife Margaret put all of
2 CONC her time and effort as wife and Queen of Scotland into religion and int
2 CONC ocreating a more civilised court in Scotland. Intelligent and veryrelig
2 CONC ious, she was determined to modernise Scotland by introducing ideasfrom E
2 CONC ngland and Europe.
2 CONT 1. She brought softness to the harsh kingdom of the north by copyingsom
2 CONC e of the ways of the Normans. She introduced spiced meats and Frenchwin
2 CONC es; lovely tapestries and rich clothes; dancing and singing ofballads.
2 CONT 2. She taught the priests to live simply and without wealth, givingthei
2 CONC r whole lives to Christian belief.
2 CONT 3. She made Sunday into a day of worship.
2 CONT 4. Around the time of 1070 she invited three English Benedictine monksf
2 CONC rom Canterbury to build a monastery at Dunfermline. This was the starto
2 CONC f monasteries in Scotland. The monks brought with them new skills infar
2 CONC ming and building.
2 CONT 5. She built a new chapel in Edinburgh Castle, in Norman style. It isth
2 CONC e oldest standing church in Scotland today. St. Margaret spent manyhour
2 CONC s there in prayer.
2 CONT 6. She gave large sums of money and land to the Church to help the poor
2 CONT 7. Margaret also launched the Queen's Ferry over the Firth of Forth toS
2 CONC t. Andrews.
2 CONT Her life of charity and kindness impressed the rough and ambitious King
2 CONC Malcolm so much that he supported his wife's good works. He allowed his
2 CONC wealth to be donated to charity and on one occasion he fed three hundre
2 CONC dneedy subjects in his royal hall.
2 CONT Before the twelve-year peace agreement that he had made with the Norman
2 CONC shad expired Malcolm Canmore launched another invasion of northernEngla
2 CONC nd, in 1091. He chose a time when William the Conqueror's sonWilliam Ru
2 CONC fus, who had by this time succeeded his father, was not inBritain, but N
2 CONC ormandy.
2 CONT Edgar Atheling returned from Belgium to take part in the invasion. Even
2 CONC although Malcolm Canmore had pledged to help Atheling regain the Englis
2 CONC hthrone, he was much more interested in putting his own family on theth
2 CONC rone. However, the English repulsed the Scottish invasion and for theth
2 CONC ird time Malcolm made a peace agreement. The English built a castle atC
2 CONC arlisle to patrol the border.
2 CONT You would have thought that the invasion attempts would have stoppedthe
2 CONC n, but they didn't as in 1093 Canmore once more attacked England. Buthe w
2 CONC as killed during the raid and his son died also from woundsreceived. Wh
2 CONC en the news reached poor Margaret she was already very sickwith a termi
2 CONC nal illness; she died four days later.
2 CONT Malcolm's life as a King was nothing more than a reckless baron robber.
2 CONC He never did help his brother-in-law succeed to the English throne. Inf
2 CONC act he didn't even have any success for himself. Again and again hewoul
2 CONC d try to invade England, but he never really achieved anything. Theexcu
2 CONC se Canmore gave for continually invading England was to advance hisbrot
2 CONC her in law’s claim. But in-fact Canmore was nothing more than agreedy, v
2 CONC icious tyrant. He cared about nothing apart from doing thingsfor his ow
2 CONC n gain.
2 CONT They were such an unlikely couple; Margaret was loving and caring, shew
2 CONC as often called Saint Margaret. He was dangerous and duplicitous,nothin
2 CONC g like his gentle wife. No matter how much Margaret tried toconvert her h
2 CONC usband from his wrong doings, she never succeeded. Margaretled a succes
2 CONC sful life in the church, while Malcolm tried, brutally, togain control a
2 CONC nd power over his brother-in-law's homeland. Margaret wasinterested in h
2 CONC elping others and she did a lot of work in the church.Malcolm on the ot
2 CONC her hand, was greedy and a tyrant. Two separate lives,in two separate w
2 CONC orlds, brought together by fate.
2 CONT
2 CONT became King of Strathclyde  and Prince of Cumbria in 1034. He succeeded
2 CONC Lulach as King of Scotland on 17th March, 1058 and was crowned on 25thA
2 CONC pril, 1058  at Scone Abbey, Perthshire
