1 NOTE St. Margaret of Scotland
2 CONT Born about 1045, died 16 Nov., 1092, was a daughter of Edward"Outremere
2 CONC ", or "the Exile", by Agatha, kinswoman of Gisela, the wife ofSt. Steph
2 CONC en of Hungary. She was the granddaughter of Edmund Ironside. Aconstant t
2 CONC radition asserts that Margaret's father and his brother Edmundwere sent t
2 CONC o Hungary for safety during the reign of Canute, but norecord of the fa
2 CONC ct has been found in that country. The date ofMargaret's birth cannot b
2 CONC e ascertained with accuracy, but it must havebeen between the years 103
2 CONC 8, when St. Stephen died, and 1057, when herfather returned to England. I
2 CONC t appears that Margaret came with him onthat occasion and, on his death a
2 CONC nd the conquest of England by theNormans, her mother Agatha decided to r
2 CONC eturn to the Continent. A stormhowever drove their ship to Scotland, wh
2 CONC ere Malcolm III received theparty under his protection, subsequently ta
2 CONC king Margaret to wife. Thisevent had been delayed for a while by Margar
2 CONC et's desire to entirereligion, but it took place some time between 1067 a
2 CONC nd 1070.
2 CONT In her position as queen, all Margaret's great influence was thrown int
2 CONC othe cause of religion and piety. A synod was held, and among the speci
2 CONC alreforms instituted the most important were the regulation of the Lent
2 CONC enfast, observance of the Easter communion, and the removal of certaina
2 CONC buses concerning marriage within the prohibited degrees. Her privatelif
2 CONC e was given up to constant prayer and practices of piety. She foundedse
2 CONC veral churches, including the Abbey of Dunfermline, built to enshrinehe
2 CONC r greatest treasure, a relic of the true Cross. Her book of theGospels, r
2 CONC ichly adorned with jewels, which one day dropped into a riverand was ac
2 CONC cording to legend miraculously recovered, is now in theBodleian library a
2 CONC t Oxford. She foretold the day of her death, whichtook place at Edinbur
2 CONC gh on 16 Nov., 1093, her body being buried beforethe high altar at Dunf
2 CONC ermline.
2 CONT In 1250 Margaret was canonized by Innocent IV, and her relics weretrans
2 CONC lated on 19 June, 1259, to a new shrine, the base of which is stillvisi
2 CONC ble beyond the modern east wall of the restored church. At theReformati
2 CONC on her head passed into the possession of Mary Queen of Scots,and later w
2 CONC as secured by the Jesuits at Douai, where it is believed tohave perishe
2 CONC d during the French Revolution. According to George Conn,"De duplici st
2 CONC atu religionis apud Scots" (Rome, 1628), the rest of therelics, togethe
2 CONC r with those of Malcolm, were acquired by Philip II ofSpain, and placed i
2 CONC n two urns in the Escorial. When, however, BishopGillies of Edinburgh a
2 CONC pplied through Pius IX for their restoration toScotland, they could not b
2 CONC e found.
2 CONT The chief authority for Margaret's life is the contemporary biographypr
2 CONC inted in "Acta SS.", II, June, 320. Its authorship has been ascribedto T
2 CONC urgot, the saint's confessor, a monk of Durham and later Archbishopof S
2 CONC t. Andrews, and also to Theodoric, a somewhat obscure monk; but inspite o
2 CONC f much controversy the point remains quite unsettled. The feastof St. M
2 CONC argaret is now observed by the whole Church on 10 June.
2 CONT
2 CONT Acta SS., II, June, 320; CAPGRAVE, Nova Legenda Angliae (London, 1515),
2 CONC 225; WILLIAM OF MALMESBURY, Gesta Regum in P.L., CLXXIX, also in RollsS
2 CONC eries, ed. STUBBS (London, 1887-9); CHALLONER, Britannia Sancta, I(Lond
2 CONC on, 1745), 358; BUTLER, Lives of the Saints, 10 June; STANTON,Menology o
2 CONC f England and Wales (London, 1887), 544; FORBES-LEITH, Life ofSt. Marga
2 CONC ret. . . (London, 1885); MADAN, The Evangelistarium of St.Margaret in A
2 CONC cademy (1887); BELLESHEIM, History of the Catholic Churchin Scotland, t
2 CONC r. Blair, III (Edinburgh, 1890), 241-63.
2 CONT
2 CONT G. ROGER HUDDLESTON
