1 NOTE Gundred, Gundreda, or Gundrada was probably born in Flanders , sister o
2 CONC fGerbod the Fleming, Earl of Chester.
2 CONT Gundred married William de Warenne, 1st Earl of Surrey (d. 20 June1088)
2 CONC , who rebuilt Lewes Castle, making it his chief residence. In 1078he an
2 CONC d Gundred founded a Cluniac Priory at Southover, adjoining Lewes,where b
2 CONC oth were buried.
2 CONT The Countess had died at Castle Acre, Norfolk, one of her husband'sesta
2 CONC tes.
2 CONT In the course of the centuries which followed both tombstonesdisappeare
2 CONC d from the priory but in 1774 William Burrell, Esq., anantiquary, disco
2 CONC vered Gundred's in Isfield Church (seven miles fromLewes), over the rem
2 CONC ains of Edward Shirley, Esq., (d. 1550), whosefather John was Clerk of t
2 CONC he Kitchen to King Henry VII, and had itremoved on October 2, 1775, to S
2 CONC t. John's Church, Southover, the nearestplace to its original site, and p
2 CONC laced inside and at the south-westcorner of the church, where, until 18
2 CONC 47, it could be seen on the floorbetween pews with a very fine inscript
2 CONC ion detailing its origins etc.
2 CONT In 1845, during excavations through the Priory grounds for the SouthCoa
2 CONC st Railway, the lead chests containing the remains of the Earl andhis C
2 CONC ountess were discovered, and deposited temporarily, for the nexttwo yea
2 CONC rs, beneath Gundred's tombstone. In 1847 a Norman Chapel waserected by p
2 CONC ublic subscription, adjoining the present vestry andchancel. Prior to r
2 CONC e-interring the remains in this chapel, both cystswere opened to ascert
2 CONC ain if there were any contents, which was found tobe the case. New cyst
2 CONC s were made and used, and the ancient onespreserved and placed in two r
2 CONC ecessed arches in the southern wall.Gundred's remains in a good state o
2 CONC f preservation although the Earl'shas lost some lead. Across the upper p
2 CONC art of the right arch is the nameGvndrada. Her tombstone is of black ma
2 CONC rble.
