1 NOTE Earl of Surrey
2 CONT Lewes Castle was built by William de Warenne at the end of the eleventh
2 CONC century. After the Norman invasion in 1066 William de Warenne was given
2 CONC land in Sussex, Surrey, and Norfolk. He built three castles but hischie
2 CONC f residence was at Lewes. Lewes Castle was built by William deWarenne s
2 CONC oon after the Norman invasion. Most experts say it wasoriginally made o
2 CONC f wood but others insist it was flint. It was probablyin the early 12th c
2 CONC entury that a sturdy stone castle was erected.
2 CONT Some sources say he died at Pevensey.
2 CONT Ruth Cobb, "'Travellers to the Town," London: The Epworth Press, 1953,p
2 CONC p. 25-26:
2 CONT The commander of this part of Sussex [around Lewes] appointed by Willia
2 CONC mthe Conqueror was one William de Warenne, who had long been high in th
2 CONC eConqueror's favour, having distinguished himself as a warrior in Franc
2 CONC e.A contemporary account says
2 CONT William de Warenne his own squadron led,
2 CONT And well sat the helmet on so knightly a head.
2 CONT ....The land over which William de Warenne was given control consistedo
2 CONC f the Burgh and the entire Rape of Lewes--about a sixteenth of Sussex.H
2 CONC e was also given the land at Castle Acre in Norfolk and at Reigate inSu
2 CONC rrey. Later he was to be created Earl of Surrey as a reward for helptha
2 CONC t he had given in quelling an insurrection of some rebelliousbarons.
2 CONT [on p. 30 Cobb refers to him as "dark-haired".]
2 CONT p. 31: Although it is now a ruin, the castle built by William de Warenn
2 CONC ein the centre [of Lewes]--and from where his descendants were to ruleu
2 CONC ntil the end of the [14th] century--still seems to dominate the place.T
2 CONC he entrance is off the High Street where, in medieval days, a MarketCro
2 CONC ss once stood. Where there was once a drawbridge, there is now apermane
2 CONC nt causeway, and the name 'Castle Ditch' is still in use for thepiece o
2 CONC f moat that protected it on one side. It is now a narrowthoroughfare.
2 CONT The entrance arch built by William de Warenne still stands, but in fron
2 CONC tof it is a magnificent Barbican, one of the finest in England, built i
2 CONC nthe [14th] century by a later de Warenne. To the left, on its highmoun
2 CONC d, stands about half of the old keep with two of the towers thatwere ad
2 CONC ded, still intact, and with windows of a much later date. Fromthe keep, t
2 CONC here is a magnificent view down the valley over which the seaonce flowe
2 CONC d when the castle guarded a port, as well as the heights ofthe downs on e
2 CONC ither side.
2 CONT See notes on his wife Gundrada for Cobb's commentary on his andGundrada
2 CONC 's founding of the Priory at Southover after visiting themonastery at C
2 CONC luny.
2 CONT Cobb, p. 54: In the time of William de Warenne, the Priory at Southover
2 CONC ,which he had built, must have needed large supplies of food--not onlyf
2 CONC or the members of the community, but also for the many travellers whost
2 CONC ayed in the guest house. The low-lying land nearby was not suitablefor t
2 CONC he growing of corn; a grange or farm elsewhere was a necessity, soone w
2 CONC as built on the land that William de Warenne had given to thePriory at S
2 CONC wanborough. The charter says that 'he gave to God and theAbbot of Cluny
2 CONC , five hides and a half of land, and also free fishing inthe waters'. T
2 CONC his must have been in 1080, for by then the building ofthe grange had b
2 CONC een completed. It is possible that some of the greatbeams in the barn w
2 CONC hich are still to be seen at the present farm atSwanborough, may have b
2 CONC een put up by the monks who worked there so longago.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancient Lineage of Magna Carta Baron: William de Warenne
2 CONT William the Conqueror was a third or fourth generation Viking whoseance
2 CONC stor was reportedly from Honefoss in what is now Norway, near theOslofj
2 CONC ord. The de Vermandois connection relates to one of the smallpost-Carol
2 CONC ingian Frankish kingdoms along the northern border ofpresent-day France
2 CONC .  Princesses from the Frankish kingdom married deWarennes on at least t
2 CONC wo occasions. Perhaps this was done to placate thefierce Vikings and to e
2 CONC nlist their help for defensive purposes. Thisstrategy was used by the K
2 CONC ing of France who arranged similar marriagesfor his daughters in 911 wh
2 CONC en he recognized the Duchy of Normandy underRollo aka Rolf or Hrolf, an a
2 CONC ltogether evil king. [1]
2 CONT The House of Warren can be traced to Hugh of Normandy, born 990, latero
2 CONC rdained Bishop of Contances. He married a sister of Gunnora, the wifeof R
2 CONC ichard I, Duke of Normandy. Rodulf, son to Hugh, a benefactor to theAbb
2 CONC ey of La Trinite du Mont, died c1050. He married first Beatrix andsecon
2 CONC dly Emma. Emma became the mother of his son William created Countde War
2 CONC enne of Normandy and later first Earl of Surrey. [2]
2 CONT William de Warenne, son of Rodulf, was born in Normandy and accompanied
2 CONC William the Conqueror to England in 1066. At the Battle of Hastings, he
2 CONC commanded a detachment and was rewarded with estates and manors inNorfo
2 CONC lk becoming the first Earl of Surrey. Before 1070, William deWarenne ma
2 CONC rried Gundred, a lady with disputed parentage since manyscholars disbel
2 CONC ieve she was the daughter of the Conqueror.  Some contendshe was the Du
2 CONC ke's daughter as proven by her tombstone at St. John'sChurch, Southover
2 CONC , Lewes: "Within this Pew stands the tombstone ofGundrad, daughter of W
2 CONC illiam the Conqueror, and wife of  William, theFirst Earl of Warren, wh
2 CONC ich having been deposited over her remains inthe Chapter-House of Lewes P
2 CONC riory and lately discovered in IffieldChurch, was removed to this place a
2 CONC t the expense of William Burrell Esq.in 1775 A.D. [3]
2 CONT Gundred died in childbirth at Castle Acre May 27, 1085, and was buriedi
2 CONC n the Priory of Lewes in County Sussex. Four of her children matured:Wi
2 CONC lliam Son & Heir, Reginald, Gundred, and Edith. William, now Earl ofSur
2 CONC rey, rebuilt, enlarged, and strengthened Lewes Castle which is nowused a
2 CONC s the museum of Sussex Archaeological Society. William de Warennefounde
2 CONC d Cluniac Priory in 1078, now a ruin, and endowed the chapterhouse of t
2 CONC he Priory. He married secondly a daughter of William, sisterof Richard G
2 CONC uet of Montmirail, and died June 24, 1088, from woundsreceived at the s
2 CONC iege of Pevensey. He is buried near Ely Cathedral inEast Anglia. [4]
