1 NOTE Born in 849 in the royal manor of Wantage in Berkshire. Apparently hism
2 CONC other encouraged their learning by showing Alfred and his brothers abea
2 CONC utifully illuminated book of poetry saying the one who could read itfir
2 CONC st could have it. Alfred, the youngest, was first. As a young man hewas h
2 CONC is brother King Ethelred's supporter in the struggle against themaraudi
2 CONC ng Vikings. In 868 he married Ealhswith by whom he had a largefamily. W
2 CONC hen in 871 he succeeded his brother Ethelwulf as king, most ofEngland w
2 CONC as in Danish hands. Even though they won several smallvictories and, in 8
2 CONC 75, a seabattle, the Danes had the upper hand. Alfredhad to take refuge i
2 CONC n 877 and, during this period of not being seen,stories emerged of his b
2 CONC eing disguised as a wandering minstrel andstrolling into Danish camps t
2 CONC o gather information. There was also thestory of his burning the cakes h
2 CONC e had been asked to watch. In May 878,joined by English forces, he drov
2 CONC e back the Danes to Chippenham. Thiswas the turning point from which Al
2 CONC fred again emerged as King ofEngland. Twice a year he called a great co
2 CONC uncil which was the beginningof the English Parliament. Apparently Alfr
2 CONC ed suffered from a strange,regularly re-occurring illness as recorded b
2 CONC y Asser, Bishop ofSherborne. According to Sir Iain Moncreiffe of That I
2 CONC lk, it might havebeen porphyria. Alfred died 26 October 899 and was bur
2 CONC ied at Winchester.His wife survived till 5 December 902.
2 CONT
2 CONT Profession : Roi de Wessex de 871 à 878, Roi des Anglo-Saxons de 878 à8
2 CONC 99.
