1 NOTE In 800 at the decease of King Brithric, Egbert was called by the voice o
2 CONC fhis countrymen to assume the Government of Wessex, and he subsequently
2 CONC succeeded in reducing all the Kingdoms of the Heptarchy under his sway.
2 CONC His reign, a long and glorious one, is memorable for the great victorie
2 CONC she achieved over the Danes. See Europaisch Stammtafeln Bund II tafel 5
2 CONC 8.
2 CONT
2 CONT became 'Subregulus" of Kent in 790/96, and succeeded Beorthric as king o
2 CONC fWessex in 802. There is no record of his coronation. From 825 onwards,
2 CONC he had established his supremacy over all other rulers in England, andw
2 CONC as effective overlord of all the south-eastern kingdoms. In 829, hesucc
2 CONC eeded Wiglaf as King of Mercia, although he was expelled thefollowing y
2 CONC ear.
2 CONT Expelled from England, he sought refuge at the Frankish Court where hem
2 CONC et and married his wife, Raedburh or Redburga, who was perhaps a nieceo
2 CONC f Charlemagne. Returned to England, he peacefully became King of Wessex
2 CONC in 802 after the death of King Beorhtric. Ostensibly they lived in peac
2 CONC eas for many years nothing was recorded until 825 when he defeatedBeorn
2 CONC wulf, King of Mercia. Egbert's son Aethelwulf drove King Baldred ofKent o
2 CONC ut of his kingdom of Kent and, by 829, Egbert was regarded as kingof al
2 CONC l England. Still peace did not prevail as the invading Danes werethen h
2 CONC elped by the Cornish, only to be expelled by Egbert who wasprobably in h
2 CONC is sixties when he died in 839.
