1 _AMTID 330126063673:1030:114017017
1 CHAN
2 DATE 2 JAN 2010
1 NOTE * REMARKS:
2 CONT       1st King of Scots (or King of Alba)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT       Kenneth was born about 800, the son of Alpin, king of Kintyre.Ken
2 CONC neth was king of the Picts and, according to national myth, firstking o
2 CONC f Scots, earning him the posthumous epithet 'An Ferbasach' ('TheConquer
2 CONC or'). His undisputed legacy was to produce a dynasty of rulerswho claim
2 CONC ed descent from him. Even though he cannot be regarded as thefather of S
2 CONC cotland, he was the founder of the dynasty which ruled thatcountry for m
2 CONC uch of the medieval period.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth's origins are uncertain, as are his ties, if any, toprevi
2 CONC ous kings of the Picts of Dál Riata (the Gaelic over-kingdom on thewest
2 CONC ern seaboard of Scotland with some territory on the northern coastsof I
2 CONC reland). Medieval genealogies are unreliable sources, but somehistorian
2 CONC s accept Kenneth's descent from the Cenél nGabrain of DálRiata.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Although later traditions provided details of his reign and death
2 CONC ,Kenneth's father Alpin is not listed as among the kings in the _DuanAl
2 CONC banach_ (Song of the Scots), the Middle Gaelic poem found with the_Lebo
2 CONC r Bretnach,_ a Gaelic version of the _Historia Brittonum_attributed to N
2 CONC ennius. The idea that Kenneth was a Gael is not entirelyrejected, but m
2 CONC odern historiography distinguishes between Kenneth as aGael by culture, a
2 CONC nd perhaps in ancestry, and Kenneth as a king ofGaelic Dál Riata. Kenne
2 CONC th could well have been the first sort of Gael.Kings of the Picts befor
2 CONC e him, from Bridei, son of Der-Ilei, his brotherNechtan as well as Oeng
2 CONC us I (Angus I), son of Fergus and his presumeddescendants, were all at l
2 CONC east partly Gallicised. The idea that theGaelic names of Pictish kings i
2 CONC n Irish annals represented translationsof Pictish ones was challenged b
2 CONC y the discovery of the inscription_Custantin filius Fircus(sa),_ the la
2 CONC tinised name of the Pictish kingCaustantin, son of Fergus, on the Duppl
2 CONC in Cross.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth's rise to power can be placed in the context of the recen
2 CONC tend of the previous dynasty, which had dominated Fortriu (an ancientPi
2 CONC ctish kingdom, often used synonymously with Pictland in general) fortwo o
2 CONC r four generations. This followed the death of King Uen, son ofOengus o
2 CONC f Fortriu, his brother Bran, Aed mac Boanta and many others inbattle ag
2 CONC ainst the Vikings in 839. The resulting succession crisisseems, if the P
2 CONC ictish Chronicle's king-lists have any validity, to haveresulted in at l
2 CONC east four would-be kings warring for supreme power.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth's reign is dated from 843, but it was probably not until8
2 CONC 48 that he defeated the last of his rivals for power. The _PictishChron
2 CONC icle_ claims that he was king in Dál Riata for two years beforebecoming P
2 CONC ictish king in 843, but this is not generally accepted. In849, Kenneth h
2 CONC ad relics of St. Columba, which may have included theMonymusk Reliquary
2 CONC , transferred from Iona to Dunkeld. Other than theseare bare facts, the _
2 CONC Chronicle of the Kings of Alba_ reports that heinvaded Saxonia six time
2 CONC s, captured Melrose and burnt Dunbar, and alsothat Vikings laid waste t
2 CONC o Pictland, reaching far into the interior.
2 CONT
2 CONT       The reign of Kenneth also saw an increased degree of Norsesettlem
2 CONC ent in the outlying areas of modern Scotland. The Shetlands, theOrkneys
2 CONC , Caithness, Sutherland, the Western Isles and the Isle of Man,and part o
2 CONC f Ross were settled; the links between Kenneth's kingdom andIreland wer
2 CONC e weakened, those with southern England and the continentalmost broken. I
2 CONC n the face of this, Kenneth and his successors wereforced to consolidat
2 CONC e their position in their kingdom, and the unionbetween the Picts and t
2 CONC he Gaels, already progressing for severalcenturies, began to strengthen
2 CONC . By the time of Donald II, the kingswould be called neither of the Gae
2 CONC ls nor of the Scots, but of Alba.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth died from a tumour on 13 February 858 at the palace ofCin
2 CONC nbelachoir, perhaps near Scone. The annals report the death as thatof t
2 CONC he 'king of the Picts', not the 'king of Alba'. The title 'king ofAlba' i
2 CONC s not used until the time of Kenneth's grandsons Donald II(Domnall mac C
2 CONC ausatin) and Constantine II (Constantin mac Aeda). TheFragmentary Annal
2 CONC s of Ireland quote a verse lamenting Kenneth's death:Because Cináerd wi
2 CONC th many troops lives no longer there is weeping inevery house; there is n
2 CONC o king of his worth under heaven as far as theborders of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Kenneth left at least two sons, Constantine and Aed, who werelate
2 CONC r kings, and at least two daughters. One daughter married Run, kingof S
2 CONC trathclyde, and Eochaid mac Run, king of the Picts, resulted fromthis m
2 CONC arriage. Kenneth's daughter Máerl Muire married two importantIrish king
2 CONC s. Her first husband was Aed Finliath of the Cenél nEógain.Niall Glúndu
2 CONC b, ancestor of the O'Neill, was the son of this marriage.Her second hus
2 CONC band was Flann Sinna of Clann Cholmáin.
2 CONT
2 CONT FROM: P L Kessler <plk@globalnet.co.uk>, ...The History Files, HomePubl
2 CONC ishing A(c)1999, www.users.globalnet.co.uk\\_plk\\history.htm,Internet. 8
2 CONC 43 AD Kenneth rules from Scone (Fortriu, modern Forteviot),capital of t
2 CONC he Southern Picts. 850 AD Kenneth rules Pictland andunites most of the c
2 CONC ountry, a feat which is extended to cover allScotland by subsequent kin
2 CONC gs.
2 CONT
2 CONT succeeded his father as King of Galloway and other parts of Scotland on
2 CONC 20 July or in August 834, and became King of the area  known as Dalriad
2 CONC ain 841. In 843/4, he became King of the Picts, thus uniting the oldGae
2 CONC lic kingdoms of Alba for the first time, and by 846 he was firmlyestabl
2 CONC ished as King of Scotland.
