1 NOTE Fredegund d. 597, Paris French FREDEGONDE, queen consort of ChilpericI, t
2 CONC he Merovingian Frankish king of Soissons. Originally a servant,Fredegun
2 CONC d became Chilperic's mistress after he had murdered his wife andqueen, G
2 CONC alswintha (c. 568). Galswintha, however, was also the sister ofBrunhild
2 CONC , the wife of Chilperic's half-brother Sigebert I, king of theeastern k
2 CONC ingdom of Austrasia. Galswintha's murder engendered a violentanimosity b
2 CONC etween Fredegund and Brunhild and an irreconcilable feud ofmore than 40 y
2 CONC ears' duration between the respective families. Fredegundwas certainly r
2 CONC esponsible for the assassination of Sigebert in 575 andmade attempts on t
2 CONC he lives of Guntram (her brother-in-law and the kingof Burgundy), Child
2 CONC ebert II (Sigebert's son), and Brunhild. After themysterious assassinat
2 CONC ion of Chilperic (584), Fredegund seized his richesand took refuge in t
2 CONC he cathedral at Paris. Both she and her survivingson, Chlotar II, were a
2 CONC t first protected by Guntram, but, when he diedin 592, Childebert II, w
2 CONC ho had taken over his throne, attacked Chlotar,albeit unsuccessfully. F
2 CONC rom Childebert's death (595) until her own,Fredegund intrigued on Chlot
2 CONC ar's behalf against Brunhild, who sought torule through Childebert's so
2 CONC ns, Theodebert II of Austrasia and TheodoricII of Burgundy. Ruthlessly m
2 CONC urderous and sadistically cruel, Fredegundcan have few rivals in monstr
2 CONC ousness.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the most bloodthirsty women in history, she had been the maid of
2 CONC Chilperic I's second wife, Galsvintha, whom he had murdered;  Fredegund
2 CONC ethen became his queen consort. However, Chilperic I's half-brother,Sig
2 CONC ebert I, King of the eastern kingdom of Austrasia, was married toBrunhi
2 CONC ld, the latter being a sister of Galsvintha. The murder ofGalsvintha ca
2 CONC used a violent feud between Fredegund and Brunhild, andalso between the t
2 CONC wo branches of the family, which lasted some fortyyears. Fredegund was c
2 CONC ertainly responsible for the murder of Sigebert in575 and made attempts o
2 CONC n the lives of Guntram, her brother-in-law andking of Burgundy, and on C
2 CONC hildebert II, Sigebert I's son, and Brunhild.In 584 her husband was mys
2 CONC teriously murdered. Fredegund thenseized his riches and took refuge in t
2 CONC he cathedral in Paris.Guntram, her brother-in-law, protected both her a
2 CONC nd her surviving son,Chlotar II, until he died in 592. Childebert II, w
2 CONC ho had succeededGuntram, unsuccesfully attacked Chlotar II. However, Ch
2 CONC ildebert II diedin 595, and, in the last two years of her life, Fredegu
2 CONC nd intrigued onChlotar II's behalf against Brunhild who sought to rule t
2 CONC hroughChildebert II's sons, Theodebert II of Austrasia and Theodoric II o
2 CONC fBurgundy. Ruthless, murderous and sadistically cruel, Fredegund must b
2 CONC eregarded as one of the most monstrous in history.
