1 NOTE By the middle of the sixth century the Merovingians had become by far t
2 CONC hemost powerful of the barbarian heirs to the Roman Empire. Almost allG
2 CONC aul was under their direct rule; they had a foothold in Italy andoverlo
2 CONC rdship over the Thuringians, Alamans, and Bavarians in Germany;and the s
2 CONC uzerainty they claimed over south-east England may have beenmore of a r
2 CONC eality than most English historians have thought. The dyingwords of Chl
2 CONC otar I were quite understandable: "Wa! What kind of king isit in heaven
2 CONC , who kills off kings as great as me?"
2 CONT
2 CONT Profession : Roi de Soissons de 511 à 561.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clotaire I klotar , d. 561, Frankish king, son of Clovis I. On hisfathe
2 CONC r's death (511) he and his brothers received equal shares of theFrankis
2 CONC h kingdom. His capital was at Soissons. In 524 he and his brotherChilde
2 CONC bert I divided the kingdom of their deceased brother Clodomir,whose chi
2 CONC ldren they murdered. With his brother Theodoric he conqueredThuringia. I
2 CONC n 534 Clotaire and Childebert seized and divided the FirstKingdom of Bu
2 CONC rgundy, and in 542 they attacked the Visigoths of Spain butwere repulse
2 CONC d before Zaragoza. The deaths of Theodebald, Theodoric'sgrandson (555), a
2 CONC nd of Childebert (558) made Clotaire sole king of theFranks. His sons C
2 CONC hilperic I and Sigebert I inherited Neustria andAustrasia respectively; h
2 CONC is sons Charibert and Guntram divided theremainder of the kingdom.
