1 NOTE Usually regarded as the real founder of Frankish power in Gaul, he unit
2 CONC edthe Romans of north Gaul under his rule by force of arms and theexped
2 CONC ience of converting to their own religion, Catholic Christianity.He the
2 CONC n united the Franks under his own rule by having all rival kingsassassi
2 CONC nated. With success he led his armies against other Germans; heconquere
2 CONC d the Thuringians to the east, and also the Alamans who weremoving from t
2 CONC heir homes in south-west Germany into what is now Alsaceand northern  S
2 CONC witzerland. In 507 Clovis led his followers south acrossthe Loire to de
2 CONC stroy the Visigothic kingdom of Alaric II. When he diedin 511, the king
2 CONC dom was ruled jointly by his four sons; it was they whodestroyed the Bu
2 CONC rgundian kingdom and who, by offering military aid tothe Ostrogoths in e
2 CONC xchange, annexed Provence to their kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clovis (CHLODWIG, or CHLODOWECH) Son of Childeric, King of the SalicFra
2 CONC nks; born in the year 466; died at Paris, 27 November, 511. Hesucceeded h
2 CONC is father as the King of the Franks of Tournai in 481. Hiskingdom was p
2 CONC robably one of the States that sprang from the division ofClodion's mon
2 CONC archy like those of Cambrai, Tongres and Cologne. Althougha Pagan, Chil
2 CONC deric had kept up friendly relations with the bishops ofGaul, and when C
2 CONC lovis ascended the throne he received a most cordialletter of congratul
2 CONC ation from St. Remigius, Archbishop of Reims. Theyoung king early began h
2 CONC is course of conquest by attacking Syagrius, sonof Aegidius, the Roman C
2 CONC ount. Having established himself at Soissons, heacquired sovereign auth
2 CONC ority over so great a part of Northern Gaul as tobe known to his contem
2 CONC poraries as the King of Soissons. Syagrius, beingdefeated, fled for pro
2 CONC tection to Alaric II, King of the Visigoths, butthe latter, alarmed by a s
2 CONC ummons from Clovis, delivered Syagrius to hisconqueror, who had him dec
2 CONC apitated in 486. Clovis then remained masterof the dominions of Syagriu
2 CONC s and took up his residence at Soissons. Itwould seem as if the episode o
2 CONC f the celebrated vase of Soissons were anincident of the campaign again
2 CONC st Syagrius, and it proves that, althougha pagan, Clovis continued his f
2 CONC ather's policy by remaining on amicableterms with Gaulish episcopate. T
2 CONC he vase, taken by the Frankish soldierswhile plundering a church, forme
2 CONC d part of the booty that was to bedivided among the army. It was claime
2 CONC d by the bishop (St. Remigius?),and the king sought to have it awarded t
2 CONC o himself in order to return itintact to the bishop, but a dissatisfied s
2 CONC oldier split the vase with hisbattle-axe, saying to this king: 'You wil
2 CONC l get only the share allottedyou by fate'. Clovis did not openly resent t
2 CONC he insult, but the followingyear, when reviewing his army he came upon t
2 CONC his same soldier and,reproving him for the the defective condition of h
2 CONC is arms, he split hisskull with an axe, saying: 'It was thus that you t
2 CONC reated the Soissonsvase.' This incident has often been cited to show th
2 CONC at although in timeof war a king has unlimited authority over his army, a
2 CONC fter the war hispower is restricted and that in the division of booty t
2 CONC he rights of thesoldiers must be respected. After the defeat of Syagriu
2 CONC s, Clovisextended his dominion as far as the Loire. It was owing to the
2 CONC assistance given him by the Gaulish episcopate that he gained possessio
2 CONC nof the country. The bishops, it is quite certain mapped out the regime
2 CONC that afterwards prevailed. Unlike that adopted in other barbariankingdo
2 CONC ms founded upon the ruins of the Roman Empire, this regimeestablished a
2 CONC bsolute equality between the Gallo-Roman natives and theirGermanic conq
2 CONC uerors all sharing the same privileges. Procopius, aByzantine writer ha
2 CONC s given us an idea of this agreement, but we know itbest by its results
2 CONC . There was no distribution of Gaulish territory bythe victors; establi
2 CONC shed in the Belgian provinces, they had lands thereto which they return
2 CONC ed after each campaign. All the free men in thekingdom of Clovis, wheth
2 CONC er they were of Roman or of Germanic origin,called themselves Franks, a
2 CONC nd we must guard against the old mistake oflooking upon the Franks afte
2 CONC r Clovis as no more than Germanicbarbarians. Master of half of Gaul, Cl
2 CONC ovis returned to Belgium andconquered the two Salic kingdoms of Cambrai a
2 CONC nd Tongres (?), where hiscousins Ragnacaire and Chararic reigned. These e
2 CONC vents have been madeknown to us only through the poetic tradition of th
2 CONC e Franks which hassingularly distorted them. According to this traditio
2 CONC n Clovis calledupon Chararic to assist him its his war against Syagrius
2 CONC , but Chararic'sattitude throughout the battle was most suspicious, as h
2 CONC e refrained fromtaking sides until he saw which of the rivals was to be v
2 CONC ictorious.Clovis longed to have revenge. Through a ruse he obtained pos
2 CONC session ofChararic and his son and threw them into prison; he then had t
2 CONC heir headsshaved, and both were ordained, the father to the priesthood a
2 CONC nd the sonto the diaconate. When Chararic bemoaned and wept over this h
2 CONC umiliationhis son exclaimed: 'The leaves of a green tree have been cut b
2 CONC ut theywill quickly bud forth again; may he who has done this perish as
2 CONC quickly!' This remark was reported to Clovis, and he had both father an
2 CONC dson beheaded. Tradition goes on to say that Ragnacaire King of Cambrai
2 CONC ,was a man of such loose morals he hardly respected his own kindred, an
2 CONC dFarron, his favourite, was equally licentious. So great was the king's
2 CONC infatuation for this man that, if given a present, he would accept itfo
2 CONC r himself and his Farron. This filled his subjects with indignationand C
2 CONC lovis, to win them over to his side before taking the field,distributed a
2 CONC mong them money, bracelets, and baldries, all in gildedcopper in fraudu
2 CONC lent imitation of genuine gold. On different occasionsRagnacaire sent o
2 CONC ut spies to ascertain the strength of Clovis's army,and upon returning t
2 CONC hey said: 'It is a great reinforcement for you andyour Farron.' Meanwhi
2 CONC le Clovis advanced and the battle began. Beingdefeated, Ragnacaire soug
2 CONC ht refuge in flight, but was overtaken; madeprisoner, and brought to Cl
2 CONC ovis, his hands bound behind him. 'Why', saidhis conqueror have you per
2 CONC mitted our blood to be humiliated by allowingyourself to be put in chai
2 CONC ns? It were better that you should die.' And,so saying, Clovis dealt hi
2 CONC m his death-blow. Then, turning to Richaire,Ragnacaire's brother, who h
2 CONC ad been taken prisoner with the king, hesaid: 'Had you but helped your b
2 CONC rother, they would not have bound him',and he slew Richaire also. After t
2 CONC hese deaths the traitors discoveredthat they had been given counterfeit g
2 CONC old and complained of it toClovis, but he only laughed at them. Rignomi
2 CONC r, one of Ragnacaire'sbrothers, was put to death at Le Mans by order of C
2 CONC lovis, who tookpossession of the kingdom and the treasure of his victim
2 CONC s. Such is thelegend of Clovis; it abounds in all kinds of improbabilit
2 CONC ies, whichcannot be considered as true history. The only facts that can b
2 CONC eaccepted are that Clovis made war upon Kings Ragnacaire and Chararic,p
2 CONC ut them to death and seized their territories. Moreover, the author oft
2 CONC his article is of opinion that these events occurred shortly after thec
2 CONC onquest of the territory of Syagrius, and not after the war against the
2 CONC Visigoths, as has been maintained by Gregory of Tours, whose onlyauthor
2 CONC ity is an oral tradition, and whose chronology in this matter isdecided
2 CONC ly misleading. Besides Gregory of Tours has not given us the nameof Cha
2 CONC raric's kingdom; it was long believed to have been established atTherou
2 CONC anne but it is more probable that Tongres was its capital city,since it w
2 CONC as here that the Franks settled on gaining a foothold inBelgium. In 492 o
2 CONC r 493 Clovis, who was master of Gaul from the Loire tothe frontiers of t
2 CONC he Rhenish Kingdom of Cologne, married Clotilda, theniece of Gondebad, K
2 CONC ing of the Burgundians. The popular epic of theFranks has transformed t
2 CONC he story of this marriage into a veritablenuptial poem the analysis of w
2 CONC hich will be found in the article onClotilda. Clotilda, who was a Catho
2 CONC lic, and very pious, won the consentof Clovis to the baptism of their s
2 CONC on, and then urged that he himselfembrace the Catholic Faith. He delibe
2 CONC rated for a long time. Finally,during a battle against the Alemanni--wh
2 CONC ich without apparent reason hasbeen called the battle of Tolbiac (Zulpi
2 CONC ch)--seeing his troops on thepoint of yielding, he invoked the aid of C
2 CONC lotilda's God, promised tobecome a Christian if only victory should be g
2 CONC ranted him. He conqueredand, true to his word was baptized at Reims by S
2 CONC t. Remigius, bishop ofthat city, his sister Albofledis and three thousa
2 CONC nd of his warriors atthe same time embracing Christianity. Gregory of T
2 CONC ours, in hisecclesiastical history of the Franks has described this eve
2 CONC nt, whichtook place amid great pomp at Christmas, 496. 'Bow thy head, O
2 CONC Sicambrian', said St. Remigius to the royal convert 'Adore what thouhas
2 CONC t burned and burn what thou hast adored.' According to aninth-century l
2 CONC egend found in the life of St. Remigius, written by thecelebrated Hinem
2 CONC ar himself Archbishop of Reims, the chrism for thebaptismal ceremony wa
2 CONC s missing and was brought from heaven in a vase(ampulla) borne by a dov
2 CONC e. This is what is known as the Sainte Ampouleof Reims, preserved in th
2 CONC e treasury of the cathedral of that city andused for the coronation of t
2 CONC he kings of France from Philip Augustus downto Charles X. The conversio
2 CONC n of Clovis to the religion of the majorityof his subjects soon brought a
2 CONC bout the union of the Gallo-Romans withtheir barbarian conquerors. Whil
2 CONC e in all the other Germanic kingdomsfounded on the ruins of the Roman E
2 CONC mpire the difference of religionbetween the Catholic natives and Arian c
2 CONC onquerers was a very activecause of destruction, in the Frankish kingdo
2 CONC m, on the contrary, thefundamental identity of religious beliefs and eq
2 CONC uality of politicalrights made national and patriotic sentiments univer
2 CONC sal and produced themost perfect harmony between the two races. The Fra
2 CONC nkish Kingdom wasthenceforth the representative and defender of Catholi
2 CONC c intereststhroughout the West, while to his conversion Clovis owed ane
2 CONC xceptionally brilliant position. Those historians who do not understand
2 CONC the problems of religious psychology have concluded that Clovis embrace
2 CONC dChristianity solely from political motives, but nothing is moreerroneo
2 CONC us. On the contrary, everything goes to prove that his conversionwas si
2 CONC ncere, and the opposite cannot be maintained without refusingcredence t
2 CONC o the most trustworthy evidence. In the year 500 Clovis wascalled upon t
2 CONC o mediate in a quarrel between his wife's two uncles, KingsGondebad of V
2 CONC ienne and Godegisil of Geneva. He took sides with thelatter, whom he he
2 CONC lped to defeat Gondebad at Dijon, and then, deeming itprudent to interf
2 CONC ere no further in this fratricidal struggle, hereturned home, leaving G
2 CONC odegisil an auxiliary corps of five thousandFranks. After Clovis's depa
2 CONC rture Gondebad reconquered Vienne, hiscapital in which Godegisil had es
2 CONC tablished himself. This reconquest waseffected by a stratagem seconded b
2 CONC y treachery, and Godegisil himselfperished on the same occasion. The po
2 CONC pular poetry of the Franks hassingularly misrepresented this interventi
2 CONC on of Clovis, pretending that,at the instigation of his wife Clotilda, h
2 CONC e sought to avenge hergrievances against her uncle Gondebad (see CLOTIL
2 CONC DA) and that the latterking, besieged in Avignon by Clovis, got rid of h
2 CONC is opponent through theagency of Aredius, a faithful follower. But in t
2 CONC hese poems there are somany fictions as to render the history in them i
2 CONC ndistinguishable. Anexpedition, otherwise important and profitable was u
2 CONC ndertaken by Clovisin the year 506 against Alaric II, King of the Visig
2 CONC oths of Aquitaine.He was awaited as their deliverer by the Catholics of t
2 CONC hat kingdom, whowere being cruelly persecuted by Arian fanatics, and wa
2 CONC s encouraged inhis enterprise by the Emperor Anastasius, who wished to c
2 CONC rush this allyof Theodoric, King of the Ostrogoths. Despite the diploma
2 CONC tic effortsmade by the latter to prevent the war, Clovis crossed the Lo
2 CONC ire andproceeded to Vouille, near Poitiers, where he defeated and slew A
2 CONC laric,whose demoralized troops fled in disorder. The Franks took posses
2 CONC sion ofthe Visigoth Kingdom as far as the Pyrenees and the Rhone, but t
2 CONC he partsituated on the left bank of this river was stoutly defended by t
2 CONC hearmies of Theodoric, and thus the Franks were prevented from seizingA
2 CONC rles and Provence. Notwithstanding this last failure, Clovis, by hiscon
2 CONC quest of Aquitaine, added to the Frankish crown the fairest of itsjewel
2 CONC s. So greatly did the Emperor Anastasius rejoice over the successattain
2 CONC ed by Clovis that, to testify his satisfaction, he sent theFrankish con
2 CONC queror the insignia of the consular dignity, an honouralways highly app
2 CONC reciated by the barbarians. The annexation of theRhenish Kingdom of Col
2 CONC ogne crowned the acquisition of Gaul by Clovis.But the history of this c
2 CONC onquest, also, has been disfigured by a legendthat Clovis instigated Ch
2 CONC loderic, son of Sigebert of Cologne, toassassinate his father, then, af
2 CONC ter the perpetration of this foul deed,caused Chloderic himself to be a
2 CONC ssassinated, and finally offered himselfto the Rhenish Franks as king, p
2 CONC rotesting his innocence of the crimesthat had been committed. The only h
2 CONC istorical element in this old story,preserved by Gregory of Tours, is t
2 CONC hat the two kings of Cologne met withviolent deaths, and that that Clov
2 CONC is, their relative, succeeded thempartly by right of birth, partly by p
2 CONC opular choice. The criminal meansby which he is said to have reached th
2 CONC is throne are pure creation of thebarbarian imagination. Master now of a v
2 CONC ast kingdom, Clovis displayedthe same talent in governing that he had d
2 CONC isplayed in conquering it.From Paris, which he had finally made his cap
2 CONC ital, he administered thevarious provinces through the agency of counts (
2 CONC comites) established ineach city and selected by him from the aristocra
2 CONC cy of both races,conformably to the principle of absolute equality betw
2 CONC een Romans andbarbarians, a principle which dominated his entire policy
2 CONC . He caused theSalic Law (Lex Salica) to be reduced to written form, re
2 CONC vised endadapted to the new social conditions under which his fellow ba
2 CONC rbaricanswere subsequently to live. Acknowledging the Church as the for
2 CONC emostcivilizing force, he protected it in every way possible, especiall
2 CONC y byproviding for it the National Council of Orleans (511), at which th
2 CONC ebishops of Gaul settled many questions pertaining to the relationsbetw
2 CONC een Church and state. Hagiographic legends attribute to Clovis thefound
2 CONC ing of a great many churches and monasteries throughout France, andalth
2 CONC ough the accuracy of this claim cannot be positively established, itis n
2 CONC evertheless certain that the influence of the council in this mattermus
2 CONC t have been considerable. However, history has preserved the memoryof f
2 CONC oundation which was undoubtedly due to Clovis: the church of theApostle
2 CONC s, later of Sainte-Genevieve, on what was then Mons Lucotetius,to the s
2 CONC outh of Paris. The king destined it as a mausoleum for himselfand his q
2 CONC ueen Clotilda, and before it was completed his mortal remainswere there i
2 CONC nterred. Clovis died at the age of forty-five. Hissarcophagus remained i
2 CONC n the crypt of Sainte-Genevieve until the time ofthe French Revolution, w
2 CONC hen it was broken open by the revolutionists,and his ashes scattered to t
2 CONC he winds, the sanctuary of the beautifulchurch being destroyed. The his
2 CONC tory of this monarch has been sohopelessly distorted by popular poetry a
2 CONC nd so grossly disfigured by thevagaries of the barbarian imagination as m
2 CONC ake the portrayal of hischaracter wellneigh impossible. However, from a
2 CONC uthentic accounts of himit may be concluded that his private life was n
2 CONC ot without virtues. As astatesman he succeeded in accomplishing what ne
2 CONC ither the genius ofTheodoric the Great nor that of any contemporary bar
2 CONC barian king couldachieve: upon the ruins of the Roman Empire he built u
2 CONC p a powerfulsystem, the influence of which dominated European civilizat
2 CONC ion duringmany centuries, and from which sprang France, Germany, Belgiu
2 CONC m, Holland,and Switzerland, without taking into account that northern S
2 CONC pain andnorthern Italy were also, for a time, under the civilizing regi
2 CONC me of theFrankish Empire. Clovis left four sons. Theodoric, the eldest, w
2 CONC as theissue of union prior to that contracted with Clotilda, who was, h
2 CONC owever,the mother of the three others, Clodomir, Childebert, and Clotai
2 CONC re. Theydivided their father's kingdom among themselves, following the b
2 CONC arbarianprinciple that sought promotion of personal rather than nationa
2 CONC linterests, and looked upon royalty as the personal prerogative of thes
2 CONC ons of kings. After the death of Clovis his daughter Clotilda, namedaft
2 CONC er her mother, married Amalric, king of the Visigoths. She diedyoung, b
2 CONC eing cruelly abused by this Arian prince, who seemed eager towreak veng
2 CONC eance on the daughter of Clovis for the tragic death of AlaricII. ARNDT (
2 CONC ed.), GREGORY OF TOURS, Historia ecclesiastica Francorum inMon. Germ. H
2 CONC ist:. Script. RR. Merovingicarum; JUNGHANS, Die Gesdichteder frankische
2 CONC n Konige Childerich und Chlodovich (Gottingen, 1857), tr.by MONOD as Hi
2 CONC store critique de rois Childeric et Clovis (Paris, 1879);RAJNA, Le orig
2 CONC ini dell' epopea francese (Florence, 1884); KURTH,Histoire poetique des M
2 CONC erovingiens (Paris 1893): IDEM, Clovis (Tours,1896, and Paris, 1901). G
2 CONC ODEFROID KURTH Transcribed by Joseph P. Thomas[The Catholic Encyclopedi
