1 NOTE FROM www.britannica.com Austrasia the eastern Frankish kingdom in theMe
2 CONC rovingian period (6th-8th century AD) of early medieval Europe, asdisti
2 CONC nct from Neustria, the western kingdom. Its mayors of the palace,leadin
2 CONC g household and government officials under the king, wereancestors of t
2 CONC he Carolingian dynasty. Covering present northeasternFrance and areas o
2 CONC f western and central Germany, the kingdom includedthe old homeland of t
2 CONC he Ripuarian (Rhineland) Franks. Ruled from 561 to613 by Sigebert I and h
2 CONC is descendants, it was briefly reunited with theother Frankish kingdoms i
2 CONC n the early 7th century. From 634, whenDagobert, sole king of the Frank
2 CONC s from 629, gave to the Austrasians hisson Sigebert III as their separa
2 CONC te king, Austrasia had its fixed capitalat Metz and its own mayor of th
2 CONC e palace. Its ascendancy over the otherFrankish kingdoms was assured wh
2 CONC en the Carolingian Austrasian mayor ofthe palace, Pepin II, defeated th
2 CONC e Neustrians at Tertry in 687. Hisgrandson, Pepin III the Short, in 751 d
2 CONC eposed the last Merovingian kingand was himself elected king instead. S
2 CONC ee also Merovingian dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Profession : Maire.du.palais d'Austrasie (679) & de Neustrie (680).
