1 NOTE FROM: www.britannica.com Charles Martel b. c. 688 d. Oct. 22, 741,Quier
2 CONC zy-sur-Oise, France Latin CAROLUS MARTELLUS, German KARL MARTELL,mayor o
2 CONC f the palace of Austrasia (the eastern part of the Frankishkingdom) fro
2 CONC m 715 to 741. He reunited and ruled the entire Frankishrealm and stemme
2 CONC d the Muslim invasion at Poitiers in 732. His byname,Martel, means 'the h
2 CONC ammer.' Charles was the illegitimate son of Pepin ofHerstal, the mayor o
2 CONC f the palace of Austrasia. By this period theMerovingian kings of the F
2 CONC rankish realm were rulers in name only. Theburden of rule lay upon the m
2 CONC ayors of the palace, who governedAustrasia, the eastern part of the Fra
2 CONC nkish kingdom, and Neustria, itswestern portion. Neustria bitterly rese
2 CONC nted its conquest and annexationin 687 by Pepin, who, acting in the nam
2 CONC e of the king, had reorganizedand reunified the Frankish realm. The ass
2 CONC assination of Pepin's onlysurviving legitimate son in 714 was followed a f
2 CONC ew months later by thedeath of Pepin himself. Pepin left as heirs three g
2 CONC randsons, and untilthey came of age, Plectrude, Pepin's widow, was to h
2 CONC old power. As anillegitimate son, Charles Martel was entirely neglected i
2 CONC n the will. Buthe was young, strong, and determined, and an intense str
2 CONC uggle for powerat once broke out in the Frankish kingdom. Both Charles a
2 CONC nd Plectrudefaced rebellion throughout the Frankish kingdom when Pepin'
2 CONC s will wasmade known. The king, Chilperic II, was in the power of Ragen
2 CONC frid, mayorof the palace of Neustria, who joined forces with the Frisia
2 CONC ns inHolland in order to eliminate Charles. Plectrude imprisoned Charle
2 CONC s andtried to govern in the name of her grandchildren, but Charles esca
2 CONC ped,gathered an army, and defeated the Neustrians in battles at Ambleve n
2 CONC earLiege (716) and at Vincy near Cambrai (717). His success made resist
2 CONC anceby Plectrude and the Austrasians useless; they submitted, and by 71
2 CONC 9Charles alone governed the Franks as mayor. Assured of Austrasia,Charl
2 CONC es now attacked Neustria itself, finally subduing it in 724. Thisfreed C
2 CONC harles to deal with hostile elements elsewhere. He attackedAquitaine, w
2 CONC hose ruler, Eudes (Odo), had been an ally of Ragenfrid, butCharles did n
2 CONC ot gain effective control of southern France until late inhis reign. He a
2 CONC lso conducted long campaigns, some as late as the 730s,against the Fris
2 CONC ians, Saxons, and Bavarians, whose brigandage endangeredthe eastern fro
2 CONC ntiers of his kingdom. In order to consolidate hismilitary gains, Charl
2 CONC es supported St. Boniface and other missionaries intheir efforts to con
2 CONC vert the German tribes on the eastern frontier toChristianity. Ever sin
2 CONC ce their arrival in Spain from Africa in 711, theMuslims had raided Fra
2 CONC nkish territory, threatening Gaul and on oneoccasion (725) reaching Bur
2 CONC gundy and sacking Autun. In 732 'Abdar-Rahman, the governor of CA3rdoba
2 CONC , marched into Bordeaux and defeatedEudes. The Muslims then proceeded n
2 CONC orth across Aquitaine to the city ofPoitiers. Eudes appealed to Charles f
2 CONC or assistance, and Charles' cavalrymanaged to turn back the Muslim onsl
2 CONC aught at the Battle of Poitiers. Thebattle itself may have been only a s
2 CONC eries of small engagements, butafter it there were no more great Muslim i
2 CONC nvasions of Frankishterritory. In 733 Charles began his campaigns to fo
2 CONC rce Burgundy toyield to his rule. In 735 word arrived that Eudes was de
2 CONC ad, and Charlesmarched rapidly across the Loire River in order to make h
2 CONC is power feltaround Bordeaux. By 739 he had completely subdued the pett
2 CONC y chieftainsof Burgundy, and he continued to fend off Muslim advances i
2 CONC nto Gaulduring the decade. Charles' health began to fail in the late 73
2 CONC 0s, andin 741 he retired to his palace at Quierzy-sur-Oise, where he di
2 CONC ed soonafter. Before his death he divided the Merovingian kingdom betwe
2 CONC en histwo legitimate sons, Pepin and Carloman. He continued to maintain t
2 CONC hefiction of Merovingian rule, refraining from transferring the royalti
2 CONC tle to his own dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chosen Duke of Austrasia (in France) in 714, he became mayor of thepala
2 CONC ce of King Clothaire IV in 720 and was the true ruler of theFranks.His f
2 CONC irst battles were with the Saxons, Alemanni and Bavarians. However,his i
2 CONC mportance was established when he rolled back the tide of theSaracen Mo
2 CONC slem conquest in a desperate battle between Tours and Poitiersin 732. P
2 CONC rior to the battle, Abdul Rahman, the Arab governor of Spain,had won a g
2 CONC reat battle near Bordeaux. This Moslem threat united theBurgundians and t
2 CONC he Gauls of Provence who then acknowledged sovereigntyto Charles Martel
2 CONC , as they saw him as their saviour from the Moslemconquests. The battle a
2 CONC t Poitiers, now almost forgotten, must rank amongthe most important as i
2 CONC t halted the Moslem conquest of Europe. Charlesfinished his work by dri
2 CONC ving the Saracens out of Burgundy and theLanguedoc in 737. Great as he w
2 CONC as, he never aspired to being more than"mayor of the palace" and Duke o
2 CONC f Austrasia. When he died in 741, hissons, Carloman and Pepin, still as j
2 CONC oint mayors of the palace, dividedthe power for Merovingian King Childe
2 CONC ric III of the Franks.
2 CONT
