1 NOTE FROM: The Columbia Encyclopedia, Sixth Edition. 2001http://www.bartleby
2 CONC .com  Austrasia (ostrA'zh), northeastern portionof the Merovingian king
2 CONC dom of the Franks  in the 6th, 7th, and 8thcent., comprising, in genera
2 CONC l, parts of E France, W Germany, and theNetherlands, with its capital v
2 CONC ariously at Metz, Reims, and Soissons. Itoriginated in the partition (5
2 CONC 11) of the realm of the Frankish kingClovis I  among his four sons afte
2 CONC r his death. Austrasia was constantlytroubled by dynastic rivalries bet
2 CONC ween its rulers and those of theneighboring kingdom of Neustria. These s
2 CONC truggles, both political andcultural, reached their climax in the fierc
2 CONC e fights between QueenBrunhilda of Austrasia and Queen Fredegunde of Ne
2 CONC ustria. During thereigns of Clotaire I, Clotaire II, and Dagobert I, Au
2 CONC strasia wastemporarily reunited with Neustria. This rivalry was only pa
2 CONC rt of theregionalism that eventually brought an end to Merovingian rule
2 CONC . With thedecline of the royal power in Austrasia, the office of mayor o
2 CONC f thepalace developed into the real seat of power and finally becameher
2 CONC editary in the family of the Carolingians. Austrasia became part ofthe C
2 CONC arolingian empire. FROM: http://www.encyclopedia.com Pepin ofHeristal (
2 CONC Pepin II) heristl pepin, d. 714, mayor of the palace(680-714) of the Fr
2 CONC ankish territory of Austrasia; grandson of Pepin ofLanden and father of C
2 CONC harles Martel. After defeating the nobles ofNeustria at the battle of T
2 CONC ertry (687), Pepin made himself mayor, orruler, of all the Frankish kin
2 CONC gdoms except Aquitaine, with theMerovingian dynasty retaining the nomin
2 CONC al kingship. He defeated theFrisians, the Alemanni, and the Bavarians a
2 CONC nd established a stronggovernment, thus laying the foundation for the e
2 CONC mpire of hisdescendants, the Carolingian mayors and kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having inherited the joint right to rule the kingdom of The Frankstoget
2 CONC her with his brother, he became sole ruler in 747 when Carlomanretired i
2 CONC nto the monastery of Monte Casino. In 751 he asked PopeZacharias to end t
2 CONC he nominal rule of the Merovingians and have the powertogether with the t
2 CONC itle of "King of The Franks". The Pope agreed andKing Childeric III was p
2 CONC laced in a monastery. Saint Boniface anointedPepin as King of The Frank
2 CONC s at Soissons. Two years later---having savedthe next pope, Stephen II, f
2 CONC rom the Lombards ---Pepin was again anointedat the Abbey of St. Denis, t
2 CONC ogether with his two young sons, Charles andCarloman, by the pope himse
2 CONC lf. Pippin was a much more able king than theMerovingians "Rois fainean
2 CONC ts" (do nothing kings). The Franks went toItaly to support the pope and d
2 CONC efeated Astolfo, King of the Lombards.Pippin was rewarded and made a se
2 CONC nator of Rome even though he couldneither read nor write. After a subse
2 CONC quent attack on the pope, he againdefeated Astolfo and made a gift to t
2 CONC he pope of Lombard lands near Rome.This bequest was the beginning of th
2 CONC e pope's status as a temporalsouvereign. Pippin died at the Abbey of St
2 CONC . Denis in 768. His sonsCharles and Carloman forthwith divided the Fran
2 CONC k domains. However,Carloman soon died leaving Charles, as the sole rule
2 CONC r of the kingdom ofThe Franks, to become the most important ruler ever t
2 CONC o have "the Great"added to his name. Charles The Great, or Carolus Magn
2 CONC us, then becamebetter known as Charlemagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Profession : Maire.du.Palais de 741 à 751, Roi des Francs de 751 à 768.
