1 NOTE Profession : Roi de Mercie en 821.
2 CONT
2 CONT In AD 821, King Cenwulf of Mercia died at Basingwerk, while campaigning
2 CONC against the Welsh of Powys. He left two daughters, Cwendreda andBurgenh
2 CONC ilda, and a son, a child of seven years old, named Cenelm or,more commo
2 CONC nly these days, Cenelm, who was chosen to succeed him.Cwendreda envied h
2 CONC er little brother and thought that, if he were killed,she might reign a
2 CONC s Queen. She therefore conspired with her lover,Askbert, who was her br
2 CONC other's tutor and guardian, and gave him money,saying, "Slay my brother f
2 CONC or me, that I may reign." Burgenilda was notprivy to this wicked deed, h
2 CONC owever, for she loved her little brother.
2 CONT So, on 3rd November, Ascbert took Cenelm out into the Forests ofWorcest
2 CONC ershire on a hunting trip. After the exertions of the chase, theyoung l
2 CONC ad soon tired with the heat, and decided to lay down under a treefor a n
2 CONC ap. Ascbert, meanwhile, began to dig a grave; but the boysuddenly awoke a
2 CONC nd admonished him, "You think to kill me here in vain,for I shall be sl
2 CONC ain in another spot. In token, thereof, see this rodblossom." And he th
2 CONC rust a stick into the ground, which instantly tookroot and began to flo
2 CONC wer. It grew, in years after, to be a great ashtree, which was known as S
2 CONC t. Kenelm's Ash. Unperturbed, Askbert took thelittle King further into t
2 CONC he forest and up to the Clent Hills, toRomsley (Worcs), where the child b
2 CONC egan to sing the "Te Deum," theassassin smote his head clean off; and t
2 CONC hen he buried him in thethicket.
2 CONT Now, at the same time, a white dove is said to have flown into thechurc
2 CONC h of St. Peter in Rome, with a letter in its beak which itdeposited on t
2 CONC he high altar. And men took the letter and tried to read,but they could n
2 CONC ot make it out, for it was written in English. At last,an Englishman wa
2 CONC s found, however, and he read the letter. It stated thatCenelm, the lit
2 CONC tle King of the Mercians, had been cruelly murdered andhis body hidden i
2 CONC n a thicket.
2 CONT So the Pope wrote letters to the kings of the English and told them wha
2 CONC tan evil deed had been done in their land, and men went forth to seek t
2 CONC hebody. As they went, they saw a pillar of light shining over a thicket i
2 CONC nWorcestershire and, there, they found the body of Cenelm. They carried
2 CONC him to the Royal Mercian Abbey at Winchcombe, in Gloucestershire, where
2 CONC he was buried with all honour and revered as a martyr. But over theplac
2 CONC e where they found his body, they built a little chapel. Today it isthe C
2 CONC hurch of St. Cenelm at Romsley in the Clent Hills.
2 CONT The story of Cenelm appears to bear little relation to the known factsa
2 CONC bout him. Somewhat older than seven, he signed a number of his father's
2 CONC charters between AD 798 & 811, he owned land in Glastonbury (Somerset)a
2 CONC nd is thought to have died, fighting the Welsh, in AD 812. In aninteres
2 CONC ting twist, however, local historian, Roger Chambers, suggeststhat ther
2 CONC e were two Cenelm half-brothers, one named after the other, andthat thi
2 CONC s may have been the elder of the two; Cenelm the younger beingthe Saint o
2 CONC f popular legend. His feast day is celebrated on 17th July,the date of h
2 CONC is translation to Winchcombe.
