1 NOTE When Prince Henry revolted against his father in 1173, Sir Robert deBoh
2 CONC onwas one of his associates. In the next year, the situationbecamecriti
2 CONC cal when Prince Henry persuaded William, the Lion King ofScotland,to jo
2 CONC in him in war against his father in return for thepromise ofNorthumberl
2 CONC and, the northernmost Shire of England.Humphrey, third baronde Bohon, l
2 CONC ed the English King's army, which metand repulsed the rebelsat Fordham i
2 CONC n Suffolk in 1173, and along withSir Richard de Lacy, metthe forces of S
2 CONC cotland and Prince Henry onJuly 13, 1174. The battleended in the comple
2 CONC te rout of the rebelliousforces. King William wascaptured and imprisone
2 CONC d. Sir Robert de Bohonfled to Scotland and PrinceHenry was forgiven. Ki
2 CONC ng William's sister,Princess Margaret of Scotlandand the widow of Conan I
2 CONC V of Bretagne,Earl of Richmond, (d. 1170), wasmarried to Humphrey de Bo
2 CONC hon, theyoung Earl of Hereford was who heir ofthe third Baron de Bohon. S
2 CONC ir Robert le Bon died in 1183, four yearsbefore his father BaronHumphre
2 CONC y de Bohon's death. Consequently, whenHumphrey the third Barondied in 1
2 CONC 187, he was succeeded by his 12 yearold grandson, Henry(1176-1220) as f
2 CONC ourth Baron de Bohon. Upon coming ofage, Henry wasknighted and made Hig
2 CONC h Constable of England and held manorhouses andcastles at Caldicot, Har
2 CONC esfield, Oaksey and Walden in additionto themain Hereford holdings. A n
2 CONC umber of months after Sir Robert deBohon fled to Scotland, KingWilliam s
2 CONC igned the humiliating Treaty ofFolaise and was released fromhis prison i
2 CONC n Normandy. Upon returning toScotland, he awarded some ofhis followers i
2 CONC ncluding Sir Robert de Bohon,some land south ofGlasgow. Sir Robert spen
2 CONC t little time on his land ashe was withPrince Henry who had been forgiv
2 CONC en by King Henry II.Unfortunately forSir Robert de Bohon, young Henry t
2 CONC he crown prince, diedsix yearsbefore his father. Sir Robert was with hi
2 CONC m at Martel inAquitaine onJune 11, 1183 when it happened. As far as it i
2 CONC s known, SirRobert de Bohon never returned to Englandbut lived out his l
2 CONC ife (d.after 1210) on his Scottish land and becamea highly respected ma
2 CONC noriallord. Legend has it that he was called 'leBon' ( the good). His s
2 CONC on andheir had similar characteristics so thathe too was known as Sir R
2 CONC ichard'le Bon' de Bon.
