1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Llywelyn was born in 1173, the son of Iorwerth Drwyndwn ab OwainGwynedd
2 CONC , prince of Gwynedd, and Margred ferch Madog. In 1205 he marriedJoan of E
2 CONC ngland, the illegitimate daughter of King John. They had a sonDafydd an
2 CONC d daughter Elen who would have progeny. By his mistressTangwystl he als
2 CONC o had a son Gruffydd and daughter Gwladys Dhu who wouldhave progeny, an
2 CONC d two daughters by other mistresses.
2 CONT
2 CONT With vision and resourcefulness, and by exploiting Welsh tradition anda
2 CONC dapting feudal habits, Llywelyn tried to create a powerful andpermanent p
2 CONC rincipality. It took a struggle, from 1188 till 1203, beforehe mastered G
2 CONC wynedd.
2 CONT
2 CONT By marrying King John's daughter Joan, he hoped to keep the peace withE
2 CONC ngland. However, his own aggression in Powys caused John to retaliatean
2 CONC d Llewelyn allied himself with the French. Taking advantage of thecivil w
2 CONC ar in England, he extended his dominion into Carmarthenshire andCardiga
2 CONC nshire. In 1216 he made himself lord of Powys, which in 1218 wasacknowl
2 CONC edged by the English.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1229, against Welsh tradition, he tried to secure recognition for hi
2 CONC sson Dafydd as his successor; by this he snubbed his elder bastard sonG
2 CONC ruffydd, a headstrong man who was deeply resentful. In 1230 Llywelynhan
2 CONC ged William de Braose, the father-in-law of his son Dafydd, forhaving h
2 CONC ad an affaire with his wife Joan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also in 1230 he took the unique title of Prince of Aberffraw and Lord o
2 CONC fSnowdon, which was claimed to imply superiority over other Welsh ruler
2 CONC s.However, he was forced to pay homage to Henry III, John's son andsucc
2 CONC essor as king of England. In 1237 he possibly had a stroke and hisson D
2 CONC afydd probably governed until 1240 when Llewellyn died.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in his favourite monastery, the Cistercian abbey ofAberco
2 CONC nwy which he had founded. His wife Joan died in 1237.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Encyclopedia Britannica Online; article entitled LlywelynAPIorwert
2 CONC h: Called 'LLYWELYN THE GREAT, Welsh prince, the mostoutstanding native
2 CONC ruler to  appear in Wales before the region came underEnglish rule in12
2 CONC 83. 'Llywelyn was the grandson of Owain Gwynedd (d.1170), a powerfulrul
2 CONC er of Gwynedd  in northern Wales. While still achild, Llywelyn wasexile
2 CONC d by his uncle, David. He  deposed David in 1194and by 1202 hadbrought m
2 CONC ost of northern Wales under his  control. In1205 he marriedJoan, the il
2 CONC legitimate daughter of England's King John(reigned1199-1216). Neverthel
2 CONC ess, when Llywelyn's attempts to extendhisauthority  into southern Wale
2 CONC s threatened English possessions,Johninvaded Wales (1211) and  overran m
2 CONC ost of Gwynedd. The prince soonwonback his lands. He secured his positi
2 CONC on  by allying withJohn'spowerful baronial opponents, and his actions h
2 CONC elped thebaronsinfluence the king's signing of Magna Carta (1215). 'Two y
2 CONC earsafter the accession of King Henry III (reigned 1216-72),the English
2 CONC acknowledged that Llywelyn controlled almost all ofWales, but by 1223th
2 CONC ey had  forced him to withdraw to the northbehind a boundary betweenCar
2 CONC digan, Dyfed, and  Builth, Powys. ManyWelsh princes in the south,howeve
2 CONC r, still accepted his  overlordship.In his last years the agedLlywelyn t
2 CONC urned his government over to his son David (prince ofGwynedd). When Lly
2 CONC welyn died, a chroniclerdescribed him as prince ofWales, which he was i
2 CONC n fact, if not inlaw.'
