1 NOTE Encyclopedia Britannica Online, article titled 'Henry the YoungKing:''a
2 CONC lso called HENRY FITZHENRY, second son of King Henry II of EnglandbyEle
2 CONC anor of Aquitaine; he was regarded, after the death of hiselderbrother, W
2 CONC illiam, in 1156, as his father's successor inEngland,Normandy, and Anjo
2 CONC u. 'In 1158 Henry, only three years of age,was betrothed to Margaret,da
2 CONC ughter of Louis VII of France and his secondwife, on condition thatMarg
2 CONC aret's dowry would be the Vexin, the borderregion betweenNormandy (then h
2 CONC eld by England) and France. Henry II tookadvantage ofPope Alexander III
2 CONC 's political difficulties to secure thePope'spermission for the childre
2 CONC n to be married in 1160. On June 14,1170,the young Henry was crowned ki
2 CONC ng (theoretically to rule inassociationwith his father) at Westminster b
2 CONC y Archbishop Roger of York.York'sofficiation, usurping a prerogative of t
2 CONC he archbishop ofCanterbury,exacerbated the dispute between the latter, n
2 CONC amely, ThomasBecket, andHenry II, which ended with Becket's murder six m
2 CONC onths later.Crownedagain on Aug. 27, 1172 (this time with Margaret), th
2 CONC e YoungKingreceived no share of his father's power. (He was nevertheles
2 CONC scalledby contemporaries and by certain later chroniclers King HenryIII
2 CONC .) 'With his mother and his brothers Richard (the future Richard I)andG
2 CONC eoffrey, he nearly overthrew Henry II in 1173. Forgiven forthisrevolt, h
2 CONC e intrigued further against his father with Louis VII.In1182-83 he wage
2 CONC d war against Richard over Poitou, and he waspreparingto fight Richard a
2 CONC gain when he died in France of dysentery.'The Young King was so popular t
2 CONC hat the people of Le Mans andRouenalmost went to war for the custody of h
2 CONC is body, and in hismother'shereditary lands he was immortalized in the '
2 CONC Lament for theYoungKing' by the troubadour Bertran de Born.'
