1 NOTE 'Richard I, the Lion-hearted, spent much of his youth in hismother'scou
2 CONC rt at Poitiers. Richard cared much more for thecontinentalpossessions o
2 CONC f his mother than for England - he also caredmuch morefor his mother th
2 CONC an for his father. Family considerationsinfluencedmuch of his life: he f
2 CONC ought along side of his brothers PrinceHenryand Geoffrey in their rebel
2 CONC lion of 1173-4; he fought for hisfatheragainst his brothers when they s
2 CONC upported an 1183 revolt inAquitane;and he joined Philip II of France ag
2 CONC ainst his father in1188,defeating Henry in 1189. 'Richard spent but six m
2 CONC onths of histen-year reign in England. Heacted upon a promise to his fa
2 CONC ther to jointhe Third Crusade anddeparted for the Holy Land in 1190 (ac
2 CONC companied byhis partner-rivalPhilip II of France). In 1191, he conquere
2 CONC d Cyprus enroute toJerusalem and performed admirably against Saladin, n
2 CONC early takingtheholy city twice. Philip II, in the meantime, returned to F
2 CONC ranceandschemed with Richard's brother John.  The Crusade failed initsp
2 CONC rimary objective of liberating the Holy Land from Moslem Turks,butdid h
2 CONC ave a positive result - easier access to the region forChristianpilgrim
2 CONC s through a truce with Saladin. Richard received word ofJohn'streachery a
2 CONC nd decided to return home; he was captured by Leopold VofAustria and im
2 CONC prisoned by Holy Roman Emperor Henry VI.Theadministrative machinery of H
2 CONC enry II insured the continuance ofroyalauthority, as Richard was unable t
2 CONC o return to his realm until1194.Upon his return, he crushed a coup atte
2 CONC mpt by John and regainedlandslost to Philip II during the German captiv
2 CONC ity. Richard's warwithPhilip continued sporadically until the French we
2 CONC re finallydefeatednear Gisors in 1198. 'Richard died April 6, 1199, fro
2 CONC m a woundreceived in a skirmish atthe castle of Chalus in the Limousin. N
2 CONC ear hisdeath, Richard finallyreconciled his position with his late fath
2 CONC er, asevidenced by SirRichard Baker in A Chronicle of the Kings of Engl
2 CONC and:'The remorse forhis undutifulness towards his father, was living in h
2 CONC imtill he died;for at his death he remembered it with bewailing, anddes
2 CONC ired to beburied as near him as might be, perhaps as thinking theyshoul
2 CONC d meetthe sooner, that he might ask him forgiveness in anotherworld.'Ri
2 CONC chard's prowess and courage in battle earned him the nicknameCoeurDe Li
2 CONC on ('heart of the lion'), but the training of his mother'scourtis revea
2 CONC led in a verse Richard composed during his germancaptivity:                        '
2 CONC No one will tell me the cause of mysorrow Whythey have made me a                        p
2 CONC risoner here.Wherefore with dolour I now makemy moan; Friends had Imany b
2 CONC ut help have I none. Shameful it isthatthey leave me to ransom, To                        l
2 CONC anguish here twowinters long.'  Reigned 1189-1199. Prisoner in Germany 1
2 CONC 192-1194.  Ahero of Medievallegends spent all but 6 months of his reign a
2 CONC broad. Hebecame Duke ofAquitaine in 1168 and of Poitiers in 1172. He jo
2 CONC ined the3rd crusadein 1189 and conquered Messina and Cyprus before arri
2 CONC ving inthe HolyLand. His victory at Arsuf gained Joppa (1191). On his w
2 CONC ay homehe wasaptured in Austria and was only released by Emporer Henry V
2 CONC Iafterpayment of an enourmous ransom (1194). He returned briefly toEngl
2 CONC andbut died in France. Most sources agree that he died withoutissue, bu
2 CONC t there are some thatclaim he had this one child.  I have noproof of ei
2 CONC ther.  It is alwaysa possiblity that he left descendants, butin this ca
2 CONC se as he wasroyalty and well documented it is doubtful.However I added i
