1 NOTE Geoffrey was also known as 'The Plantagenet' and more commonlyas'Planta
2 CONC genet, Geoffrey V the Fair, Count of Anjou and Maine'. HewasDuke of Nor
2 CONC mandy 1144-1150 abdicating ca 1151. The Plantagenetsurname was original
2 CONC ly a nickname, of the Englishroyal house of Anjou orthe Angevin dynasty
2 CONC , founded by Geoffrey V,Count of Anjou (1113-51),husband of Matilda (11
2 CONC 02-67), daughter ofKing Henry I of England. Thename is derived from the L
2 CONC atin planta('sprig') and genistae ('broomplant'), in reference to the s
2 CONC prig thatGeoffrey always wore in his cap.Burke says the marriage was 3 A
2 CONC pr1127. The name Plantagenet, accordingto Rapin, came from when Fulkthe G
2 CONC reat, being stung from remorse forsome wicked action, in orderto atone f
2 CONC or it, went a pilgrimage toJerusalem, and was scourgedbefore the Holy S
2 CONC epulchre with broom twigs.Reigning from 1154 to 1485, the Plantagenet k
2 CONC ings, in the main lineofdescent, were Henry II, Richard I, John, Henry I
2 CONC II, Edward I,EdwardII, Edward III, and Richard II; through the house of L
2 CONC ancaster,HenryIV, Henry V, and Henry VI; and through the house of York, E
2 CONC dwardIV,Edward V, and Richard III. Geoffrey was the Count of Anjou and M
2 CONC aine.From EncyclopediaBritannica Online, article entitled Geoffrey IV: '
2 CONC Alsocalled GEOFFREY PLANTAGENET, byname GEOFFREY THE FAIR, FrenchGEOFFR
2 CONC OIPLANTAGENET, OR GEOFFROI LE BEL, count of Anjou (1131-51),Maine, andT
2 CONC ouraine and ancestor of the Plantagenet kings of Englandthrough hismarr
2 CONC iage, in June 1128, to Matilda, daughter of Henry I ofEngland. OnHenry'
2 CONC s death (1135), Geoffrey claimed the duchy ofNormandy; he finallyconque
2 CONC red it in 1144 and ruled there as dukeuntil he gave it to his son,Henry (
2 CONC later King Henry II of England) in1150. Geoffrey was popular withthe No
2 CONC rmans, but he had to suppress arebellion of malcontent Angevinnobles. A
2 CONC fter a short war with LouisVII of France, Geoffrey signed atreaty (Augu
2 CONC st 1151) by which hesurrendered the whole of Norman Vexin(the border ar
2 CONC ea betweenNormandy and ãIle-de-France) to Louis.' Weis(Line 1-24) lists G
2 CONC eoffrey as Geoffrey V, not Geoffrey IV. FromEncyclopedia Britannica Onl
2 CONC ine, article entitled Geoffrey IV: 'Alsocalled GEOFFREY PLANTAGENET, by
2 CONC name GEOFFREY THE FAIR, FrenchGEOFFROIPLANTAGENET, OR GEOFFROI LE BEL, c
2 CONC ount of Anjou (1131-51),Maine, andTouraine and ancestor of the Plantage
2 CONC net kings of Englandthrough hismarriage, in June 1128, to Matilda, daug
2 CONC hter of Henry I ofEngland. OnHenry's death (1135), Geoffrey claimed the d
2 CONC uchy ofNormandy; he finallyconquered it in 1144 and ruled there as duke
2 CONC until he gave it to his son,Henry (later King Henry II of England) in11
2 CONC 50. Geoffrey was popular withthe Normans, but he had to suppress arebel
2 CONC lion of malcontent Angevinnobles. After a short war with LouisVII of Fr
2 CONC ance, Geoffrey signed atreaty (August 1151) by which hesurrendered the w
2 CONC hole of Norman Vexin(the border area betweenNormandy and ãIle-de-France
2 CONC ) to Louis.' Weis(Line 1-24) lists Geoffrey as Geoffrey V, not Geoffrey I
2 CONC V.
