1 NOTE He was the Prince of England, and Earl of Salisbury. Sources differ onm
2 CONC other, and neither is proven.  It is eitherAmabilia De Bailleol orAlice D
2 CONC e Porhoet He is a natural son of King Henry II. He was named inthe Magn
2 CONC a Carta in 1215.  From Encyclopedia Britannica, article titled:'Salisbu
2 CONC ry, WilliamLongsword, 3rd earl of' 'Longsword also spelledLONGESPâEE, a
2 CONC n illegitimate son of Henry II ofEngland, and a prominentbaron, soldier
2 CONC , and administrator under Johnand Henry III. He acquiredhis lands and t
2 CONC itle from Richard I, who in1196 gave him the hand of theheiress Ela, or I
2 CONC sabel, daughter ofWilliam, earl of Salisbury. He heldnumerous official p
2 CONC ositions inEngland under John. 'He was sent onmissions to France (1202) a
2 CONC nd to Germany (1209). In1213-14 he organizedJohn's Flemish allies, taki
2 CONC ng part in thedestruction (1213) of theFrench fleet at Damme, then the p
2 CONC ort ofBruges, and leading the rightwing of the allied army at Bouvines(
2 CONC July 27, 1214), where he wascaptured. He was exchanged and was backin E
2 CONC ngland by May 1215, when hewas employed by John in inspecting thedefens
2 CONC es of royal castles andfighting the rebels in the southwest. 'During Jo
2 CONC hn's war against thebarons, Salisbury deserted the kingafter the landin
2 CONC g of Louis of France(May 1216); he returned to royalallegiance, however
2 CONC , by March 1217,fought at Lincoln (May) andSandwich (August), and attes
2 CONC ted the Treaty ofKingston (September1217). Salisbury held various posts d
2 CONC uring theminority of Henry IIIand served against the Welsh in 1223 and i
2 CONC n Gasconyin 1225. He andhis wife were benefactors of Salisbury Cathedra
2 CONC l and laidfoundationstones of the new cathedral in 1220. William was bu
2 CONC ried thereand hiseffigy, a splendid early example, still survives.'
