1 NOTE Matilda is the Latin form of Maud, and the name of the onlysurvivingleg
2 CONC itimate child of King Henry I. She was born in 1101,generally itis said a
2 CONC t Winchester, but recent research indicates thatshe wasactually born at t
2 CONC he Royal Palace in Sutton Courtenay(Berkshire). In something of a polit
2 CONC ical coup for her father, Matildawas betrothedto the German Emperor, He
2 CONC nry V, when she was only eight.They weremarried on 7th January 1114. Sh
2 CONC e was twelve and he wasthirty-two.Unfortunately there were no children a
2 CONC nd on the Emperor'sdeath in1125, Matilda was recalled to her father's c
2 CONC ourt. Matilda's onlylegitimate brother had been killed in the disastrou
2 CONC sWreck of the WhiteShip in late 1120 and she was now her father's onlyh
2 CONC ope for thecontinuation of his dynasty. The barons swore allegianceto t
2 CONC he youngPrincess and promised to make her queen after herfather's death
2 CONC . Sheherself needed heirs though and in April 1127,Matilda found hersel
2 CONC fobliged to marry Prince Geoffrey of Anjou andMaine (the future Geoffre
2 CONC yV, Count of those Regions). He wasthirteen, she twenty-three. It istho
2 CONC ught that the two never got on.However, despite this unhappysituation t
2 CONC hey had had three sons infour years. Being absent in Anjou atthe time o
2 CONC f her father's death on 1stDecember 1135, possibly due topregnancy, Mat
2 CONC ilda was not in much of aposition to take up the thronewhich had been p
2 CONC romised her and shequickly lost out to her fast-movingcousin, Stephen. W
2 CONC ith her husband,she attempted to take Normandy. Withencouragement from s
2 CONC upporters inEngland though, it was not long beforeMatilda invaded her r
2 CONC ightfulEnglish domain and so began a long-standingCivil War from thepow
2 CONC erbase of her half-brother, Robert of Gloucester,in the WestCountry. Af
2 CONC ter three years of armed struggle, she at lastgained the upper handat t
2 CONC he Battle of Lincoln, in February 1141, whereKing Stephen wascaptured. H
2 CONC owever, despite being declared Queen or 'Ladyof theEnglish' at Winchest
2 CONC er and winning over Stephen's brother, HenryofBlois, the powerful Bisho
2 CONC p of Winchester, Matilda alienatedthecitizens of London with her arroga
2 CONC nt manner. She failed to securehercoronation and the Londoners joined a r
2 CONC enewed push fromStephen'sQueen and laid siege to the Empress in Winches
2 CONC ter. She managedtoescape to the West, but while commanding her rearguar
2 CONC d, her brotherwascaptured by the enemy. Matilda was obliged to swap Ste
2 CONC phen for Robert on1st November 1141.Thus the King soon reimposed his Ro
2 CONC yal authority. In1148, after thedeath of her half-brother, Matilda fina
2 CONC lly returned toNormandy,leaving her son, who, in 1154, would become Hen
2 CONC ry II, to fighton inEngland. She died at Rouen on 10th September 1169 a
2 CONC nd was buriedinFontevrault Abbey, though some of her entrails may possi
2 CONC bly havebeenlater interred in her father's foundation at Reading Abbey. S
2 CONC he wasthe Empress of Germany. From Encyclopedia Britannica Online,artic
2 CONC leentitled Matilda: 'Her brother's [William the Aetheling] death in 112
2 CONC 0made her HenryI's sole legitimate heir, and in 1127 he compelled theba
2 CONC ronage toaccept her as his successor, though a woman ruler wasequallyun
2 CONC precedented for the kingdom of England and the duchy ofNormandy.The  An
2 CONC gevin marriage was unpopular and flouted thebarons'stipulation that she s
2 CONC hould  not be married out of Englandwithouttheir consent. The birth of h
2 CONC er eldest son, Henry, in 1133 gavehopeof silencing this opposition, but h
2 CONC e was only two when Henry Idied(1135), and a rapid coup brought to the E
2 CONC nglish throne StephenofBlois,  son of William I the Conqueror's daughte
2 CONC r Adela. Thoughthechurch and the majority of the baronage supported Ste
2 CONC phen,Matilda'sclaims were powerfully upheld in England by her half brot
2 CONC herRobert ofGloucester and her uncle King David I of Scotland. Matilda a
2 CONC ndRobertlanded at Arundel in September 1139, and she was for a shortwhi
2 CONC lebesieged in the castle. But Stephen soon allowed her to joinherbrothe
2 CONC r, who had gone to the west country, where she had muchsupport;after a s
2 CONC tay at Bristol, she settled at Gloucester. 'She camenearest to success i
2 CONC n the summer of 1141, after Stephen hadbeen capturedat Lincoln in Febru
2 CONC ary. Elected 'lady of the English' bya clericalcouncil at Winchester in A
2 CONC pril, she entered London in June;but herarrogance and tactless demands f
2 CONC or money provoked the citizensto chaseher away to Oxford before she cou
2 CONC ld be crowned queen. Herforces wererouted at Winchester in September 11
2 CONC 41, and thereafter shemaintained asteadily weakening resistance in the w
2 CONC est country. Herwell-known escapefrom Oxford Castle over the frozen Riv
2 CONC er Thames tookplace in December1142. 'Normandy had been in her husband'
2 CONC s possession since 1144, andsheretired there in 1148, remaining near Ro
2 CONC uen to watch overtheinterests to her eldest son, who became duke of Nor
2 CONC mandy in 1150andKing Henry II of England in 1154. She spent the remaind
2 CONC er of herlifein Normandy exercising a steadying influence over HenryII'
2 CONC scontinental dominions.'
