1 NOTE Roger de Mortimer received all his father's lands (except those wherehi
2 CONC smother Gladuse was endowed). He was Captain General of all theking'sfo
2 CONC rces in Wales, but was overpowered by Llewelin, but owningtoconsanguini
2 CONC ty--he being Lewellin's nephew he was allowed to returntohis castle. Af
2 CONC ter accession of Edward I, he continued in royalfavor.He married Maud, d
2 CONC aughter and heir of William de Braose ofBrecknock.He had several sons a
2 CONC nd when he died in 1282 was succeeded byhiseldest surviving son, Edmund
2 CONC . He was the sixth Baron de Mortimer ofWigmore, he was knighted in June
2 CONC 1253 by the King at Winchester.  Heserved in Gascony in 1253-1254. Inth
2 CONC e disputes between the King and theBarons in 1258 he at first tookthe B
2 CONC aron's side and was one of thetwelve chosen by them to act withtwelve c
2 CONC hosen by the King.  On June 11,1259, he was appointed one ofthe commiss
2 CONC ioners to demand satisfactionfrom Llewelyn for breaches ofthe true.  In 1
2 CONC 262 and 1263 he was fightingagainst Llewelyn (hiscousin) with varying d
2 CONC egrees of success.  InSeptember 1264 Roger, asconstable of Cardigan, wa
2 CONC s ordered to give upthe castle to Guy deBrien, Montfort's nominee.  The L
2 CONC ords of March againbroke the trucewith Llewelyn, but by late December M
2 CONC ontfort and Llewelynhad overcomethem and Roger and the others were bani
2 CONC shed to Ireland for ayear, butdid not go.  At Evesham, August 4, 1265, R
2 CONC oger commanded therearguardand after Montfort's death his head was sent t
2 CONC o Roger'swife.(Complete Peerage, Vol IX, p. 277-80)
