1 NOTE This feudal lord, like his predecessors, was in constant strife withthe
2 CONC Welsh. He married 1st Millicent Ferrers, daughter of Robert, 4thEarl of
2 CONC Derby; 2nd, Isabel Ferrers, sister of Hugh of Oakham inRutlandshire. He
2 CONC inherited all of Hugh's lands and by his 2nd wife hadRalph, Robert andP
2 CONC hilip. He died in 1215 and was succeeded by hiseldest son, Hugh, son of
2 CONC his first wife, who died 1227, and was inturn succeeded by his halfbrot
2 CONC her Ralph de Mortimer. He was the Lord Of Wigmore. Lord Mortimer ofWigm
2 CONC ore, Herefordshire.Based upon a charge made in 1191 that he wasconspiri
2 CONC ng with the Welshagainst the King, he was forced to surrenderhis castle
2 CONC s and to'abjure' his country for three years.  In April 1194he was back i
2 CONC nEngland again, and witnessed a charter of Richard I afterhis secondCor
2 CONC onation at Winchester.  In 1195 he droves the sons ofCadwallon outof Ma
2 CONC elienydd and restored Cwmaron Castle, but in thefollowing yearRhys, Pri
2 CONC nce of South Wales defeated the cavalry of Rogerand Hugh deSay, the bat
2 CONC tle resulting in much slaughter near Radnor.Rogerforfeited his lands in N
2 CONC ormandy when Normandy was lost in 1204.Afterlanding at Dieppe in 1205 h
2 CONC e was captured and campelled to pararansom of 1,000 marks.  Some of his k
2 CONC nights served in the 1210invasionof Ireland.  (Complete Peerage, Vol. I
2 CONC X, p. 273)
