1 NOTE Roger Mortimer, 2nd Baron of Mortimer, was summoned toParliament1306-13
2 CONC 26. This nobleman, notorious in our histories as theparamourof Isabel, Q
2 CONC ueen Consort of Edward II, was in his sixteenth yearatthe death of his f
2 CONC ather. He married Jaone, daughter of PeterdeGenville, Lord of Trim, in I
2 CONC reland. In 34th of Edward I, about 1306,hereceived the honour of Knight
2 CONC hood. He aided in the Scottish wars,and in3rd of Edward II, 1310, he wa
2 CONC s made Governor of the Castle ofBuelt, andlater was Lord Lieutenant of I
2 CONC reland. During the latterpart of EdwardII's reign he attached himself t
2 CONC o the Queen, and atlength fled with herand Prince Edward to France. He l
2 CONC ater returnedand was made Earl of Marchsoon after the accession of Edwa
2 CONC rd III. Hehereupon became proud beyondmeasure (so that his son Geoffrey c
2 CONC alledhim the King of Folly) andassumed royal authority. His career was n
2 CONC othowever of long continuance,for King Edward III, becoming sensible of
2 CONC his folly and vices, had himseized in the Castle of Queen Isabel inNott
2 CONC ingham and was convictedunder various charges, the first wascomplicity i
2 CONC n the murder of EdwardII, and receiving sentence of deathwas hanged in 1
2 CONC 330. He left by Joande Geneville 4 sons and 7daughters. By marriage to J
2 CONC oanna de Geneville,a later Roger Mortimer (1287-1330)secured possession o
2 CONC f Ludlow Castle.This became the family'sprincipal power base for the ne
2 CONC xt sixgenerations. Roger Mortimer was a very powerful and ambitious Mar
2 CONC cherLord. He wasthe first of several members of his family to attempt t
2 CONC oseize thethrone of England. He fought the Scottish Wars and madeattemp
2 CONC ts toremove the King's favorites, at first with some success. In1323 he
2 CONC was imprisoned in the Tower of London, but escaped to France, aneventhe l
2 CONC ater commemorated by building St Peter's chapel in theouterbailey of Lu
2 CONC dlow Castle. In France, Mortimer formed an alliancewith Queen Isabella, w
2 CONC ho haddeserted her effeminate husband, King EdwardII of England. Theyra
2 CONC ised an army, invaded England and forced Edward toabdicate in favorof h
2 CONC is youngest son, the future Edward III. MortimerentertainedIsabella at h
2 CONC is castles on the Welsh borders and they becamefamouslovers. Meanwhile, E
2 CONC dward II was cruelly murdered at BerkeleyCastlein 1327. Following Edwar
2 CONC d's death, Mortimer, acting as regent, wasthe virtualruler of England, b
2 CONC ut he over-reached himself and aroused theanger ofother barons. In Octo
2 CONC ber 1330 he was arrested at Nottinghamandsentenced to death. He was exe
2 CONC cuted at Tyburn in London. Later, theambitions of the Mortimers became p
2 CONC art of the greatdynastic struggles ofthe mid-15th century which became k
2 CONC nown as the'War of the Roses.'  FromEncyclopedia Britannica Online, art
2 CONC icle titled: 'March, RogerMortimer,1st Earl of, 8th Baron Of Wigmore' '
2 CONC lover of the English King EdwardII's queen, Isabella of France, withwho
2 CONC m he contrived Edward'sdeposition and murder (1327). For threeyears the
2 CONC reafter he was virtualking of England during the minority ofEdward III. '
2 CONC The descendant ofNorman knights who had accompanied William theConquero
2 CONC r, he inheritedwealthy family estates and fortunes,principally in Wales a
2 CONC nd Ireland,and in 1304 became 8th Baron ofWigmore on the death of his f
2 CONC ather, the7th baron. He devoted theearly years of his majority to obtai
2 CONC ningeffective control of hisIrish lordships against his wife's kinsmen, t
2 CONC heLacys, who summoned totheir aid Edward Bruce, brother of King Robert I
2 CONC of Scotland, when hewas fighting to become king of Ireland. In 1316Mort
2 CONC imer was defeatedat Kells and withdrew to England, but afterward, asKin
2 CONC g Edward II'slieutenant in Ireland (November 1316), he was largelyinstr
2 CONC umental inovercoming Bruce and in driving the Lacys from Meath. 'In1317 h
2 CONC e was associated with the Earl of Pembroke's 'middle party'inEnglish po
2 CONC litics; but distrust of the Despensers (see Despenser,Hugh Leand Hugh L
2 CONC e) drove him, in common with other marcher lords,intoopposition and vio
2 CONC lent conflict with the Despensers in SouthWales in1321. But, receiving n
2 CONC o help from Edward II's other enemies,Roger andhis uncle Roger Mortimer o
2 CONC f Chirk made their submission inJanuary 1322.Imprisoned in the Tower of L
2 CONC ondon, Roger escaped in 1323and fled toFrance, where in 1325 he was joi
2 CONC ned by Queen Isabella, whobecame hismistress.  The exiles invaded Engla
2 CONC nd in September 1326;the fall of theDespensers was followed by the depo
2 CONC sition of Edward IIand his subsequentmurder (1327), in which Mortimer w
2 CONC as deeplyimplicated. 'Thereafter, asthe queen's paramour, Mortimer virt
2 CONC ually ruledEngland. He used hisposition to further his own ends. Create
2 CONC d Earl ofMarch in October 1328,he secured for himself the lordships ofD
2 CONC enbigh, Oswestry, and Clun,formerly belonging to the Earl ofArundel; th
2 CONC e marcher lordships of theMortimers of Chirk; andMontgomery, granted to h
2 CONC im by the queen. Hisinsatiable avarice, hisarrogance, and his unpopular p
2 CONC olicy towardScotland aroused againstMortimer a general revulsion among h
2 CONC is fellowbarons, and in October1330 the young king Edward III, at the i
2 CONC nstigationof Henry ofLancaster, had him seized at Nottingham and convey
2 CONC ed to theTower.Condemned for crimes declared to be notorious by his pee
2 CONC rsinParliament, he was hanged at Tyburn as a traitor, and his estateswe
2 CONC reforfeited to the crown.'
