1 NOTE He was also known as Foulques  'Le Jeune', Latin King ofJerusalem(1131-
2 CONC 1143), Count of Anjou  (1109-1129) as Fulk V,great-grandson ofFulk Nerr
2 CONC a. He journeyed (1120) to the Holy Land as apilgrim andreturned there i
2 CONC n 1129, making his son, Geoffrey Plantagenet,count ofAnjou as Geoffrey I
2 CONC V. Having taken as his new wife Melisende,daughterof King Baldwin II of J
2 CONC erusalem, he succeeded    hisfather-in-law in1131. Fulk's reign was dis
2 CONC turbed by dissensions amongthe Latinprinces and by the raids of the Tur
2 CONC ks, whose prisoner he wasfor atime in 1137. He was succeeded as King of J
2 CONC erusalem by his sonbyMelisende, Baldwin III.   From Encyclopedia Britan
2 CONC nica Online,articletitled Fulk: 'byname FULK THE YOUNGER, French FOULQU
2 CONC ES LE JEUNE,count of Anjou andMaine as Fulk V (1109-31) and king of Jer
2 CONC usalem(1131-43). 'Son of Fulk IV the Surly and Bertrada of Montfort, he w
2 CONC asmarried in1109 to Arenburga of Maine. Fulk exerted his control over h
2 CONC isvassalsand was later caught up in dynastic quarrels between the Frenc
2 CONC handEnglish kings. In 1128 his son Geoffrey Plantagenet marriedMatilda,
2 CONC daughter of Henry I of England, and became the progenitor ofEngland'sbr
2 CONC anch of the Angevin dynasty. Fulk first visited Palestine in1120and ret
2 CONC urned in 1129 to marry Melisend, daughter of King Baldwin IIofJerusalem
2 CONC . 'Fulk became king of Jerusalem on Baldwin II's death in1131 and spent
2 CONC the first year of his reign settling a dispute in Antioch(Turkey) andpu
2 CONC tting down a revolt led by his wife's lover, Hugh of LePuiset. In1137 h
2 CONC e allied himself with the Byzantines against a Turkishleader,'Imad ad-D
2 CONC in Zangi, of Mosul (Iraq), and in 1140 helped theMuslims ofDamascus war
2 CONC d off Zangi's armies. He protected Jerusalem inthe southby constructing a s
2 CONC eries of fortresses, including Krak ofMoab.'
