1 NOTE From The Complete Peerage, Vol, VII, p. 523-525: 'When very young heacc
2 CONC ompanied Duke William to England anddistinguished himself at thebattle o
2 CONC f Hastings, and received larggrants of lands in co. Warwick,with smalle
2 CONC r holdings in cos.Leicester, Northants, and Wilts.  On 14July 1080, as R
2 CONC obert deBellomonte, he witnessed the foundation charterof Lessay, and n
2 CONC extyear he inherited from his mother's family the comteof Meulan.Therea
2 CONC fter he is continuously styled Count (Comes) of Meulan.Afterthe death o
2 CONC f the Conqueror he adhered to William Rufus, and washighin favour at hi
2 CONC s court. He quarrelled with Robert of Normandyaboutthe castellanship of B
2 CONC rionne, in consequence of the exchangeofBrionne for Ivry made by his fa
2 CONC ther. He was imprisoned, butwasreleased at the intercession of his fath
2 CONC er Roger, whoeventuallysucceeded in obtaining Brionne in fee.  He succe
2 CONC eded to thegreaterpart of his father's lands in Normandy, including Bea
2 CONC umont,Pont-Audemer, Vatteville and Brionne. This paternal inheritance, a
2 CONC ddedtohis French comte and his great possessions in cos. WarwickandLeic
2 CONC ester, made him one of the most powerful vassals of the Crown.Hebecame o
2 CONC ne of the chief lay ministers of William Rufus, with whomhesided agains
2 CONC t Robert Courtheuse in 1098, and when William invadedtheFrench Verin in 1
2 CONC 097 he received his troops in his fortresses ofthecomte of Meulan.  Aft
2 CONC er the death of William Rufus he became oneofthe chief advisers of Henr
2 CONC y I. On the death of Ives de GrandmesnilonCrusade, Robert retained his e
2 CONC states, which Ives had mortgaged tohimcirca 1102. Thereby he acquired o
2 CONC ne-quarter of the town of Leicester,the whole of which was later grante
2 CONC d to him by the King.   Robertthusadded largely to his already vast pos
2 CONC sessions.  In 1104 he was oneofthe Norman barons who adhered to Henry o
2 CONC n his arrival in Normandy.Hewas present in the King's army at Tenchebra
2 CONC i, 28 Sep. 1106.  In1110he was besieged at Meulan by Louis VI, who took t
2 CONC he castle bystorm,but in the following year he retaliated by a raid on P
2 CONC aris, whichheplundered.  After obtaining thewhole town of Leicester he i
2 CONC s saidtohave become Earl of Leicester, but, being already Count of Meul
2 CONC an,wasnever so styled.' He was the Seigneur of Beaumont, Pont-Audemer,V
2 CONC atteville and Brionne,Count of Meulan in the French Vexin Seigneur ofBe
2 CONC aumont,Pont-Audemer, Vatteville and Brionne, Count of Meulan in theFren
2 CONC chVexin
