1 NOTE From Encyclopedia Britannica Online, article titled 'Blanche ofCastile:
2 CONC ''French BLANCHE DE CASTILLE, Spanish BLANCA DE CASTILLA, wife ofLouisV
2 CONC III of France, mother of Louis IX (St. Louis), and twice regentofFrance (
2 CONC 1226-34, 1248-52), who by wars and marital alliances didmuchto secure a
2 CONC nd unify French territories.   Blanche was the daughterofAlfonso VIII o
2 CONC f Castile and Eleanor, who was the daughter of HenryIIof England. Her g
2 CONC randmother Eleanor of Aquitaine, queen of England,traveled to Spain to t
2 CONC ake the 11-year-old Blanche to France,where amarriage treaty was conclu
2 CONC ded with Louis, the young son ofKing Philip IIAugustus. This politicall
2 CONC y motivated marriage had beenarranged byBlanche's uncle, King John of E
2 CONC ngland, and was celebratedin 1200 atPortsmouth, Hampshire. It represent
2 CONC ed only a brief truce inthe strugglebetween England and France for cont
2 CONC rol over certainFrench territories.'Blanche, who became French through m
2 CONC arriage, was gradually tobecomeFrench in spirit as well. Although she d
2 CONC id not cease to beconcernedfor her family, among them her uncle John an
2 CONC d his allies,herbrother-in-law Ferrand of Portugal, and her cousin Otto o
2 CONC fBrunswick(later Holy Roman emperor Otto IV), she rejoiced at the Frenc
2 CONC hvictoryover Otto and the English at Bouvines in 1214, marking the firs
2 CONC tstageof French unification, a goal for which she was constantly tostri
2 CONC ve.In the same year, she gave birth to Louis, the future king ofFrance. U
2 CONC pon John of England'sdeath, Blanche boldly tried to seize theEnglish th
2 CONC rone: in 1216 Louisof France invaded England on her behalf.The English s
2 CONC tood firmagainst him, and John's nine-year-old son wasfinally crowned H
2 CONC enryIII. 'A devout Roman Catholic, Blanche soon becameinvolved in what s
2 CONC hesincerely believed to be a holy war against theheretical Cathari, ase
2 CONC ct founded on the belief that good and evil hadtwo separatecreators, wh
2 CONC ich was flourishing throughout southern France.Herhusband, who became L
2 CONC ouis VIII in 1223, took part in a crusadeagainstthe Cathari but suffere
2 CONC d a fatal attack of dysentery uponreturning tothe north of France in 12
2 CONC 26. In accordance with herhusband's will,Blanche became both guardian o
2 CONC f the 12-year-old Louis andregent ofFrance. She zealously pressed to ha
2 CONC ve Louis crownedimmediately, andthe coronation took place at Reims thre
2 CONC e weeks afterLouis VIII'sdeath. 'Her most pressing problem was to deal w
2 CONC ith arebellion of the greatbarons, organized by Philip Hurepel, theille
2 CONC gitimate son of KingPhilip II Augustus, and supported by King HenryIII o
2 CONC f England. In theface of such adversity, Blanche showed herself byturns a d
2 CONC elicatediplomat, a clever negotiator, and a strong leader.Dressed in wh
2 CONC ite,on a white palfrey draped in the same colour, she rodeinto battle a
2 CONC tthe head of her troops. After an attempted abduction ofthe youngking, B
2 CONC lanche did not hesitate to replace rebel nobleassociates with commoners i
2 CONC f she thought itnecessary. She also createdlocal militias. Blanche was g
2 CONC radually ableto subdue the revolt,establish a new truce with England, a
2 CONC nd, in1229, pacify the south ofFrance by signing the Treaty of Paris wi
2 CONC thRaymond VII, count ofToulouse. France then entered an era of domestic
2 CONC stability, which saw theconstruction of many cathedrals throughoutthe c
2 CONC ountry. 'On only oneoccasion did Blanche fail to exhibit diplomatic con
2 CONC duct.In 1229 adispute between an innkeeper and some students took place i
2 CONC nthe LatinQuarter in Paris. The police were summoned, and the studentsw
2 CONC ere beatenand thrown into the Seine; such intervention in the LatinQuar
2 CONC ter,however, was contrary to the prerogatives granted to theuniversity, a
2 CONC ndthe faculty and students threatened to strike if theuniversity'sprivi
2 CONC leges were not respected. Badly advised, Blancheheld firm, but theunive
2 CONC rsity closed its doors, and the faculty andstudents left Paris forthe p
2 CONC rovinces and abroad. It was to take fouryears and the interventionof th
2 CONC e pope before the university wouldreturn to Paris with newprerogatives, t
2 CONC his time granted by Blancheherself. 'Although Louis IXcame of age on Ap
2 CONC ril 25, 1236, Blanche remained athis side as his mostloyal and steadfas
2 CONC t supporter. She lacked tact,however, with regard toher son's private l
2 CONC ife. Although Blancheherself had selected Margaret ofProvence to be Lou
2 CONC is's wife, shetreated Margaret with considerableseverity. In 1244, afte
2 CONC r Louisrecovered from a serious illness, he andhis wife, much againstBl
2 CONC anche's wishes, made a vow to go on a crusadeagainst the Muslims.They e
2 CONC mbarked in 1248, and once again the kingdomwas entrusted toBlanche. Inf
2 CONC ormed of Louis's defeat at Al-Mansurah,Egypt, and hissubsequent impriso
2 CONC nment, Blanche herself went to seek hisransom andthat of the French arm
2 CONC y. She petitioned her parents, herallies, andthe pope for funds and sup
2 CONC plies, but interest in the crusadehaddwindled. 'Although weakened by a h
2 CONC eart ailment, Blanche did notneglect herobligations as a regent. Contin
2 CONC uing to preside over councilmeetings,she signed laws and watched over t
2 CONC he poor of Paris. When someof thepoor were mistreated by the cathedral c
2 CONC hapter, she herself rode,asformerly, to open the gates to their prison. O
2 CONC n her way to the Abbeyofthe Lys, one of her favourite retreats, Blanche s
2 CONC uffered an attackof theheart ailment that was to take her life. She was r
2 CONC eturned tothe palaceof the Louvre, dressed in a nun's habit, and laid o
2 CONC n a bedof hay. There,after begging forgiveness of all and having receiv
2 CONC edthe last sacraments,she died. She was buried at Maubuisson Abbey andh
2 CONC er heart taken to theAbbey of the Lys. Louis IX was in Jaffa when helea
2 CONC rned of his mother'sdeath. The news distressed him greatly, for hewas a
2 CONC ware that he had lostnot only an incomparable parent but alsothe strong
2 CONC est supporter of hiskingship.'
