1 NOTE From Encyclopedia Britannica Online, article titled 'Alfonso VI:' 'byna
2 CONC meALFONSO THE EMPEROR, Spanish ALFONSO EL EMPERADOR, king ofLeon andCas
2 CONC tile from 1126 to 1157, son of Raymond of Burgundy and thegrandson ofAl
2 CONC fonso VI, whose imperial title he assumed. Though hisreign saw theapoge
2 CONC e of the imperial idea in medieval Spain and thoughhe won notablevictor
2 CONC ies against the Moors, he remains a somewhat hazyfigure. 'Hischildhood w
2 CONC as complicated by the struggle between his motherUrraca andher second h
2 CONC usband, Alfonso I of Aragon, for control ofCastile and Leon.Only on Urr
2 CONC aca's death (1126) did his stepfatherfinally relinquish hisclaims. Alfo
2 CONC nso was then formally accepted asemperor by the kings ofAragon and Pamp
2 CONC lona (Navarre), by the count ofBarcelona, and by variousHispano-Moorish r
2 CONC ulers. His capture ofAlmerâia (1147) from the Moors wonhim renown, as d
2 CONC id other victories,but in the end these led to littleexpansion of terri
2 CONC tory. Almerâia was lost again in 1157 andCâordobaremained in his hands f
2 CONC or only three years. In 1146 a newinvasion ofNorth African fanatics, th
2 CONC e Almohads, began. Alfonso nowalliedhimself with the Almoravids and dev
2 CONC oted the rest of his life toaseries of campaigns to check Almohad expan
2 CONC sion in southern Spain.'Despite the importance of the imperial idea at t
2 CONC his time,peninsularfractionalist tendencies were by no means dormant. A
2 CONC lfonso wasunableto prevent the establishment of Portugal as an independ
2 CONC entkingdom(1140) and, in his will, he himself divided his realm, as was
2 CONC theSpanish custom, between his two sons, Sancho III of CastileandFerdin
2 CONC and II of Leon. This act finally destroyed the concept ofempire in medi
2 CONC eval Spain.'
