2 CONC ngof Leon from 1157 to 1188, second son of Alfonso VII. 'Despite severa
2 CONC linternal revolts against his rule, Ferdinand's reignwas notable for th
2 CONC erepopulation of Leonese Extremadura and for thevictories he securedfar
2 CONC ther south against the Almohads in the last 20years of his reign.These i
2 CONC ncluded the capture of Alcâantara (1166)and Badajoz (1169). Healso gave i
2 CONC mportant support to the new militaryorder of Santiago,founded with his a
2 CONC pproval in 1170. Ferdinand, whocalled himself rexhispanorum ('king of t
2 CONC he Spaniards'), established atemporary tutelageover Castile during the m
2 CONC inority of his nephewAlfonso VIII and occupiedSegovia and Toledo (1162-
2 CONC 66), though Alfonsolater reacted violentlyagainst Ferdinand. Ferdinand w
2 CONC as alsofrequently engaged in hostilitieswith the nascent Portuguese kin
2 CONC gdombut came successfully to the rescueof the Portuguese when theAlmoha
2 CONC ds invested the key city of Santarâem(1184).'
