1 NOTE Nest ferch Rhys was a Welsh princess of Deheubarth who was renowned for
2 CONC her beauty. Nest was the daughter of Prince Rhys ap Tewdwr by his wife,
2 CONC Gwladys ferch Rhiwallon. After her father's death in 1093, Deheubarthwa
2 CONC s conquered by the Normans and King Henry I of England appointedhimself h
2 CONC er protector. Nest is thought to have borne him a son, HenryFitzRoy (11
2 CONC 03-1158).
2 CONT Around 1095 King Henry decided to marry Nest to one of his followers,Ge
2 CONC rald de Windsor, whom he appointed Constable of Pembroke.Consequently, N
2 CONC est is the maternal progenitor of the FitzGerald dynasty,one of the mos
2 CONC t celebrated families of Ireland and Great Britain.
2 CONT During Christmas 1109, Nest and her husband were visited by her cousin,
2 CONC Owain ap Cadwgan, son of Cadwgan ap Bleddyn, Prince of Powys. The story
2 CONC goes that Owain was so taken with Nest's beauty that he and fifteencomp
2 CONC anions attacked the castle of Cenarth Bychan (possibly CilgerranCastle o
2 CONC r Carew Castle, both in Pembrokeshire), seized Nest, and carriedher and h
2 CONC er children off.
2 CONT Tradition also states that Gerald escaped by jumping down the garderobe
2 CONC (i.e. the lavatory chute) to get away (one suspects that this is a Wels
2 CONC htradition). The children were later returned to Gerald. Nest is said t
2 CONC ohave borne Owain two sons, Llywelyn and Einion, before finally beingre
2 CONC turned to her husband.
2 CONT This abduction earned Nest the nickname "Helen of Wales" because it led
2 CONC to civil war on a small scale. Owain ap Cadwgan left the country toavoi
2 CONC d retribution, whilst Owain's father, Cadwgan ap Bleddyn, lost hisown l
2 CONC ands. Gerald waited for Owain to return to Wales, then ambushed andkill
2 CONC ed him in combat as retribution for kidnapping his wife andchildren. Af
2 CONC ter Gerald's death, Nest became the lover of Stephen,Constable of Cardi
2 CONC gan, by whom she had another son, Robert Fitz-Stephenwho died in 1182.
2 CONT Nest's daughter, Angharad, married William de Barry and had by him four
2 CONC sons: Robert; Philip, the founder of Ballybeg Abbey at Buttevant inIrel
2 CONC and; Walter; the historian Gerald of Wales. Her sons Philip andRobert c
2 CONC ampaigned in Ireland with Strongbow; Robert died there in 1182.Robert a
2 CONC nd Philip were the founders of the family Walsh/Welsh ofKilkenny where t
2 CONC hey built a Castle known as Castle hale of Kilkenny,Ireland
