1 NOTE From Encylcopedia Britannica Online article titled: 'DermotMacmurrough:
2 CONC ''Irish Diarmaid Macmurchada...Irish king of Leinster whose appeal toth
2 CONC eEnglish for help in settling an internal dispute led to theAnglo-Norma
2 CONC ninvasion and conquest of Ireland by England. 'After succeeding to thet
2 CONC hrone of his father, Enna, in 1126, Dermotfaced a number of rivals whod
2 CONC isputed his claim to the kingship. Heestablished his authority bykillin
2 CONC g or blinding 17 rebel chieftainsof northern Leinster in 1141. In1153 h
2 CONC e abducted the wife of TiernanO'Ruark, king of Breifne (moderncounties o
2 CONC f Leitrim and Cavan). 'A bitter feud ensued, and in 1166Dermot was driv
2 CONC en from Ireland.King Henry II of England then granted theexiled ruler p
2 CONC ermission toenlist the aid of several Anglo-Norman lordsof south Wales, n
2 CONC otablyRichard de Clare, 2nd earl of Pembroke. Returningto Leinster in 1
2 CONC 167with an advance party of Anglo-Normans, Dermotestablished a foothold
2 CONC there. Pembroke arrived in August 1170, and Dermotthen helped theinvade
2 CONC rs capture Dublin. Dermot married his daughter Evato Pembroke,and at De
2 CONC rmot's death Pembroke succeeded as ruler ofLeinster.'
