1 EVEN
2 TYPE Anecdote
2 NOTE The story of the Norman Conquest resembles a soap opera, pitting thewil
3 CONC y, deceitful villain (Dermot MacMurrough) against the well meaningbut h
3 CONC apless incompetent (Tiernan O'Rourke). MacMurrough and O'Rourkewere mor
3 CONC tal enemies. The antagonism between them dated to 1152, whenO'Rourke ha
3 CONC d been humiliated by MacMurrough's abduction of O'Rourke'swife, Dervorg
3 CONC illa. But MacMurrough may not have been as culpable as itseemed. Accord
3 CONC ing to Irish folklore, it was Dervorgilla herself, thenaged 44, who arr
3 CONC anged the abduction, with MacMurrough, then aged 42,simply going along. N
3 CONC evertheless, MacMurrough was hardly an innocentbystander, having eagerl
3 CONC y accepted the invitation, and having staged alifelike abduction, with h
3 CONC orsemen, screaming victim, and all thetrappings. O'Rourke recovered Der
3 CONC vorgilla the following year (1153), buthe never got the revenge he want
3 CONC ed.
3 CONT The subsequent hostilities between O'Rourke and MacMurrough were played
3 CONC out in the context of a larger battle between Rory O'Connor and Murtaug
3 CONC hMacLochlain for the high kingship of Ireland. O'Rourke was allied with
3 CONC O'Connor, the eventual winner, while MacMurrough supported, and moreimp
3 CONC ortantly was protected by, MacLochlain.
3 CONT In 1166, finally, after a 10 year war, O'Connor defeated MacLochlainonc
3 CONC e and for all. O'Connor was magnanimous in victory. He reducedMacLochla
3 CONC in's petit-kingdom to a small area, and took hostages, butotherwise per
3 CONC mitted him to live out his reign.
3 CONT O'Rourke had no intention of extending similar generosity toMacMurrough
3 CONC . He got his revenge later that same year when MacLochlain(MacMurrough'
3 CONC s long time protector) died, and O'Rourke, along withseveral cohorts, f
3 CONC orced MacMurrough to flee Ireland.
3 CONT But MacMurrough quickly regrouped. He sought help from Henry II, theafo
3 CONC rementioned Norman ruler of the Angevin empire. To Henry, MacMurroughre
3 CONC presented opportunity knocking. Henry had no enthusiasm for personallyl
3 CONC eading an expedition to Ireland -- after all, he had previouslydeclined t
3 CONC o do so, even after seeking and receiving the BullLaudabiliter – but he h
3 CONC ad nothing to lose by encouraging MacMurrough.Thus Henry issued an open l
3 CONC etter to his subjects, authorizing them torender military assistance to M
3 CONC acMurrough.
3 CONT MacMurrough then contacted one of the great Norman leaders in Wales, th
3 CONC elegendary "Strongbow" (a.k.a. Richard FitzGilbert de Clare, the earl o
3 CONC fStrigoil). Initially, Strongbow was reluctant, but then MacMurroughoff
3 CONC ered Strongbow his eldest daughter, Aoife (Eva), in marriage,together w
3 CONC ith the right to succeed MacMurrough as king of Leinster.Finally, Stron
3 CONC gbow agreed to lead an army into Ireland to restoreMacMurrough to power
3 CONC .
3 CONT With Strongbow on his side, MacMurrough then was also able to recruit a
3 CONC number of Norman and Flemish knights whose names now are common inIrela
3 CONC nd: FitzHenry, Carew, FitzGerald, Barry, Prendergast, Fleming,Roche, Ch
3 CONC eevers, Synott.
3 CONT The impatient MacMurrough returned to Ireland with a handful of Normans
3 CONC in 1167, but O'Connor and O'Rourke soon forced him to submit. Always th
3 CONC emaster of deceit, MacMurrough even paid O'Rourke one hundred ounces of
3 CONC gold as reparation for abducting Dervorgilla. But MacMurrough was notdi
3 CONC scouraged. He knew that help was on the way.
3 CONT The first Norman troop ships, about 600 in number, landed at Bannow Bay
3 CONC early in May 1169. MacMurrough and several hundred of his men promptlyj
3 CONC oined the Normans, and together they marched on Wexford. The Vikinginha
3 CONC bitants directly confronted the invaders, expecting to find arag-tag ou
3 CONC tfit of enthusiastic but poorly armed Irishmen. Instead theydiscovered a f
3 CONC ully armed and disciplined professional army, ready forthe kill. The Vi
3 CONC kings were driven back into Wexford, and next day thetown was forced to s
3 CONC urrender.
3 CONT Strongbow himself now set sail for Ireland. His advance guard, tenknigh
3 CONC ts and seventy archers, was led by a magnificent youngsoldier-warrior f
3 CONC rom the FitzGerald family, Raymond Carew, commonlycalled 'le Gros' ('th
3 CONC e Fat'). Le Gros landed north of Waterford andquickly built earthen ram
3 CONC parts which remain even today. Almostimmediately, an opposition army -- s
3 CONC everal thousand Vikings andGaelic-Irish from Waterford and the surround
3 CONC ing areas -- attacked leGros and his contingent of eighty Norman and Fl
3 CONC eming soldiers.
3 CONT Incredibly, le Gros and his vastly outnumbered troops prevailed. Behind
3 CONC the ramparts, le Gros had concealed a herd of cattle, which he suddenly
3 CONC stampeded into the oncoming troops, trampling the front rank of theatta
3 CONC ckers. In all the confusion, le Gros and his force routed theremaining n
3 CONC atives, seventy of whom were captured alive. As a message toWaterford, t
3 CONC he prisoners' limbs were broken, their heads severed, andtheir bodies t
3 CONC hrown over the cliffs.
3 CONT Now Strongbow and his army of about two hundred knights and a thousando
3 CONC ther troops joined le Gros, and two days later, they attackedWaterford. T
3 CONC wice the Normans were beaten off, but eventually le Grosbreached the wa
3 CONC lls at a weak point, and captured Waterford.
3 CONT Now MacMurrough's daughter, Aoife, came to Waterford, where she married
3 CONC Strongbow. A celebrated fresco in the British House of Commons depictst
3 CONC he wedding ceremony occurring at the close of the battle, against aback
3 CONC ground of burning buildings and dead Irishmen. The fresco is aclassic e
3 CONC xample of artistic license, since Aoife did not arrive untilseveral day
3 CONC s after the battle. But the artist captured brilliantly theessential el
3 CONC ements of the pact which MacMurrough had made with Strongbowtwo years e
3 CONC arlier in Wales.
3 CONT Strongbow and MacMurrough now set their sights on taking Dublin, whichw
3 CONC as a semi-independent Viking kingdom. With the wily MacMurrough leading
3 CONC the way, the Normans evaded an ambush laid by O'Connor and O'Rourke and
3 CONC arrived unscathed at the city walls. The Vikings were inclined tosurren
3 CONC der, but while negotiations were still ongoing, le Gros and Milode Coga
3 CONC n led their troops through a breach in the city walls and routedthe cit
3 CONC y's ineffectual defenders. Asgall, the Viking-Irish King ofDublin, mana
3 CONC ged to escape with some of his Viking followers. As theysailed away, As
3 CONC gall vowed to return and retake Dublin.
3 CONT MacMurrough became deathly ill in April 1171, and while Strongbow wasvi
3 CONC siting him, Asgall, who had been forced to flee only nine monthsearlier
3 CONC , made good on his vow to return. He brought with him a fleet ofships c
3 CONC arrying about a thousand Vikings, who mounted a fierce assault onStrong
3 CONC bow's Dublin. But even without Strongbow to lead them, the Normanspreva
3 CONC iled. Asgall, who was taken prisoner, was tried, convicted andbeheaded i
3 CONC n the hall of what formerly had been his own palace in Dublin.
