1 NOTE Reginald had seizen of his father's lands May 26, 1216, followingthedea
2 CONC th of his brother Giles. From DougThompson,http://freespace.virgin.net/
2 CONC doug.thompson/BraoseWeb/index1.htm:'Reginald supported Giles in his reb
2 CONC ellions against King John. Theywereboth active against the King in the b
2 CONC arons' war.  Neither waspresent atthe signing of Magna Carta because th
2 CONC ey were still rebelswho refused tocompromise. K. John aquiesced to Regi
2 CONC nald's claims tothe de Braoseestates in Wales in May 1216. He became Lo
2 CONC rd of Brecon,Abergavenny,Builth and other Marcher Lordships but was ver
2 CONC y much avassal of LlewelynFawr, Prince of Gwynedd and now his father-in
2 CONC -law.Henry III restoredReginald to favour and the Bramber estates(confi
2 CONC scated from William byK. John) in 1217. At thisseemingbetrayal, Rhys an
2 CONC d Owain, Reginald'snephews who were princesof Deheubarth, were incensed a
2 CONC nd they tookBuilth (except the castle). Llewelyn Fawr also becameangry a
2 CONC nd beseigedBrecon.    Reginald eventually surrendered toLlewelyn and ga
2 CONC ve upSeinenydd (Swansea). By 1221 they were at waragain with Llewelyn l
2 CONC ayingseige to Builth.  The seige was relieved byHenry III's forces. Fro
