1 NOTE LADY GODIVA (Godgifu, in the spelling of her time.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONC ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT An Anglo-Saxon gentlewoman, patron of the arts, equestrienne, and taxpr
2 CONC otester, etc. All the historians say that she "flourished, circa 1040- 1
2 CONC 080 A.D."
2 CONT
2 CONT Leofric, earl of Mercia, and husband of Lady Godiva, was a man of broad
2 CONC but obscure interests; a religious man thoroughly Christian, and anentr
2 CONC epreneur, raconteur, and general all around good sport. At the sametime
2 CONC , he did have an avaricious streak in him, and it is for this reasontha
2 CONC t his name has survived through the ages, but more prominently forhis i
2 CONC nteresting response to the nagging of his bleeding-heart wife, whopeste
2 CONC red him incessantly over details of the daily lives of the peasantsunde
2 CONC r his control, and similar fussy matters.
2 CONT
2 CONT It's not that she was always being petulant about everything he did, bu
2 CONC tcertain of his actions caused her to be irritable, but that part of th
2 CONC estory comes later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Actually both Leofric and Godiva were quite religious, and upon theirmo
2 CONC ve to Coventry, Warwickshire, from Shrewsbury, Shropshire (whereLeofric h
2 CONC ad earned his fortune and title from amazing successes in themutton tra
2 CONC de), they were immediately impressed by the lack of properfacilities fo
2 CONC r training and housing men of the cloth in or around theraucous little d
2 CONC istrict of Coventry (pop. 6215). How, they thought, werethe spiritual n
2 CONC eeds of these simple souls to be cared for? At the sametime, being some
2 CONC what "nouveau riche" and anxious to make their mark inwhat passed for g
2 CONC enteel society there (not having had the propercredentials to enter suc
2 CONC h circles back in Shropshire -- which may havebeen a big piece of the m
2 CONC otivation for the move), they decided to applysome of their ready cash t
2 CONC o a worthy public cause.
2 CONT
2 CONT Near the physical center of Coventry, where the bombed-out ruin ofmight
2 CONC y Coventry Cathedral stands today, Leofric and Godiva (in 1043)founded a
2 CONC nd funded an abbey (some accounts say monastery), named inhonor of Ste. E
2 CONC unice of Saxmundham (an early martyr, slain by flaying atthe hands of t
2 CONC he Romans). The abbey faced the rising sun, as was decreedit should by L
2 CONC eofric in a moment of romantic inspiration. A simpleaffair, made of wat
2 CONC tle and daub with a roof of thatched reeds in themanner of the day, it w
2 CONC as nevertheless imposing by standards in thevillage otherwise; it was t
2 CONC he biggest structure in the entire region.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before long this edifice was something of a social focus for the town,f
2 CONC unctioning not only as a center for the education and housing of thosew
2 CONC ho had received the calling, but also as a gathering point where morevu
2 CONC lgar popular events and festivities could be celebrated. Leofric andGod
2 CONC iva came to be awarded the attention and respect they longed for fromth
2 CONC e start, and they had served the purposes of the church as well. Theydi
2 CONC d not seem to mind that much of the activity circulating around theabbe
2 CONC y was decidedly Druid in origin; at least the peasants were occupiedand h
2 CONC appy. Coventry grew around the abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a gentleman, and now established philanthropist of some local repute
2 CONC ,Leofric assumed a growing role in the governance of public affairs, an
2 CONC dwas given responsibility for certain financial matters (the town hadgr
2 CONC own enough to actually have financial matters), which he quicklyunderst
2 CONC ood as presenting especially interesting opportunities. Onepublic work g
2 CONC enerated the idea of another, if only some money were athand to facilit
2 CONC ate such growth...
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Godiva's proficiency as a horsewoman had become polished to a
2 CONC considerable degree, as she had acquired a taste for the hunt and theso
2 CONC cial pleasantries which accrue to the activity "apres." Plus, thepeople s
2 CONC he met during these excursions which provided such complacency,were of a d
2 CONC isposition and delicacy of interest to which she had aspiredfor some ti
2 CONC me. She could do worse than to engage in certainpreoccupations of the i
2 CONC ntellect, and considerations of the aesthetic,and so she immersed herse
2 CONC lf in the arts and, therefore, society.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps, she earnestly thought, aid to those industrious in this fieldo
2 CONC f elevated concern (artists) would inspire the rude masses by means ofe
2 CONC xample. Commissioning a proper portrait of herself would be a goodplace t
2 CONC o start; it would be an inspiration for simpler souls, so thework was b
2 CONC egun.
2 CONT
2 CONT It only slowly entered Godiva's consciousness that the lack of successs
2 CONC he was having in interesting the base masses in artistic concerns,beaut
2 CONC iful pictures of herself spread around or not, was rooted in thefact th
2 CONC at nearly all of them spent 100% of their waking hours inpartially effe
2 CONC ctive efforts to feed and clothe themselves, and toprovide some form of s
2 CONC helter from the elements. Most were having a hardtime of it, in light o
2 CONC f the fact that Leofric, in his new-foundmegalomaniacal grand-public-wo
2 CONC rks mode, had been taxing everything hecould think of, even including a l
2 CONC evy on manure.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Godiva would not have such noble aspirations -- such as art foreve
2 CONC ryone -- placed on a back burner for the sake of boringconsiderations l
2 CONC ike a municipal water supply. Men had such ignoblevisions, always funct
2 CONC ional and mechanical, mostly never above waistlevel. This would not do, t
2 CONC he taxes must be reduced if this earlymedieval subsistence-agriculture v
2 CONC illage was going to pull itself upinto the 11th century and its more cu
2 CONC ltured concerns. She went to have apointed talk with Leofric.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beside himself with raucous laughter, Leofric injured his left wristsli
2 CONC ghtly as he fell off his stool in the hall of the village burghers,and t
2 CONC his sobered him up rather quickly. Reduce taxes in order to fosterthe p
2 CONC easants' appreciation of silly pictures? Was she mad? Nowaterworks? The
2 CONC re would be no tax reduction; as a matter of fact,Leofric added a new t
2 CONC ax on pictures, which only had to be paid by hiswife since she was the o
2 CONC nly person who had any, except for the churchwhich was exempt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their argument became a classic war of wills, taking the equally classi
2 CONC cform of nagging versus stone-walling. However, at very long last, sinc
2 CONC ehis wife would not give up and was driving him to distraction and wors
2 CONC e,Leofric capitulated, but, regarding it all as something of a sport,at
2 CONC tached an interesting condition to his offer to allow some reductionin t
2 CONC axation.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ancient Greeks, he pointed out, and those coarser Romans as well,vi
2 CONC ewed the nude human body as one of the highest expressions of theperfec
2 CONC tion of Nature. Nudity was not seen as erotic in any sense, but aspurit
2 CONC y, and a celebration of the wonderful form of a sensuous beingdisplayed i
2 CONC n all its marvelous glory for the betterment and appreciationof those e
2 CONC nlightened enough to consider this aesthetic. To present awell formed n
2 CONC ude body as an object of great beauty, even art, would beto offer a les
2 CONC son of inestimable value to the simple peasants ofCoventry, whose exper
2 CONC iences and perceptions had never been enlightenedto appreciate such per
2 CONC fection.
2 CONT
2 CONT If Lady Godiva truly believed in the crusade she was promoting, then sh
2 CONC eshould lead it herself, and offer to the citizens of Coventry an examp
2 CONC leof the glorious beauty to be understood by careful consideration of a
2 CONC perfect nude human body. There could be no shame in this, it would beth
2 CONC e most gross error to consider it as such. Was she ashamed of thewonder
2 CONC s of God's work? Besides, with all that horse-back riding, andsimilar, s
2 CONC he had lost some weight and looked pretty good.
2 CONT
2 CONT Therefore, Leofric proclaimed that if Lady Godiva would ride her horset
2 CONC hrough the crowded market-place of Coventry, in the full light ofmid-da
2 CONC y, clothed in only that which God had given her, as an example ofthe pe
2 CONC rfection of God's work and as an expression of the highestpossible aest
2 CONC hetic -- she had been spreading pictures of herself aroundanyway -- the
2 CONC n he would reduce taxes on the populace, lifting from themthe burden Go
2 CONC diva perceived, and erasing from himself any further doubthe might harb
2 CONC or of the sincerity of Godiva's convictions.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Leofric's absolute surprise, she agreed, once she had ensured thatsh
2 CONC e actually had his "permission" to do so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken aback by his wife's courage and certainty in her purposes Leofric
2 CONC ,somewhat overwhelmed, then stated that he fully accepted the truth ofL
2 CONC ady Godiva's belief in the merits of her cause, and so in response, onc
2 CONC ompletion of her ride he would not just reduce taxes, but would removea
2 CONC ll of them -- save those tolls on horses which were already in placebef
2 CONC ore he assumed his office, and which were necessary for basic needsof t
2 CONC he city.
2 CONT
2 CONT A day was chosen for the event, and while no particular effort had been
2 CONC expended to publicize the ride, talk of it had spread in whispersthroug
2 CONC hout the whole of Coventry. Not wishing to reveal that thisconcealed di
2 CONC scussion had taken place, and since people were curiousabout all aspect
2 CONC s of the affair and did not want to interrupt it, themarketplace's busi
2 CONC ness proceeded as it might have done on any other lessinteresting Thurs
2 CONC day in late August.
2 CONT
2 CONT As noon approached, so did Lady Godiva. She was not alone, but wasaccom
2 CONC panied by two female aides also on horseback, but normally clothed;one r
2 CONC ode on each side and slightly to the rear. Three horses walking onthe c
2 CONC obbles in formation at a measured gait did not have the sound ofthe usu
2 CONC al traffic and bustle, and so -- since all were secretly andeagerly ant
2 CONC icipating the event -- her appearance was announced clearlyto everyone.
2 CONT
2 CONT She sat straight and properly in the saddle with a look of composure on
2 CONC her face; relaxed, confident, unashamed. Her hair was done in two large
2 CONC braids which were curled snugly at the back of her head, one on eachsid
2 CONC e; she wore no jewelry or other adornment. People looked at her andsaw t
2 CONC hat she was not merely naked, or nude; rather she was in a higherstate o
2 CONC f presentation -- being a correct and elevated quality of hercomposure, a
2 CONC nd resulting also from the people's appraisal, appreciation,and conside
2 CONC ration beyond simple voyeurism.
2 CONT
2 CONT To all present this was an experience like no other in their lives. The
2 CONC only images of people unclothed they had ever seen were in the church:A
2 CONC dam and Eve, and the crucified Christ. This was a lady, simple andnorma
2 CONC l with a body like that of every other woman present, a human, acreatur
2 CONC e of God's earth. Though he half meant it as a joke, Leofric'swords ran
2 CONC g true: here was a celebration of being in its perfection.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps, as well, some believed with Zoroaster that sex is the bounty o
2 CONC fGod.
2 CONT
2 CONT So, all survived the event with peacefulness and dignity, and the taxes
2 CONC were removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the CHRONICA, written by Roger of Wendover (who died in 1236), theac
2 CONC count of the year 1057 tells the story of Godiva's ride in fulldetail, a
2 CONC nd is the earliest surviving written description.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even more complete versions are provided by the famous historian Ranulf
2 CONC Higden (died in 1364) in his POLYCHRONICON, and by Henry Knighton (died
2 CONC c. 1396) who followed him, which explain not only the details of therid
2 CONC e and its reasons, but also the specifics of the removed taxes, inparti
2 CONC cular that all save those on horses were eliminated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much later, King Edward I, being an inquisitive man (he devised anearne
2 CONC st, but rather awkward system for the classification of songbirdsin Wal
2 CONC es), wished to discover the truth of the Godiva story and,therefore, co
2 CONC mmissioned an inquiry of ancient records which showed thatin 1057 and t
2 CONC hereabouts, there were indeed no taxes levied in Coventryexcept those o
2 CONC n horses, which was a rather anomalous situation not seenelsewhere at t
2 CONC he time, thereby establishing the merit and probableaccuracy of the leg
2 CONC end.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tale of "Peeping Tom", who was struck blind (or dead) when he alone
2 CONC gazed upon Lady Godiva was not added until the 17th century. This isals
2 CONC o true of the detail of the story, often added, that Godiva wascovered t
2 CONC otally, except for her legs, by an enormous and improbablequantity of h
2 CONC air.
2 CONT
2 CONT Doubtless both of these embellishments were supplied later by prudishCh
2 CONC ristian churchmen who entirely missed the point and considered thatview
2 CONC ing the unclothed human body under any circumstance was a heinousact wh
2 CONC ich would damn one to eternal hell fire; they certainly thoughtthe fema
2 CONC le body to be dirty and inferior to appreciation, and onlyworthy of bei
2 CONC ng hidden from view. On the face of it such a view wouldseem to be a pe
2 CONC rversion, and affront to the beauty of God's work. TheGreeks felt that t
2 CONC he idealized human form was the only one worthy torepresent the gods on e
2 CONC arth. On the other hand, the Christian faith isunique in that it alone h
2 CONC as, throughout its history, suppressed anycelebration of the beauty of t
2 CONC he human body.
2 CONT
2 CONT So, what was Lady Godiva? A visionary; a social climber; a patron of th
2 CONC earts; a dilettante of the worst order?
2 CONT
2 CONT In any estimation, she had the guts to follow her convictions, and mayh
2 CONC ave brought a degree of enlightenment to a small corner of 11th century
2 CONC England. And, probably, no one went to Hell because of it.
2 CONT
2 CONT © Jerome C. Krause
2 CONT  A Festival in her honor was instituted as part of Coventry Fair in1678
2 CONC .
