1 NOTE Maud (Matilda) de Braose was also known as the Lady of la Haie and to t
2 CONC heWelsh as Moll Walbee. Married to William de Braose, the "Ogre ofAberg
2 CONC avenny", she was a significant warrior in her own right. Her longdefenc
2 CONC e of Pain's Castle when it was besieged by the Welsh earned it thename "
2 CONC Matilda's Castle". The local people saw her as a supernaturalcharacter. S
2 CONC he was said to have built Hay Castle single handed in onenight, carryin
2 CONC g the stones in her apron.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud's stoneWhen one fell out and lodged in her slipper she picked itou
2 CONC t and flung it to land in St Meilig's churchyard, three miles awayacros
2 CONC s the River Wye at Llowes. The nine foot high standing stone canstill b
2 CONC e seen inside the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT The final fall of her husband may owe a lot to her hasty reply to KingJ
2 CONC ohn when he requested her son William as a hostage in 1208. She refused
2 CONC on the grounds that John had murdered his nephew Arthur whom he shouldh
2 CONC ave protected. The dispute between John and the de Braose family led to
2 CONC Maud dying of starvation in Windsor Castle along with her son, while he
2 CONC rhusband, stripped of all his lands, died the following year in exile i
2 CONC nFrance.
