2 DATE 2 JAN 2010
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Thomas Hoo was Esquire of the Chamber to Thomas Beaufort, Duke ofExeter
2 CONC , whose will he witnessed in 1426. On 10 February 1430 he wasappointed S
2 CONC heriff of Bedfordshire and Buckinghamshire and on 18 June1434 he is rep
2 CONC orted to be in France with a retinue of 20 men-at-arms and60 archers. I
2 CONC n 1436 he took part in the operations in the Pays de Caux.
2 CONT
2 CONT 'And the iiii (fourth) day of Janyuar, 1436, the Lorde Talbot, and theL
2 CONC orde Scalys and Sir Thomas Kerryell and Sir Thomas Hoo went to Cauxwith t
2 CONC heir men, and roode in to the contre (centre) and brent (burnt)and slew a
2 CONC ll that myght be taken of the contre....And the x (tenth) daythey made a
2 CONC nother journey to Caux another way. And all the bestis(animals) they br
2 CONC ought before them un (in) to Caux, and ther (there)thei (they) solde a s
2 CONC chepe (sheep) for the valew (value) of a peny and akow (cow) for xiid (
2 CONC twelve pence). And thus all the contre of Caux wasdestroyed both of men a
2 CONC nd of bestis, and of all her goodis (goods)'.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1437 he was Bailiff of Mantes and on 29 June 1442, for his servicesi
2 CONC n the wars in France, he was granted £40 a year out of the issues ofNor
2 CONC folk. On 9 September 1442 he was nominated one of the commissionersto t
2 CONC reat of peace with France and again on 27 June 1444.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 August 1444 he was sent with Sir Robert de Roos and the Order ofT
2 CONC he Garter, by the King to Margaret of Anjou, the king's betrothed. On16 A
2 CONC ugust 1445 Thomas Hoo was installed Knight of The Garter. On 19 July144
2 CONC 5 was granted to him, his heirs and assigns of the Castle, Lordship,Bar
2 CONC ony and Honour of Hastings. This was with rents, services and profitswh
2 CONC atsoever and of the returns of writs, issued and of the castle, theCrow
2 CONC n having received nothing since the death of Ralph, Earl ofWestmorland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He appears as Chancellor of Normandy in 1446 and as Chancellor of Franc
2 CONC eand Captain of Mantes in 1447. On 2 June 1448, for his good services i
2 CONC nEngland, France and Normandy, he was created, the grant being to him a
2 CONC ndhis heirs male, Lord Hoo of Hoo in the county of Bedford and of Hasti
2 CONC ngsin the county of Sussex.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1449, as Chancellor of France, he was sent with the Abbot ofGloucest
2 CONC er by the Duke of Somerset to inform the King and Parliament ofthe stat
2 CONC e of affairs in France. In 1450 he appears to have returned toEngland, s
2 CONC haring the unpopularity of the Duke of Somerset. However, in1453 he was o
2 CONC n a commission to treat in Surrey and Sussex for a loan torelieve the E
2 CONC arl of Shrewsbury's army in the Duchy of Aquitaine and thecity of Borde
2 CONC aux.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his first wife he had one daughter, and by his second wife threedaug
2 CONC hters. When he died, 13 February 1455, his title became extinct.
