1 NAME Joan /of England/
2 GIVN Joan
2 SURN of England
1 SEX F
1 _UID D909AC26621A464197D86D1792FCC7BDBC50
1 _AMTID 330126065883:1030:114017017
1 CHAN
2 DATE 1 JAN 2010
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Joan was the natural daughter of John, king of England. Little is known
2 CONC about her early life; she was probably born before her father marriedIs
2 CONC abella, countess of Gloucester. Her mother's name is known from Joan'so
2 CONC bituary in the Tewkesbury Annals, where she is mysteriously called'Regi
2 CONC na Clementina' (Queen Clementia or Clemence). Joan seems to havespent h
2 CONC er childhood in France, as King John had her brought to Englandfrom Nor
2 CONC mandy in preparation for her wedding.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1226 Joan obtained a papal decree from Pope Honorius III,decla
2 CONC ring her legitimate on the basis that her parents had not beenmarried t
2 CONC o others at the time of her birth, but without giving her aclaim to the E
2 CONC nglish throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1205 Joan's father married her to Llywelyn Fawr, prince of Wales.The
2 CONC y had a son Dafydd and daughter Elen who would have progeny.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Easter 1230, William de Braose, who was Llywelyn's nominal prisonera
2 CONC t the time, was discovered together with Joan in Llywelyn's bedchamber.
2 CONC William de Braose was hanged in the marshland at the foot of GarthCelyn
2 CONC , the place known since as Gwern y Grog. Joan herself was placedout of p
2 CONC ublic view, under virtual house arrest, at Garth Celyn, fortwelve month
2 CONC s after the incident. She was then (apparently) forgiven byLlywelyn, an
2 CONC d restored as wife and princess.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was never called Princess of Wales, but in Welsh, 'Lady of Wales'.
2 CONC She died at the royal home, Garth Celyn, Aberr Garth Celyn, on the nort
2 CONC hcoast of Gwynedd in 1237. Llywelyn's great grief at her death isrecord
2 CONC ed; he founded a Franciscan friary on the seashore at Llanfaes,opposite t
2 CONC he royal home, in her honour. The friary was consecrated in1240, shortl
2 CONC y before Llywelyn's death. It was closed down in 1537 byHenry VIII of E
2 CONC ngland during the Dissolution of the Monasteries.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 19th century it was discovered that Joan's coffin was being used
2 CONC as a horse trough. Rescued from this use, it was placed in Baron HillPa
2 CONC rk, near Baumaris in Anglesey. Its most recent location is given asthe C
2 CONC hurch of St. Mary and St. Nicholas in Beaumaris.
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 MAR 1236
2 PLAC Arllechwedd Isaf, Caernarvonshire
1 DEAT
