1 _AMTID 330126066303:1030:114017017
1 CHAN
2 DATE 27 JUN 2010
1 NOTE * BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT       Henry was born in 1068 in Selby, Yorkshire, the only child ofWill
2 CONC iam the Conqueror to be born in England. He was also the only son tobe b
2 CONC orn in 'purple' as only two years previously William had become kingof E
2 CONC ngland. As the youngest child he was his mother's favourite and whenshe d
2 CONC ied she left him her English estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT       He had a good education, learning to read and write Latin as well
2 CONC as English and Law. In 1086 he was knighted by his father. When hisfath
2 CONC er died in 1087 his brother Robert received the duchy of Normandywhile W
2 CONC illiam II Rufus became king of England. Henry, having estates inboth te
2 CONC rritories like so many other Norman barons, had to maintain hisrelation
2 CONC s with two overlords.
2 CONT
2 CONT       When in 1100 William Rufus mysteriously died, Henry was elected t
2 CONC osucceed as king of England and on 6 August he was crowned in Westminst
2 CONC erAbbey. His first act as king was to restore Anselm as archbishop ofCa
2 CONC nterbury, then he started to look for a bride. For his queen heselected E
2 CONC dith of Scotland, daughter of King Malcolm Canmore and, moreimportantly
2 CONC , of St. Margaret of Wessex who was a descendant of the kingsof England p
2 CONC rior to the Conquest. In honour of the king's mother,Matilda of Flander
2 CONC s, Edith changed her name to Matilda. Restoring Anselmdid not ensure pe
2 CONC ace in the kingdom as he refused to do homage to theking, claiming to h
2 CONC old the church estates in the name of the pope.Anselm was then forced i
2 CONC nto exile, and peace was restored only in 1107when the king's sister Ad
2 CONC ela, countess of Blois, found a solutionacceptable to both: bishops wou
2 CONC ld pay homage to the king and the kingwould allow clerical investiture.
2 CONT
2 CONT       When Henry's brother Robert Curthose, duke of Normandy returnedfr
2 CONC om Crusade he proved such a bad ruler that the barons in Normandyrevolt
2 CONC ed and asked for Henry's support. Henry was also motivated by awish to e
2 CONC liminate the continuing threat from Robert and the drain on hisfiscal r
2 CONC esources from the annual payment to him, so in 1105 he led anexpedition
2 CONC ary force across the English Channel. On the morning of 28September 110
2 CONC 6, exactly 40 years after their father had landed inEngland, the decisi
2 CONC ve battle between his two sons took place in thesmall village of Tinche
2 CONC bray. Robert's army was defeated and he wascaptured. Robert was impriso
2 CONC ned and Henry became duke of Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Henry was a good diplomat; even though troubles within Normandyan
2 CONC d with France continued, he made a successful alliance when his onlydau
2 CONC ghter Matilda married the Emperor Heinrich V in 1114. In 1119 hisonly s
2 CONC on William went to the continent and married a daughter of thecount of A
2 CONC njou. On the journey home the 'White Ship' was wrecked andWilliam with h
2 CONC is entourage drowned. Henry's wife had died in 1118, buthe waited until 1
2 CONC 122 before taking a second wife, Adeliza of Louvain. Hehad fathered two l
2 CONC egitimate and probably nineteen illegitimate children,but his second ma
2 CONC rriage remained childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT       In 1126 Henry designated his daughter, the widowed EmpressMatilda
2 CONC , as his successor; and in 1127 he selected Geoffrey, count ofAnjou, as h
2 CONC er second husband even though he was ten years younger thanMatilda. Hen
2 CONC ry travelled a great deal between England and Normandy, andon 1 August 1
2 CONC 135 he left England for the last time. He died 1 December1135 at St. De
2 CONC nis-le-Fermont near Gisors. His body was taken back toEngland and burie
2 CONC d at Reading Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Encylcopedia Britannica Onlines, article entitled Henry I 'Also,HE
2 CONC NRY BEAUCLERC (GOOD SCHOLAR), French HENRI BEAUCLERC youngestandablest o
2 CONC f William I the Conqueror's sons, who as king ofEngland(1100-35) streng
2 CONC thened the crown's executive powers and, likehisfather, also ruled Norm
2 CONC andy (from1106).' 'Henry I was a skillful,intelligent monarch who achie
2 CONC ved peace inEngland, relative stability inNormandy, and notable adminis
2 CONC trativeadvances on both sides of theChannel. Under Henry, the Anglo-Nor
2 CONC manstate his father had created wasreunited. Royal justices began makin
2 CONC gsystematic tours of the Englishshires, but, although hisadministrative p
2 CONC olicies were highly efficient,they were notinfrequently regarded as opp
2 CONC ressive. His reign marked asignificantadvance from the informal, person
2 CONC al monarchy of former timestowardthe bureaucratized state that lay in t
2 CONC he future. It also markedashift from the wide-ranging imperialism of ea
2 CONC rlier Norman leaderstoconsolidation and internal development. In the ge
2 CONC nerationsbeforeHenry's accession, Norman dukes, magnates, and adventure
2 CONC rshadconquered southern Italy, Sicily, Antioch, and England. Henry wonh
2 CONC ismajor battles but preferred diplomacy or bribery to the risks oftheba
2 CONC ttlefield. Subduing Normandy in 1106, he contented himselfwithkeeping d
2 CONC omestic peace, defending his Anglo-Norman stateagainstrebellion and inv
2 CONC asion, and making alliances withneighbouringprinces.'
