1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Robert was born in Caen about 1090, an illegitimate son of King Henry I
2 CONC of England. He was known as Robert de Caen after his birthplace. Hewitn
2 CONC essed charters of the king from about April 1113 onwards and foughtat t
2 CONC he Battle of Brémulé on 20 August 1119, where his father defeatedLouis V
2 CONC I, king of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between June and September 1122 Robert was created Earl of Gloucester.I
2 CONC n 1123 he brought a force to help in capturing the castle of Brionnehel
2 CONC d by the rebellious Norman barons. In 1126 he had custody of hisuncle, D
2 CONC uke Robert of Normandy, as a prisoner at Bristol and later atCardiff. I
2 CONC n 1127 he did homage to the Empress Matilda, recognising heras his fath
2 CONC er's successor in the kingdom. On this occasion he had adispute about p
2 CONC recedence with his cousin, Stephen de Blois, which wasdecided against h
2 CONC im.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1130 he sanctioned the foundation of the abbey of Neath. In 1131, at
2 CONC the Council of Northampton, he took precedence among the five earls who
2 CONC attended. In 1133 he was sent by the king to Bayeux, after the death of
2 CONC Bishop Richard, to enquire about the fees and services due to the see b
2 CONC yits barons, knights and vavasours. He was present at the king's death i
2 CONC nDecember 1135 and received 60,000 livres from him, apparently asexecut
2 CONC or.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Stephen's accession to the English Crown and his recognition as duke
2 CONC by the Normans, Robert gave up Falaise to the king's agents, but remove
2 CONC dhis father's treasure. In March 1136 he returned to England and, after
2 CONC Easter, did homage for his English possessions. When Stephen granted hi
2 CONC sCharter of Liberties, Robert was a witness. At about this time or in t
2 CONC hefollowing year he founded the priory of St.James at Bristol as a cell t
2 CONC oTewkesbury, giving to the Lady Chapel of the priory every tenth stonew
2 CONC hile building the keep of Bristol Castle at the same time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1137 he accompanied Stephen to Normandy. However, they quarrelled an
2 CONC dthe following year his English and Welsh estates were forfeited.Thereu
2 CONC pon he prepared for war against Stephen and took up the cause ofhis hal
2 CONC f-sister Matilda in Normandy, obtaining the surrender of Caen andBayeux t
2 CONC o her husband, Geoffrey of Anjou. In September 1139 he landed inEngland w
2 CONC ith Matilda and, taking her to Arundel Castle, became hercommander in t
2 CONC he civil war that ensued.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1140 he burnt Nottingham, and in February 1141 he and his son-in-law
2 CONC Ranulph, earl of Chester, relieved Lincoln and took Stephen prisoner,se
2 CONC nding him to Bristol. He accompanied Matilda in her progress toWinchest
2 CONC er and London, and when the citizens drove her out he fled withher to O
2 CONC xford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taking part in the fighting at Winchester, he helped Matilda to escapef
2 CONC rom the city, but on 14 September 1141 he was captured at Stockbridgean
2 CONC d taken prisoner to Rochester. Shortly afterwards he was exchanged,with
2 CONC out concessions on either side, for King Stephen. Robert then joinedMat
2 CONC ilda at Bristol and proceeded with her to Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1142 Matilda sent Robert over to her husband to urge him toinva
2 CONC de England. Geoffrey declined to help until he had conqueredNormandy, s
2 CONC o Robert joined him in his campaign. When they received thenews that Ma
2 CONC tilda was besieged in Oxford, Robert hurried back to helpher, taking wi
2 CONC th him her son, the future King Henry II. He capturedWareham and other p
2 CONC laces and, on Matilda's escape from Oxford, he andHenry met her at Wall
2 CONC ingford. In 1143 Robert defeated Stephen at Wilton,and in 1144 he block
2 CONC aded Malmesbury, Stephen refusing battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda's party was now so much reduced that Stephen was able to takeFa
2 CONC ringdon, which had been fortified by Robert. In the spring of 1147Rober
2 CONC t took Henry back to Wareham and sent him over to Anjou; in thesame yea
2 CONC r he founded Margam Abbey. On 31 October 1147 Robert died of afever at B
2 CONC ristol and was buried there in the priory of St.James.
